ing Processes', 'Technical Concept', 'A non-contact machining process that removes electrically conductive workpiece material through controlled electrical discharges in a dielectric medium.', 'Electrode/workpiece gap, spark energy, dielectric, material removal rate, surface finish and tool wear.', 'Localized energy transfer; material removal; process control.', 'EDM removes conductive material through repeated electrical discharges. It is useful for hard or complex conductive materials but involves electrode wear and energy consumption.', 'Non-traditional machining; electrical discharge; Localized energy transfer; material removal; process control; Manufacturing Processes', 'Active'), ('KCR_000115', 'Von Mises', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Design of Machine Elements', 'Technical Concept', 'The von Mises yield criterion predicts yielding of ductile isotropic materials from the distortion-energy component of the stress state.', 'Equivalent von Mises stress, multiaxial stress states, yield strength comparison and ductile-metal applicability.', 'Distortion energy; yielding; multiaxial stress.', 'The von Mises criterion compares an equivalent scalar stress with the material''s yield strength and is widely used for ductile isotropic metals under multiaxial loading.', 'Yield criteria; stress invariants; Distortion energy; yielding; multiaxial stress; Design of Machine Elements', 'Active'), ('KCR_000116', 'Goodman diagram', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Design of Machine Elements', 'Principle / Criterion', 'A fatigue-design diagram relating alternating stress and mean stress to an endurance-strength criterion, commonly using a linear Goodman relation.', 'Mean stress, alternating stress, endurance strength, ultimate strength and safety factors.', 'Fatigue; mean-stress effect; safety factor.', 'The Goodman relation provides a conservative linear approximation for the effect of mean stress on fatigue strength and is used in high-cycle fatigue design.', 'Fatigue failure; S-N behaviour; Fatigue; mean-stress effect; safety factor; Design of Machine Elements', 'Active'), ('KCR_000117', 'Shaft and key design', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Design of Machine Elements', 'Method / Process', 'Design of rotating shafts and keys to transmit torque while satisfying strength, stiffness, fatigue and connection requirements.', 'Torsional/bending stresses, diameter, key dimensions, stress concentrations, deflection and torque transmission.', 'Strength; stiffness; fatigue; torque transmission.', 'Shaft design considers combined loading, torsional strength, bending, fatigue and deformation. Keys transmit torque between a shaft and hub while requiring checks against shear and bearing failure.', 'Torsion; fatigue; machine elements', 'Active'), ('KCR_000118', 'Journal bearings', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Design of Machine Elements', 'Technical Concept', 'Bearings that support a rotating shaft through sliding contact, commonly separated by a lubricating film under hydrodynamic or related regimes.', 'Bearing pressure, viscosity, clearance, Sommerfeld-type parameters, lubrication regime, friction and heat generation.', 'Lubrication; friction reduction; load capacity; thermal management.', 'Journal-bearing performance depends on shaft speed, load, clearance, lubricant properties and geometry. Hydrodynamic lubrication can sustain load through pressure generated in the lubricant film.', 'Tribology; lubrication; friction reduction; load capacity; thermal management; Design of Machine Elements', 'Active'), ('KCR_000119', 'Gear design fundamentals', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Design of Machine Elements', 'Method / Process', 'Principles used to select and proportion gears for transmitting motion and torque while satisfying strength, wear, accuracy and service-life requirements.', 'Gear geometry, module/diametral pitch, tooth forces, bending/contact stress, ratios and failure modes.', 'Power transmission; strength; wear; reliability; geometry.', 'Gear design balances kinematic requirements with tooth bending and contact strength, wear, manufacturability and service-life considerations.', 'Gear trains; fatigue; contact stress', 'Active'), ('KCR_000120', 'Laplace transforms', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Method / Process', 'An integral transform that maps suitable time-domain functions to an algebraic function of a complex variable and is widely used to analyze linear dynamic systems.', 'Transform definition, properties, inverse transform, poles/zeros and differential-equation solution.', 'Linear systems; superposition; transform-domain analysis.', 'Laplace transforms convert differential equations and convolution relationships into algebraic forms under stated existence and initial-condition assumptions.', 'Differential equations; transfer functions; Linear systems; superposition; transform-domain analysis; Control Systems Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_000121', 'Time-domain response', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'The temporal output behaviour of a dynamic system following an input, disturbance or initial condition.', 'Transient and steady-state response, rise time, settling time, overshoot, damping and natural frequency.', 'Stability; transient performance; steady-state accuracy.', 'Time-domain response metrics quantify how a dynamic system approaches its final or steady behaviour and reveal the effects of poles, damping and system parameters.', 'Dynamic systems; transfer functions; Stability; transient performance; steady-state accuracy; Control Systems Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_000122', 'Routh-Hurwitz and Nyquist criteria', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Frequency- or polynomial-domain stability-analysis methods used to determine whether a feedback system has unstable poles or encirclements associated with closed-loop instability.', 'Routh array, characteristic polynomial, Nyquist plot, encirclement criterion and open-loop/closed-loop relationships.', 'Stability; pole location; feedback; robustness.', 'Routh-Hurwitz assesses polynomial stability without explicitly solving for roots. Nyquist uses the frequency response and encirclement criterion to infer closed-loop stability under its assumptions.', 'Characteristic equation; frequency response; Stability; pole location; feedback; robustness', 'Active'), ('KCR_000123', 'Bode plot', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'A logarithmic magnitude-and-phase representation of a system''s frequency response as a function of frequency.', 'Magnitude in decibels, phase, corner frequencies, asymptotic construction and gain/phase margins.', 'Frequency response; stability margins; bandwidth.', 'Bode plots expose frequency-dependent gain and phase and support assessment of bandwidth, stability margins and compensator effects.', 'Frequency response; transfer functions; stability margins; bandwidth; Control Systems Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_000124', 'PID control', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering; Mechatronics', 'Method / Process', 'Feedback control using proportional, integral, and derivative actions to regulate a process variable based on the control error.', 'Concept-level scope: the defining mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria; curricular context is recorded in Concept_Category and Typical_Modules.', 'Defining mechanism/properties; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria for PID control.', 'PID control combines proportional response, integral accumulation and derivative prediction to shape closed-loop performance. Practical implementations require attention to saturation, noise and tuning.', 'Feedback control; controller tuning; Defining mechanism/properties; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria for PID control', 'Active'), ('