he concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Measurement isolation; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Measurement isolation; primary curricular occurrence: Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation.', 'Measurement isolation; Key elements: Measurement isolation; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Active'), ('KCR_001255', 'Phase segregation in reactors', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Separation or non-uniform distribution of phases or species in a reactor, including its effects on transport, reaction rates, residence-time behaviour, and reactor performance.', 'Curricular scope: Chemical Reaction Engineering. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Phase segregation in reactors; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Phase segregation in reactors; primary curricular occurrence: Chemical Reaction Engineering.', 'Phase segregation in reactors; Key elements: Phase segregation in reactors; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_001256', 'Soil permeability', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Foundation Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Property describing the ability of soil to transmit fluid through its interconnected pore space under a hydraulic gradient; commonly represented by hydraulic conductivity in saturated flow analysis.', 'Curricular scope: Foundation Engineering. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Soil permeability; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Soil permeability; primary curricular occurrence: Foundation Engineering.', 'Soil permeability; Key elements: Soil permeability; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Foundation Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_001257', 'Membrane permeability', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mass Transfer Operations', 'Technical Concept', 'Permeability of a membrane to a specified component or species, governing the rate at which that component passes through the membrane under a specified driving force.', 'Curricular scope: Mass Transfer Operations. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Membrane permeability; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Membrane permeability; primary curricular occurrence: Mass Transfer Operations.', 'Membrane permeability; Key elements: Membrane permeability; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Mass Transfer Operations', 'Active'), ('KCR_001258', 'Reactor mixing', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Mixing of reactants and fluid phases within a chemical reactor to reduce concentration and temperature gradients and influence transport, reaction rate, selectivity, and residence-time behaviour.', 'Curricular scope: Chemical Reaction Engineering. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Reactor mixing; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Reactor mixing; primary curricular occurrence: Chemical Reaction Engineering.', 'Reactor mixing; Key elements: Reactor mixing; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_001259', 'Geotechnical sampling', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Foundation Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Collection of representative soil, rock, groundwater, or other subsurface samples for laboratory characterization and design decisions, with attention to sample disturbance and representativeness.', 'Curricular scope: Foundation Engineering. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Geotechnical sampling; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Geotechnical sampling; primary curricular occurrence: Foundation Engineering.', 'Geotechnical sampling; Key elements: Geotechnical sampling; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Foundation Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_001260', 'Signal sampling', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Technical Concept', 'Conversion of a continuous-time signal into discrete samples at specified sampling instants; sampling rate and reconstruction requirements determine aliasing and information loss.', 'Curricular scope: Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Signal sampling; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Signal sampling; primary curricular occurrence: Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation.', 'Signal sampling; Key elements: Signal sampling; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Mechatronics; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Active'), ('KCR_001261', 'Air infiltration', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Refrigeration and Air Conditioning', 'Technical Concept', 'Uncontrolled or unintended entry of outdoor air into a conditioned space through leakage paths driven by pressure differences, wind, stack effect, or mechanical-system operation.', 'Curricular scope: Refrigeration and Air Conditioning. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Air infiltration; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Air infiltration; primary curricular occurrence: Refrigeration and Air Conditioning.', 'Air infiltration; Key elements: Air infiltration; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Refrigeration and Air Conditioning', 'Active'), ('KCR_001262', 'Reactor ignition', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Technical Concept', 'Onset of self-sustained combustion or rapid exothermic reaction in a reactive mixture when thermal and kinetic conditions permit ignition or autoignition.', 'Curricular scope: Chemical Reaction Engineering. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Reactor ignition; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Reactor ignition; primary curricular occurrence: Chemical Reaction Engineering.', 'Reactor ignition; Key elements: Reactor ignition; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Active'), ('KCR_001263', 'Membrane selectivity', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mass Transfer Operations', 'Technical Concept', 'Degree to which a membrane preferentially permeates one component relative to another, determined by differences in sorption and transport properties and expressed through appropriate selectivity metrics.', 'Curricular scope: Mass Transfer Operations. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Membrane selectivity; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Membrane selectivity; primary curricular occurrence: Mass Transfer Operations.', 'Membrane selectivity; Key elements: Membrane selectivity; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Mass Transfer Operations', 'Active'), ('KCR_001264', 'Measurement sensitivity', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Technical Concept', 'Change in measurement-system output per unit change in the measurand or input quantity, considered together with resolution, noise, range, and uncertainty when evaluating an instrument.', 'Curricular scope: Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Measurement sensitivity; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Measurement sensitivity; primary curricular occurrence: Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation.', 'Measurement sensitivity; Key elements: Measurement sensitivity; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Active'), ('KCR_001265', 'Policy coordination', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Technical Concept', 'Alignment of actions, responsibilities, instruments, and decisions among public institutions or policy actors to reduce conflict, duplication, and implementation gaps.', 'Curricular scope: Public Policy and Governance. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Policy coordination; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Policy coordination; primary curricular occurrence: Public Policy and Governance.', 'Policy coordination; Key elements: Policy coordination; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Public Policy and Governance', 'Active'), ('KCR_001266', 'Research confidentiality', 'Law', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Legal Concept', 'Protection of identifiable or sensitive research information from unauthorized access or disclosure, consistent with informed consent, ethics requirements, data-management plans, and applicable law.', 'Curricular scope: Research Methods in Social Sciences. The concept is represented at the level of the stated mechanism, properties, constraints, and application criteria.', 'Key elements: Research confidentiality; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria.', 'Canonical knowledge concept for Research confidentiality; primary curricular occurrence: Research Methods in Social Sciences.', 'Research confidentiality; Key elements: Research confidentiality; governing conditions; assumptions and limitations; application/performance criteria; Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Active'), ('KCR_001267', 'Engine calibration', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Internal Combustion Engines', 'Technical Concept', 'Adjustment and validation of engine control parameters and maps so that the engine meets specified performance, emissions, drivability, and operating constraints across its intended operating range.', 'Internal Combustion Engines → Engine Control and Diagnostics: calibration of control parameters and maps, validation against performance and emissions constraints, and operating-range considerations.', 'Calibration targets; parameter/map adjustment; operating-point coverage; performance and emissions constraints; validation and trade-offs.', 'Canonical engine-control calibration concept; distinct from metrological calibration of measurement instruments.', 'engine calibration; engine control calibration; Calibration targets; parameter/map adjustment; operating-point coverage; performance and emissions constraints', 'Active') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO repo_kprs (kpr_id, name, domain, discipline, principle_type, principle_granularity, definition, scope, core_ideas, semantic_description, keywords_synonyms, status) VALUES ('KPR_000001', 'Pigeonhole Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science; Discrete Mathematics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A counting principle stating that if more objects are assigned to fewer containers, at least one container must contain more than one object.', 'Applies to finite counting and allocation arguments in which the number of objects exceeds the number of available categories or containers; it does not by itself identify which container contains the repeated objects.', 'Counting; cardinality comparison; unavoidable repetition', 'A foundational counting principle that converts a simple cardinality inequality into a guaranteed existence result. It is used in combinatorics, discrete mathematics, hashing arguments, and allocation reasoning.', 'Pigeonhole principle; Dirichlet principle; box principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000002', 'Principle of Mathematical Induction', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science; Discrete Mathematics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A proof principle in which a proposition is established for a base case and then shown to propagate from an arbitrary valid case to the next case.', 'Applies to statements indexed over an inductive domain such as the natural numbers; the induction step must explicitly establish the implication from the assumed case to the successor case.', 'Base case; inductive hypothesis; inductive step; well-founded progression', 'Mathematical induction establishes universal statements over inductively structured domains by combining a valid base case with a valid propagation step.', 'Mathematical induction; induction principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000003', 'Equivalence-Class Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science; Discrete Mathematics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The principle that an equivalence relation partitions a set into disjoint equivalence classes, with every element belonging to exactly one class.', 'Applies when the relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. It should not be generalized to arbitrary relations.', 'Reflexivity; symmetry; transitivity; partitioning', 'An equivalence relation induces a partition of a set into classes of mutually equivalent elements. The resulting quotient structure supports classification and abstraction.', 'Equivalence-class principle; quotient-set principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000004', 'Conservation of Mass', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering; Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The principle that mass is conserved in a system except where the system boundary permits mass to enter or leave, with accumulation equal to inflow minus outflow plus any specified generation or consumption terms.', 'Applies to defined system or control-volume boundaries and requires consistent accounting of all relevant mass streams; chemical reaction changes species composition but does not destroy total mass in ordinary engineering balances.', 'Mass balance; accumulation; inflow; outflow; control volume', 'Conservation of mass provides the fundamental balance relation used to formulate material balances in fluid systems, process calculations, reactors, and environmental systems.', 'Mass conservation; mass-balance principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000005', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering; Civil Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The principle that energy cannot be created or destroyed; for a defined system or control volume, energy changes are accounted for through transfers such as heat and work and, where applicable, mass flow.', 'Applies to properly defined system boundaries and consistent energy accounting. It does not imply that energy quality or ability to perform useful work is conserved.', 'Energy balance; heat; work; internal energy; mass-energy transfer', 'Conservation of energy provides the first-order accounting principle underlying energy balances across thermodynamics, fluid systems, heat transfer, and process engineering.', 'Energy conservation; first-law conservation principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000006', 'Second Law of Thermodynamics', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'The second law establishes the directionality of spontaneous thermodynamic processes and the non-decrease of total entropy for an isolated system.', 'Applies to thermodynamic processes and cycles subject to the stated system boundaries and equilibrium assumptions; it constrains achievable conversion efficiency and distinguishes reversible from irreversible processes.', 'Entropy; irreversibility; directionality; reversible limit; efficiency limit', 'The second law supplements energy conservation by imposing feasibility and direction constraints on thermodynamic processes.', 'Second law; entropy principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000007', 'Newton''s Second Law of Motion', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Civil Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'The net external force on a body equals the time rate of change of its linear momentum; for constant mass, this reduces to force equal to mass times acceleration.', 'Applies in inertial reference frames and with forces defined consistently. The simple F = ma form assumes constant mass; variable-mass systems require momentum accounting.', 'Force; momentum; acceleration; inertial frame', 'Newton''s second law provides the governing dynamic relation connecting applied forces to translational motion and momentum change.', 'Newton''s second law; equation of motion', 'Active'), ('KPR_000008', 'Newton''s Third Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Civil Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'When two bodies interact, the force exerted by one on the other is accompanied by an equal-magnitude force exerted by the other in the opposite direction.', 'Applies to paired interaction forces between bodies; the forces act on different bodies and therefore should not be cancelled within the free-body diagram of a single body.', 'Action-reaction; interaction pair; force balance', 'Newton''s third law describes the paired nature of interaction forces and is fundamental to free-body analysis and contact-force reasoning.', 'Newton''s third law; action-reaction principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000009', 'Hooke''s Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Constitutive Principle', 'Atomic', 'Within the linear elastic range of a material or idealized element, deformation is proportional to the applied load or stress is proportional to strain.', 'Applies only within the specified linear-elastic regime and under the assumptions of the adopted material model; it does not describe plastic or strongly nonlinear response.', 'Elasticity; proportionality; stiffness; stress-strain relation', 'Hooke''s law supplies the constitutive relation between stress and strain in the linear elastic regime and underlies stiffness calculations.', 'Hooke''s law; linear elasticity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000010', 'Principle of Superposition', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a linear system, the response to a combination of independent inputs equals the sum of the responses to each input acting separately.', 'Requires linearity of the governing system over the range considered. It does not generally apply to nonlinear material behaviour, nonlinear circuits, or nonlinear dynamics.', 'Linearity; additive response; independent loading', 'Superposition allows complex linear responses to be decomposed into simpler component responses and then recombined.', 'Superposition principle; linear superposition', 'Active'), ('KPR_000011', 'Continuity Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Chemical Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The continuity principle expresses conservation of mass through a control volume, relating mass accumulation to net mass flow across the boundary.', 'Applies to defined control volumes or streamtubes with consistent density, velocity, and area descriptions; simplified forms require the relevant steady-state and incompressibility assumptions.', 'Mass conservation; flux; accumulation; flow rate', 'The continuity principle is the mass-conservation foundation for fluid-flow analysis and pipe/network calculations.', 'Continuity equation; continuity principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000012', 'Bernoulli Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For steady, incompressible flow along a streamline under the stated ideal-flow assumptions, pressure, elevation, and kinetic-energy terms satisfy a constant mechanical-energy relation.', 'The elementary form applies to steady, incompressible, inviscid flow along a streamline with no shaft work or heat-transfer contribution between the selected points; extended energy equations are required otherwise.', 'Pressure head; velocity head; elevation head; mechanical energy', 'Bernoulli''s principle is an energy relation for idealized fluid flow and provides a basis for interpreting pressure-velocity-elevation trade-offs.', 'Bernoulli equation; Bernoulli principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000013', 'Darcy''s Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'Darcy''s law relates the specific discharge through a porous medium to the hydraulic gradient and hydraulic conductivity under the conditions for which Darcy flow is valid.', 'Applies to laminar flow through porous media represented by an appropriate hydraulic conductivity and gradient; it is not a universal law for non-Darcy or highly inertial porous-media flow.', 'Hydraulic gradient; hydraulic conductivity; specific discharge; porous medium', 'Darcy''s law provides the constitutive relation used to quantify groundwater and porous-media flow.', 'Darcy law; Darcy''s equation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000014', 'Mohr-Coulomb Failure Criterion', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering', 'Criterion', 'Atomic', 'The Mohr-Coulomb criterion states that shear failure occurs when the mobilized shear stress reaches a resistance governed by effective normal stress, cohesion, and friction angle.', 'Applies as an idealized frictional failure criterion for soils and rocks within the assumptions of the selected effective-stress and strength model; it does not capture all nonlinear or strain-dependent failure behaviour.', 'Shear strength; effective stress; cohesion; friction angle', 'The criterion provides a simplified failure envelope for frictional geomaterials and supports bearing, slope, excavation, and foundation assessments.', 'Mohr-Coulomb criterion; Coulomb failure criterion', 'Active'), ('KPR_000015', 'Fourier''s Law of Heat Conduction', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'Fourier''s law states that conductive heat flux is proportional to the negative temperature gradient, with thermal conductivity providing the proportionality factor.', 'Applies to conductive heat transfer within the assumptions of the constitutive model; thermal conductivity may depend on temperature, direction, material state, and microstructure.', 'Temperature gradient; thermal conductivity; conductive heat flux', 'Fourier''s law is the constitutive basis for conduction heat-transfer equations and thermal resistance formulations.', 'Fourier law; law of heat conduction', 'Active'), ('KPR_000016', 'Fick''s First Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'Fick''s first law relates diffusive flux to the negative concentration gradient for diffusion under conditions where the constitutive law is applicable.', 'Applies primarily to steady or quasi-steady diffusion representations with an appropriate concentration coordinate and constant or specified diffusivity; transient accumulation requires the second law.', 'Concentration gradient; diffusivity; diffusive flux', 'Fick''s first law provides the constitutive relation for molecular diffusion and mass-transfer calculations.', 'Fick''s first law; diffusion law', 'Active'), ('KPR_000017', 'Arrhenius Temperature Dependence', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Constitutive Principle', 'Atomic', 'The Arrhenius relation represents the temperature dependence of a reaction-rate constant through an exponential dependence on inverse absolute temperature and activation energy.', 'Applies to reaction systems for which the Arrhenius representation is an adequate kinetic model over the stated temperature range; it is not a universal mechanistic explanation of all temperature effects.', 'Rate constant; activation energy; absolute temperature; exponential dependence', 'The Arrhenius relation connects temperature with kinetic rate constants and is widely used in reaction engineering and kinetic parameter estimation.', 'Arrhenius equation; Arrhenius relation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000018', 'Le Chatelier''s Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'When an equilibrium system is subjected to a change in conditions, its equilibrium composition shifts in the direction that tends to oppose the imposed change.', 'Applies to systems at or near chemical equilibrium under the specified perturbation; it predicts the direction of response rather than providing the complete quantitative equilibrium composition.', 'Equilibrium; perturbation; composition shift; temperature; pressure', 'Le Chatelier''s principle provides qualitative reasoning about the response of chemical equilibria to changes in operating conditions.', 'Le Chatelier principle; equilibrium-shift principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000019', 'Law of Mass Action', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'At chemical equilibrium, the equilibrium composition is related to the activities or concentrations of reactants and products through an equilibrium constant for the specified reaction.', 'Applies to reactions represented under the adopted thermodynamic and concentration/activity convention; equilibrium constants depend on temperature and the chosen standard-state formulation.', 'Equilibrium constant; activities; concentrations; stoichiometry', 'The law of mass action provides the foundation for equilibrium-constant expressions and quantitative equilibrium calculations.', 'Mass-action law; law of mass action', 'Active'), ('KPR_000020', 'Ohm''s Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'For an ohmic element under specified conditions, voltage is proportional to current with resistance as the proportionality factor.', 'Applies to elements exhibiting approximately linear voltage-current behaviour over the operating range; many semiconductor and nonlinear devices do not obey a constant-resistance form.', 'Voltage; current; resistance; linear electrical behaviour', 'Ohm''s law is the constitutive relation for linear resistive electrical elements and is used in circuit analysis.', 'Ohm law; V-I relation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000021', 'Kirchhoff''s Current Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The algebraic sum of currents at a circuit node is zero, reflecting conservation of electric charge under the lumped-circuit model.', 'Applies at circuit nodes under the adopted lumped-element approximation; displacement-current effects and distributed electromagnetic behaviour may require a field-based treatment.', 'Charge conservation; node current; current balance', 'Kirchhoff''s current law provides the node-balance equation used in electrical network analysis.', 'KCL; Kirchhoff current law', 'Active'), ('KPR_000022', 'Kirchhoff''s Voltage Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The algebraic sum of voltage changes around a closed circuit loop is zero under the lumped-circuit approximation.', 'Applies to lumped circuits where the magnetic flux linkage and distributed electromagnetic effects are represented consistently; rapidly varying electromagnetic systems may require Maxwell-based analysis.', 'Voltage balance; loop; potential difference; energy accounting', 'The algebraic sum of voltage changes around a closed circuit loop is zero under the lumped-circuit approximation. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to lumped circuits where the magnetic flux linkage and distributed electromagnetic effects are represented consistently; rapidly varying electromagnetic systems may require Maxwell-based analysis. Key ideas include Voltage balance; loop; potential difference; energy accounting.', 'KVL; Kirchhoff voltage law', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000023', 'Faraday''s Law of Electromagnetic Induction', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'A time-varying magnetic flux through a circuit induces an electromotive force proportional to the negative rate of change of flux linkage.', 'Applies to electromagnetic induction with the sign convention represented by Lenz''s law; circuit-level and field-level formulations must use compatible definitions of flux and induced emf.', 'Magnetic flux; induced emf; flux linkage; time variation', 'Faraday''s law connects changing magnetic flux with induced voltage and is foundational to transformers, generators, and inductive systems.', 'Faraday law; electromagnetic induction', 'Active'), ('KPR_000024', 'Lenz''s Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'The induced current or electromotive response has a direction that opposes the change in magnetic flux responsible for the induction.', 'Applies to electromagnetic induction and determines the sign/direction of induced response consistently with energy conservation.', 'Induced current; opposing change; magnetic flux; direction', 'Lenz''s law gives the directional interpretation of electromagnetic induction and is the sign principle accompanying Faraday''s law.', 'Lenz law; opposition principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000025', 'Maximum Power Transfer Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a linear source network with fixed source parameters, maximum power is delivered to a load when the load resistance equals the source''s Thevenin resistance in a resistive network.', 'Applies to the stated source/load model and assumes the source network and load conditions represented by the theorem; reactive networks require the corresponding complex-conjugate matching condition.', 'Load matching; Thevenin resistance; delivered power; source network', 'The principle identifies the impedance-matching condition that maximizes power delivered to a specified load.', 'Maximum power transfer theorem; load matching', 'Active'), ('KPR_000026', 'Sampling Theorem', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'A band-limited continuous-time signal can be reconstructed from uniformly spaced samples when the sampling frequency is greater than twice the highest frequency present.', 'Requires the signal to be appropriately band-limited and sampling to satisfy the stated conditions; practical reconstruction also depends on filtering and implementation quality.', 'Band limitation; sampling frequency; Nyquist frequency; reconstruction', 'The sampling theorem establishes the minimum theoretical sampling rate needed to avoid aliasing for an ideal band-limited signal.', 'Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem; sampling theorem', 'Active'), ('KPR_000027', 'Routh-Hurwitz Stability Criterion', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Electrical Engineering', 'Criterion', 'Atomic', 'The Routh-Hurwitz criterion determines whether all roots of a real polynomial lie in the open left half-plane using coefficients arranged in a Routh array.', 'Applies to characteristic polynomials with the conditions and coefficient structure required by the criterion; special cases such as zero rows require the established Routh procedures.', 'Characteristic polynomial; pole location; stability; Routh array', 'The criterion provides an algebraic test of continuous-time linear-system stability without explicitly solving for all polynomial roots.', 'Routh criterion; Routh-Hurwitz test', 'Active'), ('KPR_000028', 'Nyquist Stability Criterion', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Criterion', 'Atomic', 'The Nyquist criterion determines closed-loop stability from the encirclement of critical points by the frequency response of the open-loop transfer function, together with the open-loop pole information.', 'Applies to linear feedback systems under the assumptions of the Nyquist formulation and requires correct accounting of unstable open-loop poles and encirclement direction.', 'Frequency response; encirclement; open-loop poles; closed-loop stability', 'The criterion links frequency-domain response to closed-loop stability and provides a basis for stability analysis of feedback systems.', 'Nyquist criterion; Nyquist stability test', 'Active'), ('KPR_000029', 'Bayes'' Rule', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science; Statistics; Machine Learning', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'Bayes'' rule relates a posterior probability to a likelihood, prior probability, and evidence through conditional probability.', 'Applies when the relevant conditional and marginal probabilities are defined; probability-zero evidence requires appropriate treatment and the interpretation depends on the chosen probabilistic model.', 'Conditional probability; prior; likelihood; posterior; evidence', 'Bayes'' rule provides the probability-update relationship underlying Bayesian inference, classification, diagnosis, and decision analysis.', 'Bayes rule; Bayes theorem', 'Active'), ('KPR_000030', 'CAP Theorem', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'The CAP theorem states that a distributed data system cannot simultaneously provide all three of strong consistency, availability, and partition tolerance under a network partition.', 'Applies to distributed systems in which network partitions are possible and to the precise consistency and availability definitions used in the theorem; it is not a statement that systems can provide only two properties under all conditions.', 'Consistency; availability; partition tolerance; distributed systems', 'CAP provides a reasoning framework for understanding trade-offs in distributed data systems when partitions occur.', 'CAP theorem; consistency-availability-partition tolerance', 'Active'), ('KPR_000031', 'Byzantine Fault Tolerance Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A Byzantine fault-tolerant system must tolerate arbitrary or inconsistent component behaviour while maintaining the specified correctness properties when the fault assumptions are satisfied.', 'Applies only under explicit assumptions about the number and behaviour of faulty participants, communication model, quorum structure, and protocol guarantees.', 'Arbitrary faults; quorum; agreement; validity; fault threshold', 'The principle defines the need to preserve protocol-level safety and liveness properties despite adversarial or inconsistent component behaviour within the assumed fault model.', 'Byzantine fault tolerance; BFT principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000032', 'End-to-End Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The end-to-end principle places functions that depend on complete application-level knowledge at the endpoints rather than relying solely on lower network layers.', 'Applies as an architectural design principle, not an absolute prohibition on lower-layer support; performance, security, reliability, and operational requirements can justify lower-layer mechanisms.', 'Layering; endpoints; application semantics; network architecture', 'The principle guides distributed and network architecture by distinguishing functions that can be implemented effectively at endpoints from those requiring network-layer support.', 'End-to-end argument; end-to-end principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000033', 'Principle of Least Privilege', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A subject should be granted only the minimum privileges necessary to perform its authorized tasks.', 'Applies to access-control design and should be evaluated against actual task requirements, trust boundaries, privilege inheritance, and operational constraints.', 'Authorization; minimum privilege; attack surface; access control', 'Least privilege limits the consequences of misuse or compromise by reducing unnecessary authority.', 'Least privilege; minimum necessary privilege', 'Active'), ('KPR_000034', 'Separation of Concerns Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Computer Science', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A system should separate distinct concerns so that each concern can be reasoned about, developed, tested, and changed with limited interference from others.', 'Applies to architecture and software design; the appropriate separation depends on cohesion, coupling, interfaces, and system requirements.', 'Modularity; cohesion; coupling; concern isolation', 'Separation of concerns improves manageability by localizing responsibilities and reducing unnecessary interactions among unrelated aspects of a system.', 'Separation of concerns; SoC', 'Active'), ('KPR_000035', 'Principle of Least Action', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The actual path of a system between specified states makes the action stationary with respect to permitted variations under the formulation of classical mechanics.', 'Applies within the variational formulation and boundary conditions of the physical system; ''stationary'' is more precise than universally describing the action as strictly minimal.', 'Action; Lagrangian; stationary variation; trajectory', 'The principle of stationary action provides a unifying variational formulation for classical mechanics and connects directly to Euler-Lagrange equations.', 'Least action; stationary action; Hamilton''s principle', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000036', 'Archimedes'' Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the body.', 'Applies to bodies immersed in fluids under hydrostatic assumptions appropriate to the formulation; dynamic flow effects require additional analysis.', 'Buoyancy; displaced fluid; hydrostatic pressure', 'A body immersed in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the body. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to bodies immersed in fluids under hydrostatic assumptions appropriate to the formulation; dynamic flow effects require additional analysis. Key ideas include Buoyancy; displaced fluid; hydrostatic pressure.', 'Archimedes principle; buoyancy principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000037', 'Pascal''s Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A pressure change applied to a confined fluid is transmitted throughout the fluid in accordance with the hydrostatic pressure relation.', 'Applies to confined fluids under the assumptions of Pascal''s law; hydraulic-machine applications additionally require accounting for geometry, losses, and mechanical efficiency.', 'Pressure transmission; confined fluid; hydraulic amplification', 'A pressure change applied to a confined fluid is transmitted throughout the fluid in accordance with the hydrostatic pressure relation. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to confined fluids under the assumptions of Pascal''s law; hydraulic-machine applications additionally require accounting for geometry, losses, and mechanical efficiency. Key ideas include Pressure transmission; confined fluid; hydraulic amplification.', 'Pascal law; Pascal principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000038', 'Reynolds Similarity Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Dynamic similarity requires corresponding dimensionless groups to be matched when comparing geometrically similar flows under the relevant governing physics.', 'Applies when the selected dimensionless groups capture the dominant physical mechanisms; matching Reynolds number alone is insufficient when other forces or transport mechanisms are significant.', 'Dynamic similarity; Reynolds number; dimensionless groups; scaling', 'Similarity principles support model testing and scale-up by identifying dimensionless conditions that preserve the dominant physical behaviour.', 'Dynamic similarity; similitude principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000039', 'Conservation of Momentum', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The total linear momentum of a system changes only through the net external impulse or, in control-volume form, the net momentum flux and external forces.', 'Applies to a defined system or control volume with all relevant external forces and momentum fluxes accounted for; simplified forms require explicit assumptions.', 'Momentum balance; external force; momentum flux; impulse', 'Momentum conservation is the governing balance principle for dynamics, fluid momentum equations, jet forces, and process equipment analysis.', 'Momentum conservation; momentum-balance principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000040', 'Conservation of Charge', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'Electric charge is conserved: the net charge of an isolated system remains constant, with current representing charge transport across boundaries.', 'Applies to electromagnetic systems subject to charge continuity; circuit and field formulations must account for charge accumulation consistently.', 'Charge conservation; current; continuity equation; charge density', 'Electric charge is conserved: the net charge of an isolated system remains constant, with current representing charge transport across boundaries. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to electromagnetic systems subject to charge continuity; circuit and field formulations must account for charge accumulation consistently. Key ideas include Charge conservation; current; continuity equation; charge density.', 'Charge conservation; charge continuity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000041', 'Principle of Effective Stress', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For saturated soil under the classical effective-stress framework, the stress governing skeleton response is total stress minus pore-water pressure.', 'Applies to the assumptions of saturated soil mechanics and the adopted stress convention; partially saturated and complex multiphase systems require extended formulations.', 'Total stress; pore pressure; effective stress; soil skeleton', 'Effective stress separates total stress from pore-fluid pressure and is central to strength, consolidation, and deformation analysis.', 'Effective stress principle; Terzaghi effective stress principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000042', 'Principle of Virtual Work', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A body or structural system in equilibrium has zero virtual work for every kinematically admissible virtual displacement consistent with the constraints.', 'Applies to equilibrium and compatible virtual displacements under the adopted idealization; the virtual displacement is a mathematical variation, not necessarily an actual motion.', 'Equilibrium; virtual displacement; admissibility; work', 'The principle provides an alternative formulation of equilibrium and supports structural analysis, finite elements, and mechanism reasoning.', 'Virtual work principle; principle of virtual work', 'Active'), ('KPR_000043', 'Principle of Feedback', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Feedback uses measured or inferred system output to influence subsequent system input in order to modify system behaviour relative to a desired condition.', 'Applies to feedback architectures with explicitly defined sensing, comparison, actuation, and dynamics; feedback does not inherently guarantee stability or improved performance.', 'Feedback loop; reference; error; controller; response', 'The feedback principle is the conceptual basis for closed-loop control and regulation.', 'Feedback principle; closed-loop principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000044', 'Separation Principle for Linear Control', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Under the conditions of the linear-quadratic-Gaussian framework, controller design and state estimation can be treated independently and then combined.', 'Applies specifically under the assumptions of the LQG formulation; it should not be generalized to arbitrary nonlinear, constrained, or non-Gaussian control problems.', 'LQG; state estimation; controller design; separation', 'The separation principle simplifies LQG design by allowing estimator and controller components to be designed independently under the stated assumptions.', 'LQG separation principle; LQG; state estimation; controller design; separation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000045', 'Principle of Mass Transfer Driving Force', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Mass-transfer rate is driven by a difference between the actual state and an appropriate equilibrium or reference state, with resistance or transfer coefficients relating the driving force to flux.', 'Applies to the selected mass-transfer representation and phase-equilibrium assumptions; the definition of driving force depends on the chosen concentration, partial-pressure, fugacity, or other basis.', 'Driving force; equilibrium; transfer coefficient; resistance', 'The driving-force principle provides the conceptual basis for film, resistance, and overall mass-transfer formulations.', 'Mass-transfer driving force principle; Driving force; equilibrium; transfer coefficient; resistance', 'Active'), ('KPR_000046', 'Principle of Thermal Resistance', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For one-dimensional steady conduction or analogous linear heat-transfer paths, heat-transfer rate is proportional to the temperature difference divided by the total thermal resistance.', 'Applies to linearized, steady heat-transfer networks where resistances can be defined consistently; contact, radiation, and nonlinear conductivity may require modified representations.', 'Temperature difference; thermal resistance; heat-transfer rate; series/parallel resistance', 'For one-dimensional steady conduction or analogous linear heat-transfer paths, heat-transfer rate is proportional to the temperature difference divided by the total thermal resistance. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to linearized, steady heat-transfer networks where resistances can be defined consistently; contact, radiation, and nonlinear conductivity may require modified representations. Key ideas include Temperature difference; thermal resistance; heat-transfer rate; series/parallel resistance.', 'Thermal resistance principle; resistance-network method', 'Active'), ('KPR_000047', 'Principle of Dimensional Homogeneity', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A physically meaningful engineering equation must be dimensionally homogeneous, with quantities on each additive side expressed in compatible dimensions.', 'Applies to equations representing physical quantities; dimensional consistency is necessary but does not by itself establish physical correctness.', 'Dimensions; units; dimensional consistency; scaling', 'Dimensional homogeneity provides a basic validation rule for physical equations and supports dimensional analysis.', 'Principle of dimensional homogeneity; dimensional consistency', 'Active'), ('KPR_000048', 'Buckingham Pi Theorem', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering; Chemical Engineering', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'A dimensionally homogeneous physical relationship involving a specified set of dimensional variables can be represented using a reduced number of independent dimensionless groups.', 'Applies when the variables and fundamental dimensions are correctly identified and the dimensional matrix has the required rank.', 'Dimensionless groups; dimensional matrix; variable reduction; similarity', 'The theorem provides the formal foundation for dimensional analysis and the construction of dimensionless correlations.', 'Buckingham Pi theorem; Pi theorem', 'Active'), ('KPR_000049', 'Principle of Minimum Potential Energy', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a stable conservative elastic system under appropriate constraints, the equilibrium configuration corresponds to a local minimum of total potential energy.', 'Applies to conservative systems and stable equilibrium configurations within the adopted elastic model; unstable equilibria are not minima.', 'Potential energy; equilibrium; stability; conservative system', 'The principle provides an energy-based formulation of equilibrium and is widely used in structural mechanics and variational methods.', 'Minimum potential energy principle; Potential energy; equilibrium; stability; conservative system', 'Active'), ('KPR_000050', 'Principle of Virtual Displacements', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a system in static equilibrium, the total virtual work of external and internal forces is zero for every admissible virtual displacement.', 'Applies to static equilibrium under compatible virtual displacements and the adopted idealization.', 'Virtual displacement; equilibrium; internal work; external work', 'The principle converts equilibrium into a variational statement useful for structural analysis and finite-element formulations.', 'Virtual displacement principle; virtual work formulation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000051', 'Principle of Superposition in Linear Circuits', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'In a linear electrical network, the response produced by multiple independent sources equals the sum of responses produced by each source acting alone, with other independent sources deactivated appropriately.', 'Requires linear circuit elements and linear operating conditions; dependent sources remain active according to the circuit model.', 'Linearity; independent sources; additive response; circuit analysis', 'The circuit form of superposition supports decomposition of linear network responses into source-by-source contributions.', 'Circuit superposition theorem; Linearity; independent sources; additive response; circuit analysis', 'Active'), ('KPR_000052', 'Thevenin Equivalence Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'Any linear two-terminal network can be represented at its terminals by an equivalent voltage source in series with an equivalent resistance or impedance.', 'Applies to linear two-terminal networks and the selected terminal conditions; nonlinear networks require an appropriate local or nonlinear equivalent treatment.', 'Two-terminal network; equivalent source; Thevenin voltage; Thevenin resistance', 'Thevenin equivalence reduces a linear network to a compact source-load representation for analysis and design.', 'Thevenin theorem; Thevenin equivalent', 'Active'), ('KPR_000053', 'Norton''s Equivalence Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'Any linear two-terminal network can be represented by an equivalent current source in parallel with an equivalent resistance or impedance.', 'Applies to linear two-terminal networks under the adopted circuit model.', 'Two-terminal network; equivalent current source; Norton current; Norton resistance', 'Any linear two-terminal network can be represented by an equivalent current source in parallel with an equivalent resistance or impedance. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to linear two-terminal networks under the adopted circuit model. Key ideas include Two-terminal network; equivalent current source; Norton current; Norton resistance.', 'Norton theorem; Norton equivalent', 'Active'), ('KPR_000055', 'Precautionary Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Where there are plausible risks of serious or irreversible environmental harm, lack of full scientific certainty should not automatically be used as a reason to postpone proportionate preventive measures.', 'Applies to environmental decision-making under scientific uncertainty and must be interpreted with the legal threshold, proportionality, evidence, and procedural requirements of the governing legal regime.', 'Scientific uncertainty; prevention; serious harm; proportionality', 'The precautionary principle guides decisions where environmental risk may be significant but scientific evidence remains incomplete.', 'Precautionary principle; precaution', 'Active'), ('KPR_000056', 'Polluter Pays Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'The polluter pays principle assigns the costs of preventing, controlling, and remedying pollution to the party responsible for the pollution, subject to the applicable legal framework.', 'Applies as a legal/policy principle within the relevant jurisdiction and does not automatically determine every question of liability, compensation, or causation.', 'Polluter responsibility; prevention costs; remediation; environmental liability', 'The principle supports allocation of pollution-control and remediation costs to responsible actors.', 'Polluter pays principle; polluter pays', 'Active'), ('KPR_000057', 'Sustainable Development Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law; Climate Change Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'The sustainable development principle requires decision-making to balance present development needs with the protection of environmental and social conditions needed for future generations.', 'Applies as a legal and governance principle subject to the constitutional, statutory, and judicial framework of the jurisdiction; it does not prescribe a single universal balancing formula.', 'Intergenerational equity; development; environmental protection; balancing', 'The principle frames development decisions as requiring integration of economic, social, and environmental considerations.', 'Sustainable development principle; sustainable development', 'Active'), ('KPR_000058', 'Public Trust Doctrine', 'Law', 'Environmental Law', 'Legal Doctrine', 'Atomic', 'The public trust doctrine holds that certain natural resources are subject to public-interest obligations and cannot be treated solely as ordinary private assets where the doctrine applies.', 'Applies to resources and circumstances recognized by the governing legal system; the precise scope of trusteeship and remedies is jurisdiction-specific.', 'Public resources; trusteeship; public interest; natural resources', 'The doctrine constrains state or resource-management decisions by recognizing duties associated with resources held for public benefit.', 'Public trust doctrine; public trust', 'Active'), ('KPR_000059', 'Intergenerational Equity Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law; Climate Change Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Environmental decision-making should account for the rights and interests of future generations when present actions can impose long-lived environmental costs or reduce future options.', 'Applies where recognized in the governing legal or policy framework; it does not require all present benefits to be rejected in favour of future interests.', 'Future generations; long-term impacts; environmental inheritance; equity', 'Intergenerational equity introduces a temporal dimension to environmental governance and sustainability decisions.', 'Intergenerational equity; equity across generations', 'Active'), ('KPR_000060', 'Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle', 'Law', 'Climate Change Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'States share responsibility for addressing global environmental problems while their responsibilities can differ according to relevant circumstances, including historical contribution and capacity, as recognized in the applicable regime.', 'Applies within the specific international environmental or climate regime in which the principle is invoked; its legal effect depends on the treaty and negotiating context.', 'Common responsibility; differentiation; historical contribution; capacity', 'CBDR provides a normative basis for differentiated responsibilities in international environmental and climate governance.', 'CBDR; common but differentiated responsibilities', 'Active'), ('KPR_000061', 'No-Harm Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law; Climate Change Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'States should ensure that activities within their jurisdiction or control do not cause significant environmental damage to other states or areas beyond national jurisdiction, subject to the applicable international legal framework.', 'Applies to transboundary environmental harm and is interpreted through the relevant customary and treaty law; significance and due-diligence requirements are context-dependent.', 'Transboundary harm; due diligence; territorial jurisdiction; environmental impact', 'The no-harm principle establishes a foundational constraint on activities that may cause significant transboundary environmental damage.', 'No-harm rule; transboundary harm principle', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000062', 'Environmental Participation Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Law; Public Policy', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Affected people and stakeholders should have meaningful opportunities to participate in environmental decision-making, subject to the governing legal and procedural framework.', 'Applies to participation processes recognized by law or policy; participation does not necessarily imply a veto over the final decision.', 'Access to information; participation; consultation; procedural fairness', 'Participation strengthens procedural legitimacy and can improve the information available to environmental decision-makers.', 'Participation principle; public participation', 'Active'), ('KPR_000063', 'Rule of Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Public and private actors are governed by publicly established law, with legal accountability, procedural fairness, and limits on arbitrary exercise of power.', 'Applies as a constitutional and legal governance principle whose precise institutional requirements depend on the jurisdiction.', 'Legality; accountability; predictability; equality before law; judicial review', 'The rule of law provides a foundational governance principle for lawful, reviewable, and non-arbitrary exercise of authority.', 'Rule of law; legality principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000064', 'Principle of Natural Justice', 'Law', 'Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Natural justice requires fair decision-making procedures, commonly including an opportunity to be heard and an impartial decision-maker, subject to the governing legal framework.', 'Applies to administrative, disciplinary, and other decision contexts where procedural fairness is required; content varies with the statutory and factual setting.', 'Audi alteram partem; impartiality; hearing; procedural fairness', 'Natural justice provides a foundational standard for fair administrative and adjudicative procedures.', 'Natural justice; procedural fairness', 'Active'), ('KPR_000065', 'Principle of Proportionality', 'Law', 'Law; Public Policy', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'A public measure restricting a protected interest should bear a rational and proportionate relationship to a legitimate objective, with the intensity of review determined by the applicable legal framework.', 'Applies within the relevant constitutional or administrative-law test; the elements of proportionality differ across jurisdictions and legal contexts.', 'Legitimate aim; suitability; necessity; balancing', 'Proportionality structures judicial or administrative review of measures that affect protected rights or interests.', 'Proportionality principle; proportionality test', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000066', 'Pacta Sunt Servanda', 'Law', 'Contract Law', 'Legal Doctrine', 'Atomic', 'A validly formed contract is to be performed in good faith according to its agreed terms, subject to applicable law and recognized grounds for modification or avoidance.', 'Applies to binding agreements within the governing contract-law regime; mandatory law, impossibility, illegality, and recognized doctrines can qualify performance obligations.', 'Binding agreement; performance; good faith; contractual obligation', 'The doctrine underpins contractual reliability by treating agreed obligations as legally significant commitments.', 'Pacta sunt servanda; agreements must be kept', 'Active'), ('KPR_000067', 'Contra Proferentem Principle', 'Law', 'Contract Law', 'Legal Doctrine', 'Atomic', 'Where an ambiguous contractual term remains unresolved under the applicable interpretive rules, the ambiguity may be construed against the party responsible for drafting the term.', 'Applies only where the governing legal system recognizes the doctrine and where ambiguity remains after applicable interpretive principles have been considered.', 'Contract interpretation; ambiguity; drafting party; allocation of uncertainty', 'The principle allocates unresolved drafting ambiguity against the party that introduced the wording in contexts where the doctrine applies.', 'Contra proferentem; Contract interpretation; ambiguity; drafting party; allocation of uncertainty', 'Active'), ('KPR_000068', 'Separate Legal Personality Doctrine', 'Law', 'Corporate Law', 'Legal Doctrine', 'Atomic', 'A corporation has a legal personality distinct from its shareholders or members, so corporate rights and liabilities are ordinarily separate from those of the individuals behind the entity.', 'Applies to incorporated entities subject to statutory exceptions, including circumstances in which courts or legislation permit attribution, lifting of the corporate veil, or direct liability.', 'Corporate personality; separate entity; shareholder liability; veil', 'Separate legal personality provides the foundational legal structure for corporate rights, obligations, ownership, and liability.', 'Separate legal personality; corporate personality', 'Active'), ('KPR_000069', 'Fiduciary Duty Principle', 'Law', 'Corporate Law', 'Legal Doctrine', 'Atomic', 'A fiduciary must exercise entrusted power for proper purposes and with the loyalty, care, and conflict-management obligations imposed by the applicable legal regime.', 'Applies only where a fiduciary relationship or statutory fiduciary duty exists; exact duties vary by role and jurisdiction.', 'Loyalty; conflicts of interest; proper purpose; trust; duty of care', 'Fiduciary duties constrain the use of entrusted power and protect beneficiaries, principals, shareholders, or other legally protected interests.', 'Fiduciary duty; duty of loyalty', 'Active'), ('KPR_000070', 'Principle of Informed Consent', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Participation in research should be voluntary and based on adequate information about the study, material risks, potential benefits, and participant rights.', 'Applies to human-subject research subject to the applicable ethics framework; consent requirements may be modified only under recognized ethical and regulatory conditions.', 'Voluntariness; disclosure; autonomy; participant rights', 'Informed consent protects participant autonomy and supports ethically valid participation in research.', 'Informed consent; consent principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000071', 'Principle of Respect for Persons', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Research should respect individuals as autonomous agents and provide additional protections for people with diminished autonomy.', 'Applies to human-subject research and requires assessment of autonomy, vulnerability, and appropriate protections.', 'Autonomy; vulnerability; protection; voluntary participation', 'Respect for persons is a foundational research-ethics principle underlying informed consent and additional safeguards for vulnerable participants.', 'Respect for persons; autonomy principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000072', 'Principle of Beneficence', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Research should seek to maximize reasonably expected benefits and minimize foreseeable harms to participants and affected groups.', 'Applies to ethical research planning and requires context-specific assessment of risks, benefits, alternatives, and mitigation measures.', 'Risk-benefit assessment; harm minimization; benefit maximization', 'Beneficence provides a framework for evaluating whether research risks are justified by anticipated benefits and whether harms can be reduced.', 'Beneficence; risk-benefit principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000073', 'Principle of Justice in Research', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'The burdens and benefits of research should be distributed fairly, with participant selection and inclusion justified by the research question rather than convenience or exploitation.', 'Applies to participant selection, access, compensation, and distribution of research burdens and benefits under the relevant ethical framework.', 'Fair selection; distribution; inclusion; exploitation', 'The justice principle addresses fairness in who bears research risks and who can benefit from research participation.', 'Research justice; justice principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000074', 'Principle of Evidence-Based Policy', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Policy decisions should systematically use the best available relevant evidence while explicitly accounting for uncertainty, values, feasibility, and distributional consequences.', 'Applies to policy formulation and evaluation; evidence is one input to decision-making and does not eliminate legitimate value judgments or political choices.', 'Evidence; uncertainty; evaluation; policy design; implementation', 'Evidence-based policy improves the traceability of policy reasoning by distinguishing empirical claims from values, assumptions, and implementation constraints.', 'Evidence-based policymaking; evidence-informed policy', 'Active'), ('KPR_000075', 'Principle of Transparency', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Public institutions should make decision-relevant information sufficiently accessible, clear, and timely to permit understanding and scrutiny, subject to legitimate confidentiality limits.', 'Applies to public governance and disclosure systems within the applicable legal framework; transparency does not require unrestricted disclosure of protected information.', 'Disclosure; accessibility; clarity; public scrutiny', 'Transparency supports accountability by enabling stakeholders to understand decisions, processes, resource use, and performance.', 'Transparency principle; openness', 'Active'), ('KPR_000076', 'Principle of Accountability', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Actors entrusted with public authority or resources should explain and justify their decisions and remain subject to appropriate oversight and consequences.', 'Applies to the institutional roles and accountability mechanisms established by the relevant governance framework.', 'Answerability; oversight; justification; consequences', 'Actors entrusted with public authority or resources should explain and justify their decisions and remain subject to appropriate oversight and consequences. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to the institutional roles and accountability mechanisms established by the relevant governance framework. Key ideas include Answerability; oversight; justification; consequences.', 'Accountability principle; public accountability', 'Active'), ('KPR_000077', 'Principle of Participation', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'People affected by public decisions should have meaningful opportunities to contribute information, preferences, or deliberative input through appropriate processes.', 'Applies to participatory governance mechanisms appropriate to the decision context; participation does not necessarily confer decision rights.', 'Stakeholder participation; consultation; deliberation; inclusion', 'Participation improves procedural legitimacy and can contribute local knowledge and diverse perspectives to policy decisions.', 'Participation principle; participatory governance', 'Active'), ('KPR_000078', 'Opportunity-Cost Principle', 'Social Science', 'Development Economics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The opportunity cost of choosing an option is the value of the best feasible alternative forgone.', 'Applies when alternatives are mutually constrained by scarce resources, time, or other decision limits; the relevant opportunity cost depends on the feasible choice set.', 'Scarcity; trade-off; alternative forgone; resource allocation', 'Opportunity cost provides the basic decision principle for reasoning about trade-offs under scarcity.', 'Opportunity cost; cost of forgone alternative', 'Active'), ('KPR_000079', 'Principle of Diminishing Marginal Returns', 'Social Science', 'Development Economics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Holding other relevant inputs fixed, successive additions of a variable input eventually produce smaller increments in output under the conditions of the production model.', 'Applies to the specified production function and input regime; it does not imply that total output necessarily decreases when the variable input rises.', 'Marginal product; fixed input; variable input; production', 'Diminishing marginal returns explains why the marginal contribution of an input can decline as its use expands while other inputs remain constrained.', 'Diminishing marginal returns; diminishing marginal product', 'Active'), ('KPR_000080', 'Principle of Comparative Advantage', 'Social Science', 'Development Economics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'An agent has comparative advantage in an activity when it can perform that activity at a lower opportunity cost than another agent.', 'Applies to comparative-cost analysis with clearly defined alternatives and opportunity costs; it is distinct from absolute advantage.', 'Opportunity cost; specialization; trade; relative productivity', 'Comparative advantage provides the economic principle explaining why specialization and exchange can create gains from trade even when productivity differs.', 'Comparative advantage; comparative-cost principle', 'Active'), ('KPR_000081', 'Principle of Methodological Transparency', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Research methods, decisions, data-processing steps, analytical procedures, and relevant limitations should be documented sufficiently for others to understand how conclusions were produced.', 'Applies to empirical research and requires disclosure proportionate to the method, data sensitivity, and ethical constraints; it does not require disclosure of protected personal or proprietary information.', 'Reproducibility; documentation; analytic decisions; limitations', 'Research methods, decisions, data-processing steps, analytical procedures, and relevant limitations should be documented sufficiently for others to understand how conclusions were produced. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to empirical research and requires disclosure proportionate to the method, data sensitivity, and ethical constraints; it does not require disclosure of protected personal or proprietary information. Key ideas include Reproducibility; documentation; analytic decisions; limitations.', 'Methodological transparency; transparent reporting', 'Active'), ('KPR_000082', 'Principle of Measurement Validity', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A measurement or operationalization should adequately represent the construct it is intended to measure within the stated theoretical and empirical context.', 'Applies to operational definitions and measurement instruments; validity is evidence-based and context-specific rather than a permanent intrinsic property of an instrument.', 'Construct validity; operationalization; measurement evidence', 'Measurement validity links abstract constructs to observable indicators and supports defensible interpretation of research measures.', 'Validity principle; measurement validity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000083', 'Newton''s First Law of Motion', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Civil Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'A body remains at rest or in uniform straight-line motion unless acted upon by a nonzero net external force.', 'Applies in inertial reference frames and defines the force-free motion assumed in classical mechanics.', 'Inertia; inertial frame; uniform motion; net force', 'Newton''s first law establishes inertial motion and provides the conceptual basis for identifying inertial reference frames and interpreting force as a cause of changes in motion.', 'Newton''s first law; law of inertia', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000084', 'Work-Energy Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering; Civil Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The net work done by forces on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy.', 'Applies to the adopted mechanical system and force/path representation; potential and nonconservative effects must be accounted for consistently.', 'Work; kinetic energy; force; energy transfer', 'The work-energy principle converts force and motion information into an energy relation and can simplify dynamics problems where time is not the primary variable.', 'Work-energy theorem; work-energy principle', 'Unlinked'), ('KPR_000085', 'Conservation of Angular Momentum', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Conservation Principle', 'Atomic', 'The angular momentum of a system changes only through the net external moment about the selected reference point or axis.', 'Applies to a clearly defined system and reference point/axis with all relevant external moments and angular-momentum fluxes accounted for.', 'Angular momentum; external moment; rotational dynamics; system boundary', 'Angular-momentum conservation governs rotational dynamics and supports analysis of gyroscopic motion, rotors, and isolated rotating systems.', 'Angular momentum conservation; rotational conservation law', 'Active'), ('KPR_000086', 'Rational-Agent Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Artificial Intelligence', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'An intelligent agent should select actions that maximize expected performance according to its objectives and available information.', 'Applies to agents making decisions under specified objectives, percepts, actions, and environmental uncertainty; it does not imply perfect rationality or omniscience.', 'objective-directed action; percepts; expected performance; limited information', 'An intelligent agent should select actions that maximize expected performance according to its objectives and available information. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to agents making decisions under specified objectives, percepts, actions, and environmental uncertainty; it does not imply perfect rationality or omniscience. Key ideas include objective-directed action; percepts; expected performance; limited information.', 'rational action; rational agent; expected utility; agent rationality', 'Active'), ('KPR_000087', 'Heuristic Dominance Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Artificial Intelligence', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A more informative admissible heuristic can reduce search effort without sacrificing optimality in algorithms such as A*.', 'Applies to heuristic search when heuristic admissibility and consistency conditions are satisfied.', 'heuristic informativeness; admissibility; search effort; optimality', 'Relates heuristic quality to the number of states explored while preserving solution guarantees under stated conditions.', 'heuristic dominance; admissible heuristic; A*', 'Active'), ('KPR_000088', 'Bayesian Decision Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Artificial Intelligence', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Decisions under uncertainty should combine prior beliefs, observed evidence, and utilities to select actions with highest expected utility.', 'Applies to probabilistic decision problems with explicit beliefs and utility models.', 'prior; evidence; posterior; utility; expected utility', 'Decisions under uncertainty should combine prior beliefs, observed evidence, and utilities to select actions with highest expected utility. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to probabilistic decision problems with explicit beliefs and utility models. Key ideas include prior; evidence; posterior; utility; expected utility.', 'Bayesian decision theory; expected utility', 'Active'), ('KPR_000089', 'Empirical Risk Minimization Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Machine Learning', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A learning algorithm can select a model by minimizing empirical loss over observed training data, subject to the assumptions and regularization used.', 'Applies to supervised learning objectives and does not by itself guarantee generalization.', 'empirical loss; training data; hypothesis class; regularization', 'A learning algorithm can select a model by minimizing empirical loss over observed training data, subject to the assumptions and regularization used. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to supervised learning objectives and does not by itself guarantee generalization. Key ideas include empirical loss; training data; hypothesis class; regularization.', 'ERM; empirical risk', 'Active'), ('KPR_000090', 'Bias-Variance Trade-off Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Machine Learning', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Prediction error can reflect a trade-off among systematic bias, estimator variance, and irreducible noise.', 'Applies to predictive modelling under repeated sampling and is not a universal decomposition for every loss/model without conditions.', 'bias; variance; noise; generalization', 'Prediction error can reflect a trade-off among systematic bias, estimator variance, and irreducible noise. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to predictive modelling under repeated sampling and is not a universal decomposition for every loss/model without conditions. Key ideas include bias; variance; noise; generalization.', 'bias variance tradeoff; model complexity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000091', 'Regularization Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Machine Learning', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Constraining model complexity through a penalty or equivalent structural restriction can reduce overfitting and improve out-of-sample performance.', 'Applies when the regularized objective and penalty are defined; the choice of regularization strength remains data-dependent.', 'complexity control; penalty; overfitting; generalization', 'Links complexity control to generalization rather than treating regularization as a specific algorithm.', 'regularization; complexity penalty', 'Active'), ('KPR_000092', 'No-Free-Lunch Theorem for Optimization', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Machine Learning', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'Without assumptions about the problem distribution, averaged performance across all possible problems cannot make one optimization algorithm universally superior to all others.', 'Scope is theoretical and depends on the specified averaging assumptions; it does not say all algorithms perform identically on real datasets.', 'problem distribution; inductive assumptions; algorithm performance', 'Establishes the importance of inductive bias and problem structure in algorithm selection.', 'no free lunch theorem; NFLT', 'Active'), ('KPR_000094', 'Impossibility of Deterministic Consensus in Asynchronous Systems', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Distributed Systems', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'In a purely asynchronous distributed system with even one possible crash failure, no deterministic consensus protocol can guarantee termination in all executions.', 'Applies to the FLP model and should not be generalized to partially synchronous systems or randomized algorithms.', 'asynchrony; crash failure; consensus; termination', 'In a purely asynchronous distributed system with even one possible crash failure, no deterministic consensus protocol can guarantee termination in all executions. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to the FLP model and should not be generalized to partially synchronous systems or randomized algorithms. Key ideas include asynchrony; crash failure; consensus; termination.', 'FLP impossibility; consensus impossibility', 'Active'), ('KPR_000098', 'Water-Cement Ratio Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Concrete Technology', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a given concrete system and curing regime, the water-to-cementitious-material ratio strongly influences hydration, porosity, strength, and durability.', 'Applies to specified materials, mixture proportions, curing and age; it is not a universal one-variable predictor of every concrete property.', 'water-cement ratio; hydration; porosity; strength; durability', 'For a given concrete system and curing regime, the water-to-cementitious-material ratio strongly influences hydration, porosity, strength, and durability. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to specified materials, mixture proportions, curing and age; it is not a universal one-variable predictor of every concrete property. Key ideas include water-cement ratio; hydration; porosity; strength; durability.', 'water-cement ratio law; Abrams'' law', 'Active'), ('KPR_000099', 'Abrams'' Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Concrete Technology', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'For comparable concrete materials and curing conditions, compressive strength varies systematically with the water-cement ratio, commonly represented by an empirical inverse relationship.', 'Applies within the material, age, curing and mixture ranges for which the empirical relation is established.', 'compressive strength; water-cement ratio; empirical relationship', 'For comparable concrete materials and curing conditions, compressive strength varies systematically with the water-cement ratio, commonly represented by an empirical inverse relationship. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies within the material, age, curing and mixture ranges for which the empirical relation is established. Key ideas include compressive strength; water-cement ratio; empirical relationship.', 'Abrams law; compressive strength; water-cement ratio; empirical relationship', 'Active'), ('KPR_000100', 'Rule of Mixtures', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Engineering Materials', 'Rule', 'Atomic', 'For specified composite assumptions, an effective property can be estimated from constituent properties weighted by their volume fractions.', 'Applies to properties and loading directions for which the assumed mixture model is appropriate.', 'volume fraction; constituent properties; composite property', 'For specified composite assumptions, an effective property can be estimated from constituent properties weighted by their volume fractions. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to properties and loading directions for which the assumed mixture model is appropriate. Key ideas include volume fraction; constituent properties; composite property.', 'rule of mixtures; volume fraction; constituent properties; composite property', 'Active'), ('KPR_000102', 'Darcy-Buckingham Law', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Water Resources Engineering', 'Law', 'Atomic', 'The Darcy-Buckingham law relates water flux in variably saturated porous media to the gradient of total hydraulic potential through an unsaturated hydraulic conductivity that depends on soil water state.', 'Applies primarily to water flow in unsaturated or variably saturated porous media within the continuum and constitutive assumptions of the Darcy-Buckingham formulation; hydraulic conductivity may depend on pressure head or water content, and preferential or strongly non-Darcian flow requires additional treatment.', 'Water flux; total hydraulic potential gradient; unsaturated hydraulic conductivity; porous-media flow', 'The Darcy-Buckingham law extends Darcy-type flux-gradient reasoning to unsaturated soil water flow by allowing hydraulic conductivity to vary with the soil water state. It forms a constitutive component of variably saturated flow models such as the Richards equation.', 'Darcy-Buckingham law; unsaturated Darcy law; unsaturated water flux; hydraulic potential gradient', 'Active'), ('KPR_000103', 'Unit Hydrograph Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Water Resources Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a linear time-invariant catchment model, direct runoff response to a specified unit effective rainfall pulse can be superposed and scaled to represent other effective rainfall inputs.', 'Applies under the assumptions of linearity and time invariance used by unit-hydrograph theory.', 'effective rainfall; direct runoff; convolution; linearity', 'For a linear time-invariant catchment model, direct runoff response to a specified unit effective rainfall pulse can be superposed and scaled to represent other effective rainfall inputs. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies under the assumptions of linearity and time invariance used by unit-hydrograph theory. Key ideas include effective rainfall; direct runoff; convolution; linearity.', 'unit hydrograph principle; effective rainfall; direct runoff; convolution; linearity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000104', 'Uniformitarianism Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Engineering Geology', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Geological processes operating today provide a basis for interpreting past geological features, subject to changes in rates, environments and boundary conditions.', 'Applies to geological interpretation without assuming that past rates and conditions were identical to present ones.', 'geological processes; present; past; interpretation', 'Geological processes operating today provide a basis for interpreting past geological features, subject to changes in rates, environments and boundary conditions. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to geological interpretation without assuming that past rates and conditions were identical to present ones. Key ideas include geological processes; present; past; interpretation.', 'uniformitarianism; actualism', 'Active'), ('KPR_000105', 'Superposition Principle in Stratigraphy', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Engineering Geology', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'In an undisturbed sequence of sedimentary strata, older layers generally underlie younger layers.', 'Applies to sedimentary sequences that have not been overturned or otherwise structurally disrupted.', 'stratigraphic order; relative age; sedimentary layers', 'In an undisturbed sequence of sedimentary strata, older layers generally underlie younger layers. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to sedimentary sequences that have not been overturned or otherwise structurally disrupted. Key ideas include stratigraphic order; relative age; sedimentary layers.', 'law of superposition; stratigraphy', 'Active'), ('KPR_000106', 'Design-for-Manufacture Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'CAD/CAM', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Product geometry and specifications should be developed with manufacturing processes, tolerances, material behaviour and production constraints considered from the outset.', 'Applies to design decisions intended for manufacturability; exact rules depend on process and production context.', 'manufacturability; tolerances; process constraints; design', 'Product geometry and specifications should be developed with manufacturing processes, tolerances, material behaviour and production constraints considered from the outset. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to design decisions intended for manufacturability; exact rules depend on process and production context. Key ideas include manufacturability; tolerances; process constraints; design.', 'DFM; design for manufacture', 'Active'), ('KPR_000109', 'Hall-Petch Relationship', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Engineering Materials', 'Constitutive Principle', 'Atomic', 'For many polycrystalline materials over applicable grain-size ranges, yield strength increases as grain size decreases according to an empirical inverse-square-root relation.', 'Applies over material and grain-size regimes where the empirical relationship is valid.', 'grain size; yield strength; grain boundaries', 'Provides an empirical structure-property relationship, not a universal law for all materials.', 'Hall Petch relation; grain size; yield strength; grain boundaries', 'Active'), ('KPR_000111', 'Newtonian Fluid Constitutive Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Fluid Flow and Heat Transfer', 'Constitutive Principle', 'Atomic', 'For a Newtonian fluid, shear stress is proportional to shear rate through dynamic viscosity under the applicable conditions.', 'Applies to fluids behaving approximately Newtonian over the relevant shear-rate and temperature range.', 'shear stress; shear rate; viscosity', 'For a Newtonian fluid, shear stress is proportional to shear rate through dynamic viscosity under the applicable conditions. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to fluids behaving approximately Newtonian over the relevant shear-rate and temperature range. Key ideas include shear stress; shear rate; viscosity.', 'Newtonian constitutive law; shear stress; shear rate; viscosity', 'Active'), ('KPR_000115', 'Closed-Loop Stability Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Process Control and Instrumentation', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Closed-loop behaviour is governed by the poles or equivalent stability characteristics of the feedback system, so controller design must account for the plant, feedback path and controller together.', 'Applies to defined mathematical models and stability criteria; nonlinear and time-varying systems require appropriate analysis.', 'closed loop; poles; stability; feedback', 'Closed-loop behaviour is governed by the poles or equivalent stability characteristics of the feedback system, so controller design must account for the plant, feedback path and controller together. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to defined mathematical models and stability criteria; nonlinear and time-varying systems require appropriate analysis. Key ideas include closed loop; poles; stability; feedback.', 'closed-loop stability; closed loop; poles; stability; feedback', 'Active'), ('KPR_000116', 'Internal Model Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Process Control and Instrumentation', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Robust asymptotic rejection of a class of disturbances or tracking of reference signals requires the controller to contain an appropriate internal model of those signal dynamics.', 'Applies to specified signal classes and closed-loop regulation/tracking objectives.', 'internal model; disturbance rejection; tracking; robustness', 'Links controller structure to the dynamics of signals that must be tracked or rejected.', 'internal model principle; internal model; disturbance rejection; tracking; robustness', 'Active'), ('KPR_000117', 'Electromechanical Energy Conversion Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Electromechanical devices exchange energy between electrical and mechanical domains through electromagnetic fields, with conversion governed by energy and co-energy relationships.', 'Applies to defined magnetic systems and appropriate energy/co-energy formulations.', 'magnetic field; electrical energy; mechanical energy; co-energy', 'Provides the energy basis for torque and force production in electrical machines.', 'electromechanical energy conversion; magnetic field; electrical energy; mechanical energy; co-energy', 'Active'), ('KPR_000119', 'Symmetrical Components Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Power Systems', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'An unbalanced three-phase set can be represented as the sum of positive-, negative- and zero-sequence balanced sets.', 'Applies to three-phase systems and phasor-domain analysis under the assumptions of symmetrical-component theory.', 'positive sequence; negative sequence; zero sequence; unbalance', 'An unbalanced three-phase set can be represented as the sum of positive-, negative- and zero-sequence balanced sets. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to three-phase systems and phasor-domain analysis under the assumptions of symmetrical-component theory. Key ideas include positive sequence; negative sequence; zero sequence; unbalance.', 'symmetrical components; positive sequence; negative sequence; zero sequence; unbalance', 'Active'), ('KPR_000120', 'Power-Angle Stability Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Power Systems', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The ability of synchronous machines to maintain synchronism depends on the relationship between electrical power transfer and rotor angle under the applicable network and machine model.', 'Applies to specified synchronous-machine and network models; stability assessment requires dynamic conditions and disturbance definition.', 'rotor angle; synchronism; transfer power; stability', 'The ability of synchronous machines to maintain synchronism depends on the relationship between electrical power transfer and rotor angle under the applicable network and machine model. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to specified synchronous-machine and network models; stability assessment requires dynamic conditions and disturbance definition. Key ideas include rotor angle; synchronism; transfer power; stability.', 'power angle relationship; rotor angle; synchronism; transfer power; stability', 'Active'), ('KPR_000121', 'Volt-Second Balance Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Power Electronics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'In steady periodic operation of an inductor, the average inductor voltage over one switching period is zero.', 'Applies to periodic steady-state converter operation where inductor flux does not drift.', 'inductor voltage; switching period; steady state; duty ratio', 'In steady periodic operation of an inductor, the average inductor voltage over one switching period is zero. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to periodic steady-state converter operation where inductor flux does not drift. Key ideas include inductor voltage; switching period; steady state; duty ratio.', 'volt-second balance; inductor voltage; switching period; steady state; duty ratio', 'Active'), ('KPR_000122', 'Charge-Balance Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Power Electronics', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'In steady periodic operation of a capacitor, the average capacitor current over one switching period is zero.', 'Applies to periodic steady-state converter operation without net capacitor charge drift.', 'capacitor current; switching period; steady state', 'In steady periodic operation of a capacitor, the average capacitor current over one switching period is zero. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to periodic steady-state converter operation without net capacitor charge drift. Key ideas include capacitor current; switching period; steady state.', 'charge balance; capacitor current; switching period; steady state', 'Active'), ('KPR_000123', 'Measurement Traceability Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'A measurement result is traceable when it can be related to a stated reference through an unbroken documented chain of calibrations, each contributing to measurement uncertainty.', 'Applies to measurement results with defined references, calibration chains and uncertainty contributions; traceability is not itself a statement of accuracy.', 'traceability; calibration chain; reference; uncertainty', 'A measurement result is traceable when it can be related to a stated reference through an unbroken documented chain of calibrations, each contributing to measurement uncertainty. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to measurement results with defined references, calibration chains and uncertainty contributions; traceability is not itself a statement of accuracy. Key ideas include traceability; calibration chain; reference; uncertainty.', 'metrological traceability; traceability; calibration chain; reference; uncertainty', 'Active'), ('KPR_000124', 'Uncertainty Propagation Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Uncertainty in a result derived from input quantities depends on the uncertainties and sensitivity coefficients of those inputs under the adopted measurement model.', 'Applies to defined measurement models and stated uncertainty evaluation methods.', 'uncertainty; sensitivity coefficient; measurement model; covariance', 'Uncertainty in a result derived from input quantities depends on the uncertainties and sensitivity coefficients of those inputs under the adopted measurement model. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to defined measurement models and stated uncertainty evaluation methods. Key ideas include uncertainty; sensitivity coefficient; measurement model; covariance.', 'uncertainty propagation; uncertainty; sensitivity coefficient; measurement model; covariance', 'Active'), ('KPR_000125', 'Convolution Principle for LTI Systems', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Signals and Systems', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'The output of a linear time-invariant system is the convolution of its input with the system''s impulse response.', 'Applies to LTI systems with appropriate mathematical conditions.', 'convolution; impulse response; LTI; system output', 'The output of a linear time-invariant system is the convolution of its input with the system''s impulse response. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to LTI systems with appropriate mathematical conditions. Key ideas include convolution; impulse response; LTI; system output.', 'convolution property; convolution; impulse response; LTI; system output', 'Active'), ('KPR_000126', 'Fourier Decomposition Principle', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Signals and Systems', 'Principle', 'Atomic', 'Signals satisfying the relevant conditions can be represented in terms of sinusoidal or complex-exponential components whose amplitudes and phases describe spectral content.', 'Applies within the convergence and transform conditions of Fourier analysis.', 'frequency domain; spectrum; sinusoidal components; transform', 'Signals satisfying the relevant conditions can be represented in terms of sinusoidal or complex-exponential components whose amplitudes and phases describe spectral content. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies within the convergence and transform conditions of Fourier analysis. Key ideas include frequency domain; spectrum; sinusoidal components; transform.', 'Fourier principle; spectral decomposition', 'Active'), ('KPR_000127', 'Final-Value Theorem', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Signals and Systems', 'Theorem', 'Atomic', 'Under the stability and pole conditions required by the theorem, the final value of a time-domain signal can be obtained from the low-frequency behaviour of its Laplace transform.', 'Applies only when the required pole conditions hold; it cannot be used indiscriminately for unstable systems.', 'Laplace transform; final value; poles; stability', 'Under the stability and pole conditions required by the theorem, the final value of a time-domain signal can be obtained from the low-frequency behaviour of its Laplace transform. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies only when the required pole conditions hold; it cannot be used indiscriminately for unstable systems. Key ideas include Laplace transform; final value; poles; stability.', 'final value theorem; Laplace transform; final value; poles; stability', 'Active'), ('KPR_000128', 'Mitigation Hierarchy Principle', 'Law', 'Environmental Impact Assessment Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Environmental decision-making should prioritize avoiding impacts, then minimizing them, restoring affected systems, and offsetting residual impacts where legally and scientifically appropriate.', 'Applies to impact-management decisions and should not be treated as permission to offset avoidable impacts.', 'avoid; minimize; restore; offset; residual impact', 'Environmental decision-making should prioritize avoiding impacts, then minimizing them, restoring affected systems, and offsetting residual impacts where legally and scientifically appropriate. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to impact-management decisions and should not be treated as permission to offset avoidable impacts. Key ideas include avoid; minimize; restore; offset; residual impact.', 'mitigation hierarchy; avoid; minimize; restore; offset; residual impact', 'Active'), ('KPR_000129', 'Freedom of Contract Principle', 'Law', 'Contract Law and Commercial Agreements', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Parties generally have autonomy to enter agreements and determine their terms within mandatory legal limits.', 'Applies subject to capacity, legality, public policy, consumer protection and other mandatory rules.', 'party autonomy; consent; contractual terms; mandatory law', 'Parties generally have autonomy to enter agreements and determine their terms within mandatory legal limits. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies subject to capacity, legality, public policy, consumer protection and other mandatory rules. Key ideas include party autonomy; consent; contractual terms; mandatory law.', 'freedom of contract; party autonomy', 'Active'), ('KPR_000130', 'Collective Bargaining Principle', 'Law', 'Labour and Employment Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Workers and employers should be able to negotiate employment conditions collectively through recognized representative processes subject to applicable law.', 'Applies to collective labour relations and depends on statutory recognition and bargaining frameworks.', 'collective bargaining; representation; employment conditions', 'Workers and employers should be able to negotiate employment conditions collectively through recognized representative processes subject to applicable law. Its valid use is bounded by: Applies to collective labour relations and depends on statutory recognition and bargaining frameworks. Key ideas include collective bargaining; representation; employment conditions.', 'collective bargaining principle; collective bargaining; representation; employment conditions', 'Active'), ('KPR_000131', 'Equal Remuneration Principle', 'Law', 'Labour and Employment Law', 'Normative Principle', 'Atomic', 'Workers should receive equal re