e''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to architecture and software design; the appropriate separation depends on cohesion, coupling, interfaces, and system requirements.', 'Use Separation of Concerns Principle to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Design patterns.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to Design patterns.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Design patterns.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Separation of Concerns Principle, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Separation of Concerns Principle for Design patterns. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Separation of Concerns Principle; Design patterns', 'Active'), ('KFR_000122', 'Apply Pascal''s Principle to hydrostatics', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Pascal''s Principle to hydrostatics under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to confined fluids under the assumptions of Pascal''s law; hydraulic-machine applications additionally require accounting for geometry, losses, and mechanical efficiency.', 'Use Pascal''s Principle to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning hydrostatics.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to hydrostatics.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning hydrostatics.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Pascal''s Principle, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Pascal''s Principle for hydrostatics. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Pascal''s Principle; hydrostatics', 'Active'), ('KFR_000123', 'Apply Principle of Virtual Work to Virtual work', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Principle of Virtual Work to Virtual work under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to equilibrium and compatible virtual displacements under the adopted idealization; the virtual displacement is a mathematical variation, not necessarily an actual motion.', 'Use Principle of Virtual Work to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Virtual work.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to Virtual work.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Virtual work.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Principle of Virtual Work, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Principle of Virtual Work for Virtual work. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Principle of Virtual Work; Virtual work', 'Active'), ('KFR_000124', 'Apply Principle of Minimum Potential Energy to Beam deflection methods; Virtual work', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Principle of Minimum Potential Energy to Beam deflection methods, Virtual work under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to conservative systems and stable equilibrium configurations within the adopted elastic model; unstable equilibria are not minima.', 'Use Principle of Minimum Potential Energy to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Beam deflection methods, Virtual work.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to Beam deflection methods, Virtual work.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Beam deflection methods, Virtual work.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Principle of Minimum Potential Energy, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Principle of Minimum Potential Energy for Beam deflection methods, Virtual work. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Principle of Minimum Potential Energy; Beam deflection methods; Virtual work', 'Active'), ('KFR_000125', 'Apply Principle of Virtual Displacements to Virtual work', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Civil Engineering; Mechanical Engineering', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Principle of Virtual Displacements to Virtual work under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to static equilibrium under compatible virtual displacements and the adopted idealization.', 'Use Principle of Virtual Displacements to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Virtual work.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to Virtual work.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning Virtual work.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Principle of Virtual Displacements, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Principle of Virtual Displacements for Virtual work. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Principle of Virtual Displacements; Virtual work', 'Active'), ('KFR_000126', 'Apply Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle to UNFCCC; Paris Agreement', 'Law', 'Climate Change Law', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle to United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Paris Agreement under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies within the specific international environmental or climate regime in which the principle is invoked; its legal effect depends on the treaty and negotiating context.', 'Use Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Paris Agreement.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Paris Agreement.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Paris Agreement.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle for United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Paris Agreement. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Common but Differentiated Responsibilities Principle; UNFCCC; Paris Agreement; United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC)', 'Active'), ('KFR_000127', 'Apply Pacta Sunt Servanda to performance; discharge by agreement', 'Law', 'Contract Law', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Pacta Sunt Servanda to performance, discharge by agreement under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to binding agreements within the governing contract-law regime; mandatory law, impossibility, illegality, and recognized doctrines can qualify performance obligations.', 'Use Pacta Sunt Servanda to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning performance, discharge by agreement.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to performance, discharge by agreement.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning performance, discharge by agreement.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Pacta Sunt Servanda, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Pacta Sunt Servanda for performance, discharge by agreement. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Pacta Sunt Servanda; performance; discharge by agreement', 'Active'), ('KFR_000128', 'Apply Contra Proferentem Principle to interpretation; exclusion clauses', 'Law', 'Contract Law', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Contra Proferentem Principle to interpretation, exclusion clauses under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies only where the governing legal system recognizes the doctrine and where ambiguity remains after applicable interpretive principles have been considered.', 'Use Contra Proferentem Principle to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning interpretation, exclusion clauses.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to interpretation, exclusion clauses.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning interpretation, exclusion clauses.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Contra Proferentem Principle, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Contra Proferentem Principle for interpretation, exclusion clauses. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Contra Proferentem Principle; interpretation; exclusion clauses', 'Active'), ('KFR_000129', 'Apply Separate Legal Personality Doctrine to company organs; shareholder rights', 'Law', 'Corporate Law', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Separate Legal Personality Doctrine to company organs, shareholder rights under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to incorporated entities subject to statutory exceptions, including circumstances in which courts or legislation permit attribution, lifting of the corporate veil, or direct liability.', 'Use Separate Legal Personality Doctrine to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning company organs, shareholder rights.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to company organs, shareholder rights.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning company organs, shareholder rights.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Separate Legal Personality Doctrine, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Separate Legal Personality Doctrine for company organs, shareholder rights. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Separate Legal Personality Doctrine; company organs; shareholder rights', 'Active'), ('KFR_000130', 'Apply Principle of Beneficence to informed consent; research ethics review', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Principle of Beneficence to informed consent, research ethics review under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to ethical research planning and requires context-specific assessment of risks, benefits, alternatives, and mitigation measures.', 'Use Principle of Beneficence to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning informed consent, research ethics review.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to informed consent, research ethics review.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning informed consent, research ethics review.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Principle of Beneficence, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Principle of Beneficence for informed consent, research ethics review. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Principle of Beneficence; informed consent; research ethics review', 'Active'), ('KFR_000131', 'Apply Principle of Justice in Research to informed consent; research ethics review', 'Social Science', 'Social Science; Research Methods', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Principle of Justice in Research to informed consent, research ethics review under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to participant selection, access, compensation, and distribution of research burdens and benefits under the relevant ethical framework.', 'Use Principle of Justice in Research to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning informed consent, research ethics review.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to informed consent, research ethics review.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning informed consent, research ethics review.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Principle of Justice in Research, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Principle of Justice in Research for informed consent, research ethics review. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Principle of Justice in Research; informed consent; research ethics review', 'Active'), ('KFR_000132', 'Apply Rule of Mixtures to fibres; matrices; laminates', 'Engineering & Technology', 'Engineering Materials', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Rule of Mixtures to fibres, matrices, laminates under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to properties and loading directions for which the assumed mixture model is appropriate.', 'Use Rule of Mixtures to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning fibres, matrices, laminates.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to fibres, matrices, laminates.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning fibres, matrices, laminates.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Rule of Mixtures, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Rule of Mixtures for fibres, matrices, laminates. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Rule of Mixtures; fibres; matrices; laminates', 'Active'), ('KFR_000133', 'Apply Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction to attraction; relationships; prosocial behaviour', 'Social Science', 'Social Psychology', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction to attraction, relationships, prosocial behaviour under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to social-exchange settings and cultural contexts; it is not a universal moral rule.', 'Use Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning attraction, relationships, prosocial behaviour.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to attraction, relationships, prosocial behaviour.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning attraction, relationships, prosocial behaviour.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction for attraction, relationships, prosocial behaviour. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Reciprocity Principle in Social Interaction; attraction; relationships; prosocial behaviour', 'Active'), ('KFR_000134', 'Apply Replication Principle to reproducibility; Measurement reproducibility', 'Social Science', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Replication Principle to reproducibility, Measurement reproducibility under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to findings for which the design, data, procedures or conceptual target permit meaningful replication; replication need not produce identical numerical results.', 'Use Replication Principle to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning reproducibility, Measurement reproducibility.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to reproducibility, Measurement reproducibility.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning reproducibility, Measurement reproducibility.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Replication Principle, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Replication Principle for reproducibility, Measurement reproducibility. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Replication Principle; reproducibility; Measurement reproducibility', 'Active'), ('KFR_000135', 'Apply Central Limit Theorem to descriptive statistics; hypothesis testing', 'Social Science', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Central Limit Theorem to descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies under stated assumptions and does not imply that every raw dataset is normally distributed.', 'Use Central Limit Theorem to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Central Limit Theorem, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Central Limit Theorem for descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Central Limit Theorem; descriptive statistics; hypothesis testing', 'Active'), ('KFR_000136', 'Apply Law of Large Numbers to probability sampling; descriptive statistics', 'Social Science', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Analysis / Application', 'Atomic', 'Operational capability to apply Law of Large Numbers to probability sampling, descriptive statistics under the governing principle''s stated assumptions and validity conditions.', 'Applies to specified stochastic assumptions and does not guarantee that any finite sample is close to the expectation.', 'Use Law of Large Numbers to obtain a technically justified analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning probability sampling, descriptive statistics.', 'Problem data, variables, relationships, assumptions, and boundary conditions relevant to probability sampling, descriptive statistics.', 'A principle-grounded analysis, calculation, classification, or decision concerning probability sampling, descriptive statistics.', 'Verify the applicability conditions of Law of Large Numbers, map the concept-specific variables and relationships to the principle, and derive only the conclusions permitted by that principle.', 'Operationalizes Law of Large Numbers for probability sampling, descriptive statistics. The function connects concept-specific evidence to a principle-grounded result within the stated scope and should not be generalized beyond the principle''s assumptions.', 'Law of Large Numbers; probability sampling; descriptive statistics', 'Active') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO repo_scrs (scr_id, name, sustainability_dimension, domain, definition, scope, core_ideas, semantic_description, keywords_synonyms, sustainability_principle, principle_origin, underlying_mechanism, derived_from_practice_or_system, evidence_status, implementation_maturity, transferability, boundary_conditions, recommendation_trigger, status) VALUES ('SCR_0001', 'Functional Diversity and Adaptive Capacity', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Ecology; Resilience; Systems', 'Functional diversity and adaptive capacity describe the sustainability value of maintaining sufficiently varied components, roles, capabilities or response strategies so that a system can continue functioning, adapt, or recover when conditions change. Diversity matters here because different components may respond differently to the same disturbance, provide complementary functions, or provide alternatives when one pathway fails.', 'Applies when variation among components has a demonstrable or plausible functional relationship to resilience, adaptation, recovery, or response diversity. The relevant unit of diversity may be biological species, functional traits, technologies, infrastructure pathways, organizations, knowledge sources, or other system components. Diversity is not inherently sustainable: additional components can also increase resource demand, coordination burden, incompatibility, or risk.', 'Maintain useful variation; preserve functional redundancy where failure is consequential; combine complementary capabilities; avoid unnecessary monocultures or single-path dependencies; assess the cost of diversity against its resilience benefit; distinguish diversity itself from the functions diversity enables.', 'The principle connects biodiversity and other forms of system diversity to the ability to absorb disturbance, adapt and recover.', 'biodiversity; diversity; redundancy; resilience; adaptive capacity', 'A system should preserve diversity where diversity provides the variation, redundancy or complementary functions needed to remain resilient under changing conditions.', 'Nature-Derived; Ecological-Relationship-Derived', 'Diverse components can respond differently to disturbance, reducing the chance that one failure disables the whole system.', 'Biodiversity and resilient ecological systems; diverse agricultural and social systems.', 'UN SDG 15; IPCC AR6 sustainability and resilience literature.', 'Established', 'Agriculture; ecology; infrastructure; organizations; data/AI; communities', 'Diversity should not be treated as inherently beneficial if it creates unmanaged hazards or unnecessary complexity.', 'Candidate discovery when the graph contains diversity, redundancy, modularity, biodiversity or resilience concepts; strengthen only when the application context shows functional diversity matters.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0002', 'Resource Regeneration Capacity', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Ecology; Natural Resources; Water; Agriculture', 'Resource regeneration capacity is the ability of a resource stock, ecological process, or supporting system to replenish or recover at a rate sufficient to sustain an intended pattern of use over the relevant time horizon. Sustainability therefore depends not simply on whether a resource is currently available, but on the relationship between withdrawals or disturbance and the processes and conditions that restore the resource.', 'Applies primarily to renewable or regenerating resources such as groundwater, surface water, soil fertility, biomass, fisheries, forests, and ecosystem functions. Regeneration may be spatially and seasonally variable and may be reduced by pollution, land-use change, climate change, or excessive extraction. Non-renewable resources require depletion, substitution, recycling, and transition analysis rather than a regeneration-rate test.', 'Match use to regeneration; protect the processes that enable renewal; distinguish stock from flow; account for spatial and temporal variability; avoid treating short-term availability as evidence of sustainable supply; monitor regeneration and recovery conditions.', 'The principle connects resource demand with the rate and conditions of renewal rather than short-term availability.', 'regeneration; sustainable withdrawal; replenishment; resource use; carrying capacity', 'A system that depends on renewal should not use a resource faster than the processes that replenish the resource or the conditions that sustain its renewal.', 'Nature-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'The sustainable rate of use is constrained by regeneration and supporting ecological processes.', 'Traditional water allocation; fisheries and forestry management; sustainable groundwater withdrawal.', 'UN SDG Goals 6, 12 and 14; FAO sustainability principles; IPCC AR6.', 'Established', 'Water; fisheries; forestry; agriculture; energy; materials', 'Renewal rates vary spatially and temporally; technological substitution does not remove ecological limits.', 'Candidate discovery when resource stocks, flows, recharge, regeneration, extraction or renewal constraints are present; strengthen when the application context contains a measurable renewal rate.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0003', 'Ecological Function Integration', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Ecology; Aquaculture; Ecological Engineering', 'Ecological function integration recognizes that ecological processes can provide functions that would otherwise require engineered, material, or energy-intensive interventions, while simultaneously supporting ecosystem health. The sustainability opportunity arises when an ecological process is deliberately retained, restored, or incorporated into system design rather than treated only as background environmental context.', 'Applies only where the ecological process actually performs a relevant function and where its performance, reliability, safety, maintenance requirements, and ecological consequences can be evaluated. Ecological processes should not be treated as interchangeable with engineered systems merely because they provide a superficially similar service.', 'Use ecological processes as functional system components where appropriate; identify the mechanism of service provision; preserve ecological integrity; compare ecological and engineered alternatives on equivalent functional outcomes; account for variability, maintenance, failure, and co-benefits.', 'The principle abstracts from cases in which a human system is redesigned to work with an existing ecological interaction.', 'ecological function; biological control; ecological engineering; nature-based process', 'When natural ecological interactions can safely perform a useful function, human systems should support those interactions rather than unnecessarily replacing them with resource-intensive technical processes.', 'Nature-Derived; Ecological-Relationship-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'An organism or ecological relationship performs a function that would otherwise require an external technical input.', 'Pearl farming using reef fish to control biofouling on culture equipment.', 'Sustainable Pearls; UN SDG partnership material; marine ecosystem management literature.', 'Demonstrated in specific contexts', 'Aquaculture; agriculture; wastewater; landscape management; ecological engineering', 'Do not assume every natural process is beneficial, controllable or appropriate for production.', 'Candidate discovery when an ecological interaction or ecosystem function could plausibly provide a required service; validate against ecological evidence and safety/boundary conditions before recommending.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0004', 'Local Ecological Fit', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Water; Architecture; Civil Engineering; Agriculture', 'Local ecological fit is the principle that sustainability performance depends partly on how well a system''s design, operation, materials, timing, and management are matched to the physical, ecological, climatic, hydrological, and social conditions of its place. A technically valid solution can perform poorly when assumptions developed elsewhere do not hold locally.', 'Applies when local conditions materially influence resource flows, hazards, ecosystem response, user behaviour, maintenance, or system performance. Local adaptation does not imply that locally developed solutions are automatically superior or that standardized technology should be rejected; both must be evaluated against the same functional and sustainability criteria.', 'Design for place; make local climate and hydrology explicit; account for local ecology and terrain; consider local materials and maintenance capacity; incorporate relevant local knowledge; test whether transferred assumptions remain valid under local boundary conditions.', 'The principle treats local environmental and social conditions as design inputs rather than external constraints. Distinguishing emphasis: the fit being tested is primarily ecological, hydrological, climatic, hazard, or other place-specific environmental performance.', 'place-based design; local ecological fit; climate-responsive design; local conditions', 'Human systems should work with local ecological conditions and processes rather than forcing the same design or operating model onto fundamentally different places.', 'Nature-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived; Traditional-Technology-Derived', 'Local conditions determine available resources, natural flows, hazards and appropriate operating regimes.', 'Traditional irrigation, vernacular architecture, climate-adapted settlements.', 'UNESCO traditional knowledge/irrigation documentation; FAO; UN SDG Goals 6 and 11.', 'Established in context', 'Civil engineering; architecture; agriculture; urban planning; water management', 'Local fit must be tested against contemporary population, safety, health and performance requirements.', 'Candidate discovery when site-specific climate, hydrology, ecology, hazards or local operating conditions materially affect design; context evidence should determine relevance.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0005', 'Seasonal and Cyclical Alignment', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Water; Agriculture; Fisheries; Energy', 'Seasonal and cyclical alignment is the practice of matching resource use, system operation, ecological intervention, or demand management to predictable temporal patterns in availability, regeneration, climate, ecological activity, or demand. Aligning timing can reduce peak stress and avoid unnecessary capacity or resource use.', 'Applies where meaningful seasonal, periodic, diurnal, tidal, biological, agricultural, climatic, or demand cycles exist and can be characterized sufficiently for decision-making. Historical cycles cannot be assumed to remain stationary under climate change or other system transitions; uncertainty and changing cycle characteristics must be considered.', 'Match demand to availability where feasible; exploit predictable cycles; avoid peak-period ecological or resource stress; design storage or flexibility for mismatch periods; monitor changes in cycle timing and amplitude; distinguish genuine cyclic structure from short-term variability.', 'The principle treats time as a sustainability variable rather than assuming constant resource availability.', 'seasonality; cycles; timing; ecological calendar; seasonal allocation', 'Resource systems should respond to seasonal and cyclical variation rather than treating availability, demand or ecological capacity as constant.', 'Historical-Practice-Derived; Nature-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'Seasonal variation changes resource availability, regeneration and risk.', 'Traditional irrigation calendars; seasonal fisheries closures; rain-fed agriculture.', 'FAO; UNESCO traditional knowledge; UN SDG Goals 2, 6 and 14.', 'Established in context', 'Agriculture; water; fisheries; energy; tourism; urban systems', 'Historical cycles cannot be assumed unchanged under climate change or altered land use.', 'Candidate discovery when the system has seasonal, periodic or cyclical variation in resource availability, demand, regeneration or risk.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0006', 'Shared Resource Governance', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Commons; Water; Fisheries; Public Resources', 'Shared resource responsible management holds that resources used by multiple people, organizations, or jurisdictions remain sustainable only when access, use, monitoring, maintenance, and responsibility are connected through appropriate governance arrangements. responsible management is therefore a relationship between resource condition and the institutions or communities that depend on it.', 'Applies to shared or common-pool resources and public or collectively managed systems where individual actions can affect resource condition or other users. Governance arrangements must reflect the resource characteristics, user community, legal authority, scale, enforcement capacity, and distribution of rights and responsibilities; no single governance form is universally applicable.', 'Connect rights with responsibilities; define legitimate users and duties; establish monitoring and feedback; address collective-action problems; maintain accountability; adapt rules when resource conditions change; recognize the role of local knowledge and participation.', 'The principle connects resource viability with institutions and user participation. Distinguishing emphasis: sustainability depends on governance arrangements linking shared users to rules, monitoring, maintenance, and collective responsibility.', 'commons; responsible management; collective action; shared resources; community governance', 'A shared resource is more likely to remain viable when those who depend on it also have responsibilities and meaningful roles in maintaining the conditions that sustain it.', 'Commons-Governance-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived; Community-Practice-Derived', 'Users have information, incentives and responsibilities that can support monitoring and maintenance.', 'Community irrigation, fisheries and common-resource governance.', 'UN SDG Goals 6 and 14; scholarly commons-governance evidence.', 'Established in contexts', 'Water; fisheries; forests; campus commons; digital commons', 'Community participation is not automatically effective; rules, power, accountability and ecological conditions matter.', 'Candidate discovery when a shared resource has identifiable users, responsibilities, monitoring or maintenance needs; application context may introduce participation/governance recommendations.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0007', 'Human–Ecological Coupling', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Socio-Ecological Systems; Water; Climate; Planning', 'Human–ecological coupling recognizes that human systems and ecological systems can exchange material, energy, information, risks, and benefits, creating feedbacks in which changes in one affect the condition and functioning of the other. Sustainability analysis therefore considers these interactions rather than treating society and ecology as independent externalities.', 'Applies where human activity materially alters ecological processes or where ecological conditions materially influence health, livelihoods, infrastructure, resource availability, safety, or other human outcomes. The relevant system boundary must capture the feedback pathway and appropriate spatial and temporal scales.', 'Make human–ecological feedbacks explicit; identify dependencies and impacts in both directions; include ecological conditions in system performance; identify trade-offs and synergies; avoid one-way impact assumptions; analyse coupled-system dynamics.', 'The principle prevents ecological effects from being treated as external to social or engineering systems.', 'socio-ecological systems; feedback; coupled systems; environmental governance', 'When people, communities and ecosystems depend on the same resource system, decisions should account for reciprocal effects rather than optimizing one component in isolation.', 'Socio-Ecological-Derived; Systems-Thinking-Derived', 'Feedback connects resource use, ecosystem condition and human outcomes.', 'Integrated watershed and ecosystem management.', 'IPCC AR6; UN SDG Goals 6, 11 and 15.', 'Established as a systems approach', 'Water; urban planning; agriculture; climate adaptation; public policy', 'The coupling structure must be evidenced rather than assumed.', 'Candidate discovery when human and ecological components are coupled through resource flows, feedbacks or impacts; validate the specific cross-boundary mechanism.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0008', 'Reciprocal Resource Responsibility', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Commons; Governance; Public Policy', 'Reciprocal responsible management links the benefits, access, rights, or services obtained from a shared system with corresponding responsibilities to maintain the conditions that sustain those benefits. Reciprocity does not require equal contributions; it requires that legitimate use and responsibility are connected in a way that is appropriate to roles, capacity, rights, and institutional context.', 'Applies where users receive continuing benefits from a resource, infrastructure system, ecosystem service, community arrangement, or other shared asset and where their actions can affect its continued functioning. Responsibilities may be differentiated by capacity, contribution, legal status, historical role, or degree of impact.', 'Link benefit with responsibility; recognize differentiated contributions; maintain accountability for resource condition; support mutual obligation; distinguish reciprocity from simple participation; ensure responsibilities are legitimate and enforceable in context.', 'The principle connects distribution of benefits with maintenance obligations. Distinguishing emphasis: benefits or access are explicitly linked to proportionate responsibilities for sustaining the shared system; broad governance participation alone is insufficient.', 'reciprocity; responsible management obligations; resource rights; accountability', 'Those who receive benefits from a shared resource should also contribute, in proportion to legitimate responsibilities, to maintaining the resource and the system that sustains it.', 'Reciprocity-Derived; Commons-Governance-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived', 'Reciprocal arrangements can align incentives for maintenance and long-term resource viability.', 'Community water allocation and maintenance arrangements.', 'UN SDG Goals 6 and 16; commons-governance scholarship.', 'Established in contexts', 'Water; community infrastructure; public resources; digital commons', 'Responsibilities must be legitimate, transparent and proportionate.', 'Candidate discovery when benefits and responsibilities are jointly distributed among users of a shared resource; distinguish reciprocity from participation and from generic governance.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0009', 'Intergenerational Options', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Intergenerational Sustainability; Development', 'Intergenerational options expresses the sustainability requirement that present decisions should not unnecessarily reduce the ability of future people to meet essential needs, respond to uncertainty, or choose among viable development pathways. The emphasis is on preserving future capability and options, particularly where present actions create long-lived commitments or irreversible losses.', 'Applies to decisions involving resource depletion, persistent pollution, long-lived infrastructure, cumulative environmental change, technological lock-in, loss of knowledge or ecological function, and other effects whose consequences extend across generations. It does not require preserving every existing condition; justified change is compatible with the principle when future capability is not unnecessarily constrained.', 'Preserve future choice; identify irreversible and difficult-to-reverse effects; account for long-lived commitments; maintain ecological and institutional capacity; consider cumulative effects; avoid transferring avoidable risks or depleted options to future generations.', 'Intergenerational sustainability is about preserving future capability, not predicting exactly what future people will want.', 'intergenerational equity; future generations; long-term options; responsible management', 'Current decisions should preserve the ecological, social and knowledge conditions that allow future generations to meet needs and exercise meaningful choices.', 'Intergenerational-Culture-Derived; Policy-and-Normative-Derived', 'Long-lived decisions can constrain future choices through depletion, lock-in or irreversible damage.', 'Intergenerational equity in sustainable development.', 'UN 2030 Agenda; UN SDG 12; UN SDG 13; KPR Intergenerational Equity Principle.', 'Established', 'Infrastructure; energy; water; land use; policy; education', 'Future preferences are uncertain; the principle focuses on preserving capabilities and options.', 'Candidate discovery when a decision has long-lived effects, depletion, lock-in or irreversible consequences that can constrain future choices.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0010', 'Loss Prevention Before Supply Expansion', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Resource Management; Food; Water; Energy', 'Loss prevention before supply expansion prioritizes reducing avoidable losses, leakage, waste, degradation, and unnecessary demand before increasing extraction, production, transport capacity, or external supply. The principle treats the existing system''s unproductive losses as a potential source of capacity that can be recovered before additional resource throughput is committed.', 'Applies where losses or avoidable demand are material, measurable or reasonably estimable and where interventions can reduce them without unacceptable rebound or lifecycle impacts. It is not a universal rule against supply expansion; additional capacity may still be necessary after technically and economically credible loss-prevention opportunities have been evaluated.', 'Measure losses before adding supply; distinguish technical losses from unavoidable losses; reduce leakage and waste; improve demand management; compare conservation with new supply on equivalent functional outcomes; check rebound and lifecycle effects.', 'The principle changes the first question from ''How do we supply more?'' to ''Why is what we already have insufficient?''. Distinguishing emphasis: the primary recoverable capacity comes from reducing physical or operational losses, leakage, waste, or degradation within the existing system.', 'loss prevention; demand management; efficiency; sufficiency; avoidable loss', 'Before increasing supply or extracting additional resources, examine whether existing resources are being lost, wasted, degraded or unnecessarily demanded.', 'Contemporary-Practice-Derived; Cross-Domain-Synthesis; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived', 'Preventing loss can reduce the need for new extraction or capacity.', 'Food-loss prevention; water leakage reduction; energy-loss reduction.', 'UN SDG 12.3 and 12.2; UN SDG 6.4; UN SDG 9.1.', 'Established cross-domain approach', 'Food; water; energy; infrastructure; manufacturing; data centers', 'Efficiency gains must not create rebound effects or shift burdens elsewhere.', 'Candidate discovery when loss, leakage, waste, avoidable demand or degradation can be quantified before additional supply or extraction is considered.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0011', 'Residual Resource Value', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Industrial Ecology; Waste; Circularity', 'Residual resource value recognizes that an output, by-product, effluent, heat stream, material residue, biological residue, or information product that has lost value in its original process may still contain usable function or recoverable value in another process. Sustainability improves when technically viable secondary uses are evaluated before residuals are treated as waste.', 'Applies where a residual has sufficient quality, quantity, timing, location, safety, and compatibility for a secondary use or recovery pathway. Secondary use is not automatically preferable: treatment, transport, contamination risk, additional energy demand, market dependence, and downstream impacts must be included in the assessment.', 'Look beyond the original process boundary; identify residual function; match residual quality to secondary requirements; prioritize safe recovery and cascading use; assess logistics and lifecycle impacts; distinguish genuine resource recovery from burden shifting.', 'The principle challenges process boundaries that define outputs as waste without evaluating residual function. Distinguishing emphasis: the concept identifies residual value that may remain in an output or waste stream even before a specific receiving process or exchange partner is established.', 'by-product; resource recovery; waste-to-resource; cascading use', 'A useful output from one process should be evaluated as a potential input to another process before it is classified as waste and discarded.', 'Cross-Domain-Synthesis; Contemporary-Practice-Derived; Engineering-Derived', 'A system boundary can hide useful exchanges between processes.', 'Industrial symbiosis; nutrient recovery; wastewater reuse; food by-product utilization.', 'UN SDG 12.4 and 12.5; UN SDG Indicator Framework.', 'Established approach', 'Chemical engineering; civil/environmental engineering; agriculture; manufacturing; campuses', 'Not every waste stream is a safe or viable resource.', 'Candidate discovery when outputs, by-products, residuals or waste streams may have technically defensible value as inputs to another process.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0012', 'Long-Life Use', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Design; Maintenance; Asset Management', 'Long-life use holds that retaining the useful function of a product, component, facility, infrastructure asset, or system for longer can preserve embodied materials, energy, labour, capital, and knowledge that would otherwise be required for replacement. Longevity is valuable when the retained system continues to deliver the required function safely and efficiently.', 'Applies when durability and continued use provide a net lifecycle benefit and do not create disproportionate operating energy, maintenance, safety, performance, or adaptation penalties. A longer physical life is not automatically more sustainable if the asset becomes functionally obsolete or imposes large ongoing burdens.', 'Preserve useful function; design and operate for durability; maintain assets; account for embodied value; compare extension with replacement using lifecycle assessment; distinguish physical longevity from functional relevance.', 'Sustainability can arise from preserving existing functional value rather than repeatedly creating replacement capacity.', 'durability; long-life use; maintenance; embodied value; lifecycle', 'Systems should retain useful materials, components and functions for as long as practical before discarding and replacing them.', 'Historical-Practice-Derived; Engineering-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'Maintenance and continued use preserve already invested material, energy, labour and knowledge.', 'Repair traditions; durable vernacular structures; maintainable infrastructure.', 'UN SDG 12; lifecycle and sustainable design literature.', 'Established approach', 'Buildings; infrastructure; equipment; products; software systems', 'Longer life is not automatically better if obsolete systems are unsafe or inefficient.', 'Candidate discovery when durability, maintenance, retained function or asset life is relevant; do not infer repairability unless the context supports it.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0013', 'Repairability and Life Extension', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Repair; Maintenance; Circular Economy', 'Repairability and life extension emphasizes restoring useful function through inspection, maintenance, repair, refurbishment, component replacement, or upgrading when this avoids unnecessary disposal and replacement. The sustainability value arises from retaining existing material, manufacturing effort, infrastructure, skills, and functional capability.', 'Applies when a system can be safely diagnosed and restored to an acceptable performance level and when repair does not create unacceptable reliability, safety, energy, environmental, or economic burdens. Repairability depends on access to parts, tools, information, skills, modularity, standards, and institutional support.', 'Design for diagnosis and repair; preserve replaceable components; maintain technical information and skills; evaluate repair versus replacement on lifecycle outcomes; include safety and reliability; avoid treating repair as beneficial when continued operation is materially inferior.', 'The principle makes repair an option to be evaluated before replacement rather than an automatic rule. Distinguishing emphasis: the primary mechanism is retaining an existing product or asset in useful service through repair, reuse, or life extension.', 'repair; maintainability; repairability; life extension', 'Where a product, structure or system can be safely restored to useful function, repair should be considered before replacement.', 'Historical-Practice-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'Repair restores function while retaining much of the original asset''s embodied value.', 'Traditional craft repair; equipment maintenance; infrastructure rehabilitation.', 'UN SDG 12; circular economy literature.', 'Established approach', 'Products; infrastructure; equipment; buildings; software', 'Repair can be inappropriate when failure modes, safety or lifecycle impacts make replacement preferable.', 'Candidate discovery when safe restoration of useful function is technically feasible and replacement would discard significant embodied value.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0014', 'Functional Sufficiency', 'LIFE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Engineering; Design; Sufficiency', 'Functional sufficiency distinguishes the service, capability, or outcome that a person or system actually requires from the quantity of material, energy, space, infrastructure, or throughput used to deliver it. Sustainability can improve when the required function is met with less unnecessary resource demand rather than maximizing output or capacity.', 'Applies when a meaningful functional requirement can be defined independently of the resource-intensive means used to provide it. Sufficiency does not mean simply reducing consumption regardless of need; essential service levels, safety, accessibility, reliability, and legitimate human requirements must remain satisfied.', 'Define the required function first; avoid unnecessary demand; separate service from throughput; size systems to actual need; evaluate efficiency and sufficiency together; protect essential access and performance.', 'The principle separates service provision from resource throughput.', 'functional requirement; sufficiency; resource demand; efficiency', 'A system should meet its intended human purpose without demanding materially more energy, water, land or other resources than the function requires.', 'Engineering-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'Resource demand should be evaluated against the actual service or capability required.', 'Efficient buildings, transport, water systems and manufacturing.', 'UN SDG 12.2; engineering sustainability literature.', 'Established', 'All engineering disciplines; architecture; services; digital systems', 'Efficiency alone can be insufficient if demand keeps expanding or the service definition is wrong.', 'Candidate discovery when the design objective is service/capability delivery and resource demand can be evaluated against the required function rather than output volume alone.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0015', 'Natural Gradient Utilization', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Water; Architecture; Energy; Engineering', 'Natural gradient utilization recognizes that naturally occurring gradients and flows—such as gravitational head, pressure differences, temperature differences, solar radiation, wind, or topographic elevation—can sometimes provide part of a required function without continuous external energy input. Sustainable design can exploit these conditions where they are reliable enough for the intended service.', 'Applies only where the natural gradient is physically available, sufficiently stable or manageable, and compatible with required performance, safety, control, reliability, and environmental conditions. Using a natural gradient does not eliminate embodied energy or other lifecycle impacts and should not be confused with universally passive operation.', 'Use available environmental gradients; minimize unnecessary pumping or active energy conversion; design around natural flows; account for variability and storage; protect ecological processes; verify that passive or low-energy operation still meets the required function.', 'The principle encourages designers to treat natural gradients and flows as design resources.', 'gravity; passive design; natural flow; gradient; passive environmental control', 'Where natural gradients, flows or environmental conditions can safely provide part of a required function, design should consider using them before adding active energy or material inputs.', 'Nature-Derived; Traditional-Technology-Derived; Engineering-Derived', 'A natural potential difference or environmental condition performs part of the required work.', 'Qanat/aflaj gravity conveyance; passive cooling and ventilation.', 'UNESCO traditional water systems; sustainable building literature.', 'Established design approach', 'Water; buildings; drainage; energy; transport', 'Natural systems are site-specific and may not meet reliability or capacity requirements alone.', 'Candidate discovery when natural head, pressure, temperature difference, gravity, airflow or another environmental gradient can safely perform part of the required function.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0016', 'Distributed Resilience', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Resilience; Infrastructure; Computing', 'Distributed resilience describes the capacity gained when critical functions are supported by multiple geographically, functionally, or organizationally distributed components or pathways rather than depending on a single point of failure. Distribution can allow local continuation of service when part of a system is disrupted.', 'Applies when failure of centralized components, networks, facilities, supply chains, or decision points could create unacceptable consequences and when distributed alternatives can maintain essential function. Distribution can also increase coordination, maintenance, cybersecurity, interoperability, and lifecycle burdens; these trade-offs must be evaluated.', 'Reduce critical single points of failure; distribute capacity where justified; retain local capability; design for graceful degradation; assess coordination costs; distinguish redundancy that improves resilience from duplication that merely increases resource use.', 'Resilience can be increased by distributing critical functions and decision points.', 'distributed resilience; redundancy; decentralization; fault tolerance', 'Critical functions should not depend unnecessarily on a single resource, component, pathway or decision point when distributed alternatives can provide greater resilience.', 'Systems-Thinking-Derived; Engineering-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived', 'Distributed systems can continue partial operation when one component fails.', 'Distributed water storage; decentralized energy; distributed computing.', 'Engineering resilience literature; UN SDG 9 and 11.', 'Established approach', 'Power; water; ICT; infrastructure; health; communities', 'Distribution is not automatically more resilient; interconnection and governance can create new vulnerabilities.', 'Candidate discovery when critical service continuity can benefit from distributed resources, pathways, components or decision points; test failure modes and trade-offs.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0017', 'Ecological Infrastructure', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY', 'Civil Engineering; Architecture; Urban Ecology', 'Ecological infrastructure treats ecological processes, habitats, vegetation, soils, wetlands, waterways, and other ecosystem structures as components that can contribute to human and environmental functions alongside or in combination with conventional infrastructure. The principle emphasizes multifunctional infrastructure that maintains or restores ecological processes while meeting its intended service.', 'Applies where ecological components can provide measurable functions such as water retention, filtration, cooling, erosion control, habitat, flood attenuation, or connectivity. Ecological infrastructure must be assessed for performance variability, land requirements, maintenance, hazards, time to maturity, and compatibility with the primary service.', 'Integrate ecological function into infrastructure design; pursue multifunctionality; protect and restore ecosystem processes; combine grey and ecological infrastructure where appropriate; measure both service and ecological outcomes; design for long-term maintenance.', 'The principle shifts infrastructure evaluation from damage minimization alone toward potential ecological contribution. Distinguishing emphasis: ecological processes or structures perform an infrastructure-like service alongside or instead of conventional infrastructure.', 'ecological infrastructure; multifunctional infrastructure; nature-positive design', 'Infrastructure should be evaluated not only for the service it provides to people but also for ecological functions that its location and design can preserve, restore or create.', 'Nature-Derived; Problem-Inversion-Derived; Anticipatory-Design-Derived', 'Infrastructure occupies physical space and can alter flows, habitat and microclimate; design can intentionally shape these effects.', 'Green infrastructure; constructed wetlands; habitat-integrated infrastructure.', 'UN SDG Goals 6, 11 and 15; nature-based solutions literature.', 'Emerging', 'Civil; architecture; urban planning; water; transport', 'Ecological claims require evidence; ''green'' appearance is not sufficient.', 'Candidate discovery when infrastructure interacts materially with habitat, water, microclimate or ecological processes and can be designed for multiple functions.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0018', 'Nature-Positive Occupied Space', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Urban Ecology; Architecture; Infrastructure', 'Nature-positive occupied space proposes that land, buildings, campuses, transport corridors, and other developed spaces should be evaluated not only by the human functions they accommodate but also by whether their occupied footprint can retain, restore, or create measurable ecological functions. The objective is to reduce the ecological loss associated with development and, where feasible, produce net ecological gains.', 'Applies at the site and landscape scale where design and management can influence habitat, biodiversity, water retention, soil function, thermal regulation, connectivity, or ecological resources. A planted or green surface is not automatically nature-positive; ecological quality, additionality, permanence, maintenance, and displacement effects must be assessed.', 'Treat occupied space as multifunctional; retain ecological structure where possible; restore habitat and ecological processes; connect fragmented habitat; manage water and heat through ecological functions; verify ecological outcomes rather than relying on labels.', 'A stronger form of nature-positive design that asks what ecological function can be created in the same footprint. Distinguishing emphasis: the occupied or developed footprint is evaluated for measurable ecological retention, restoration, or net gain, even when ecological function is not replacing an infrastructure service.', 'nature-positive infrastructure; ecological function; multifunctionality', 'A system that occupies ecological space should seek opportunities to provide, rather than merely avoid damaging, ecological functions.', 'Problem-Inversion-Derived; Nature-Derived; Anticipatory-Design-Derived', 'The occupied footprint becomes an opportunity for habitat, water retention, cooling, carbon or other functions.', 'Green roofs, constructed wetlands, bioswales, habitat-integrated structures.', 'UN SDG Goals 11 and 15; emerging nature-positive design literature.', 'Emerging', 'Buildings; roads; campuses; cities; industrial sites', 'Ecological function must be defined, measured and maintained.', 'Candidate discovery when occupied or developed space can support additional ecological functions without compromising the primary service or creating material hazards.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0019', 'Ecological Limits', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Ecological Limits; Development; Climate', 'Ecological limits recognizes that human systems operate within biophysical conditions and ecosystem capacities that cannot be removed simply by technological substitution or economic optimization. Some ecological functions, thresholds, stocks, and feedbacks constrain what levels of extraction, pollution, disturbance, or transformation can be sustained.', 'Applies when system performance depends materially on ecological stocks, flows, thresholds, carrying capacity, assimilative capacity, biodiversity, climate stability, or other biophysical conditions. Limits are context- and scale-dependent and should be supported by evidence; the principle does not imply that every ecological state has a single fixed threshold.', 'Identify ecological dependencies and constraints; distinguish physical limits from assumed limits; detect thresholds and nonlinear feedbacks; avoid unlimited substitution assumptions; keep system demand within defensible ecological conditions; monitor conditions that could change the limit.', 'The principle distinguishes technological mitigation from the continued functioning of life-support systems.', 'ecological limits; thresholds; substitution; planetary boundaries', 'Development should be adapted to ecological limits and system capacity rather than assuming that technology can indefinitely substitute for degraded natural functions.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived; Systems-Thinking-Derived', 'Natural systems provide functions that may be difficult, costly or impossible to fully replace.', 'Climate adaptation, water management, biodiversity protection.', 'IPCC AR6; UN SDG Goals 6, 13 and 15.', 'Established principle', 'All fields involving resource or ecological dependencies', 'Limits are context-specific and should be supported by evidence rather than vague claims.', 'Candidate discovery when a system depends on ecological capacity, natural limits or ecosystem functions that are not fully replaceable by technical substitution.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0020', 'Burden Transfer', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Systems Analysis; Lifecycle; Justice', 'Burden transfer identifies situations in which an apparent sustainability improvement in one location, group, lifecycle stage, environmental compartment, or time period is achieved by shifting costs, risks, emissions, resource depletion, or health and social burdens elsewhere. A sustainability claim is incomplete when its relevant burden transfers are outside the assessment boundary.', 'Applies whenever an intervention changes material or energy flows, production location, waste destination, labour conditions, exposure, resource sourcing, or timing of impacts. The appropriate boundary should include materially affected actors and lifecycle stages without expanding the analysis so broadly that causal relevance is lost.', 'Check where burdens go; use lifecycle and supply-chain perspectives; examine distributional effects; identify temporal transfers; avoid double counting; distinguish genuine burden reduction from relocation or delayed realization.', 'Sustainability assessment should track where benefits and burdens go, not only whether one local metric improves.', 'burden shifting; externalities; lifecycle; system boundary; trade-offs', 'When solving a problem, evaluate whether the proposed solution transfers the environmental, social or health burden to another place, group, life stage or future time.', 'Systems-Thinking-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived; Justice-Derived', 'A solution changes flows or risks across boundaries.', 'Lifecycle assessment; pollution displacement; outsourcing of hazardous work.', 'UN SDG 12; UN SDG 10; IPCC lifecycle and equity literature.', 'Established analytical principle', 'Engineering; business; policy; supply chains; urban systems', 'Not every upstream/downstream effect is equally material; prioritization is required.', 'Candidate discovery when an intervention may relocate environmental, health, social or temporal burdens; require explicit boundary and affected-party analysis.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0021', 'Equitable Sustainability', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Justice; Development; Public Policy', 'Equitable sustainability holds that sustainability outcomes include the distribution of benefits, burdens, risks, opportunities, access, and decision-making power among people and groups. A technically or environmentally effective intervention can still be unsustainable in social terms when it systematically concentrates burdens or excludes affected populations from relevant benefits or opportunities.', 'Applies where environmental or technological decisions have differentiated effects because of income, vulnerability, geography, access, institutional power, disability, age, occupation, or other contextually relevant factors. Equity analysis must be evidence-based and should not infer vulnerability solely from group labels.', 'Examine distribution, not only aggregate performance; identify disproportionately affected groups; protect essential access; assess affordability and exposure; consider procedural as well as distributive equity; make trade-offs visible.', 'The principle prevents aggregate improvement from hiding systematic harm to less powerful groups.', 'environmental justice; equity; distribution; vulnerability; fairness', 'A sustainability intervention should improve or protect human wellbeing without requiring another population or community to bear disproportionate costs or risks.', 'Justice-Derived; Environmental-Justice-Derived; Human-Wellbeing-Derived', 'Benefits and burdens are socially distributed rather than experienced equally.', 'Environmental justice; equitable public-service design.', 'UN SDG Goal 10; UN 2030 Agenda; IPCC AR6 equity literature.', 'Established', 'Infrastructure; health; environment; policy; technology', 'Equity does not always mean identical outcomes; the relevant distribution must be defined.', 'Candidate discovery when benefits, costs, risks or access are unevenly distributed across populations; use application evidence rather than discipline alone.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0022', 'Human Capability and Dignity', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Human Development; Design; Policy', 'Human capability and dignity frames sustainability in terms of whether systems expand or constrain people''s substantive ability to live healthy, secure, meaningful, autonomous, and socially valued lives. It shifts attention from aggregate production or consumption alone toward the capabilities, freedoms, access, agency, and conditions through which people can achieve valued outcomes.', 'Applies to technologies, infrastructure, institutions, services, workplaces, education, health systems, and policies that materially affect human opportunity or wellbeing. Capability should be operationalized through context-specific outcomes; the principle does not imply that all preferences or increases in consumption automatically represent improved wellbeing.', 'Protect dignity and agency; expand meaningful capabilities; ensure access to essential services; distinguish output from human outcome; identify constraints on autonomy and opportunity; assess who gains or loses capability.', 'The principle places people as ends of development rather than only inputs or consumers.', 'human wellbeing; dignity; capability; human development; agency', 'Systems intended to serve people should be designed around human needs, dignity and capabilities rather than treating people primarily as consumers, inputs or units of economic output.', 'Human-Needs-Derived; Human-Dignity-Derived; Capability-Derived', 'Human outcomes are broader than income, consumption or productivity.', 'Human development and capability approaches; rights-based design.', 'UN SDG Goals 3, 4 and 10; UN 2030 Agenda.', 'Established', 'Health; education; engineering; economics; policy; technology', 'Capability is context-dependent and must be defined with affected people.', 'Candidate discovery when a system materially affects human capability, dignity, autonomy, access or wellbeing; evaluate people as affected agents rather than only demand units.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0023', 'Preventive Sustainability', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY', 'Health; Safety; Public Health; Engineering', 'Preventive sustainability prioritizes changing upstream causes of avoidable harm when credible evidence shows that prevention can reduce the probability, magnitude, or recurrence of environmental, health, safety, or social damage. It favors interventions that alter causal conditions rather than repeatedly managing consequences after harm has occurred.', 'Applies when the causal pathway to a material harm is sufficiently understood and preventive intervention is feasible and proportionate. Prevention is distinct from treatment, mitigation, compensation, and emergency response, although these may remain necessary where prevention cannot eliminate risk.', 'Identify causes before symptoms; intervene upstream; reduce exposure and hazard at source; compare prevention with downstream control; account for residual risk; evaluate whether prevention creates new burdens.', 'The principle moves intervention upstream from response to prevention. Distinguishing emphasis: use this concept for broad proactive avoidance of sustainability harm across design or operations, rather than for the narrower sequence of preventing pollution or damage before remediation.', 'prevention; risk reduction; upstream intervention; public health', 'Where preventable harm can be reduced by changing the conditions that produce it, prevention should be considered before relying primarily on intervention after harm occurs.', 'Health-and-Prevention-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived', 'Removing or reducing causal conditions can reduce recurring harm and associated resource demand.', 'Public-health prevention; occupational safety; pollution prevention.', 'UN SDG Goal 3; UN SDG Goal 8; IPCC risk-management literature.', 'Established', 'Health; workplace; environmental engineering; infrastructure; policy', 'Prevention is not always possible; residual response capacity remains necessary.', 'Candidate discovery when causal conditions of avoidable harm can be changed upstream; distinguish prevention from downstream mitigation or treatment.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0024', 'Care and Dependency', 'LIFE; PEOPLE', 'Care; Health; Social Systems', 'Care and dependency recognizes that human wellbeing depends on relationships and systems of care throughout the life course and during periods of illness, disability, ageing, childhood, crisis, or other dependency. Sustainable systems therefore need to accommodate care needs, accessibility, caregiver capacity, and the unequal distribution of care work.', 'Applies wherever system design, infrastructure, employment, public services, technology, or policy affects people whose ability to act independently is limited or temporarily reduced. Dependency is context-specific and should not be equated with incapacity or reduced human value.', 'Design for accessibility and care; recognize dependency as a normal life-course condition; support caregivers; reduce avoidable care burdens; maintain continuity of essential support; evaluate whether system design excludes people with different capabilities.', 'The principle broadens sustainability beyond autonomous economic actors.', 'care; dependency; vulnerability; life course; social care', 'Systems of care should recognize dependency and vulnerability as normal features of human life rather than treating care as an external or residual activity.', 'Care-Derived; Life-Course-Derived; Human-Wellbeing-Derived', 'Human wellbeing depends on care relationships and supportive institutions.', 'Care systems; accessible infrastructure; public health and social support.', 'UN SDG Goals 3, 5 and 10; human-development literature.', 'Established concept', 'Healthcare; architecture; transport; education; workplaces; public policy', 'Care arrangements differ culturally and institutionally; avoid assuming one model.', 'Candidate discovery when system performance depends on care, dependency, accessibility or vulnerability; include affected human needs in the application context.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0025', 'Social Cohesion and Mutual Support', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Sociology; Social Psychology; Governance', 'Social cohesion and mutual support concerns the relationships, trust, networks, shared norms, and cooperative capacities that enable people and organizations to coordinate, assist one another, resolve disputes, and respond collectively to disturbance. These social capacities can influence whether technical or institutional interventions function in practice.', 'Applies when cooperation, trust, information exchange, mutual aid, collective response, or conflict resolution materially affects system performance or resilience. Social cohesion should not be equated with uniformity or absence of disagreement; legitimate disagreement, deliberation, and institutional conflict resolution can strengthen a system.', 'Build trustworthy relationships; preserve communication networks; enable mutual aid; support constructive conflict resolution; recognize social networks as system capacity; avoid assuming cohesion where evidence of cooperation is absent.', 'The principle treats social relationships as part of the conditions enabling collective resilience.', 'social networks; trust; cooperation; social cohesion; prosocial behaviour', 'A sustainable society should preserve the relationships, trust and mutual support that enable people to cooperate through ordinary needs and periods of stress.', 'Social-Relationship-Derived; Social-Cohesion-Derived; Collective-Action-Derived', 'Social connections influence information flow, cooperation and collective response.', 'Community networks; mutual-aid systems; participatory institutions.', 'UN SDG Goal 16; social-science evidence.', 'Established concept', 'Communities; organizations; campuses; governance; digital platforms', 'Social cohesion can also exclude outsiders; inclusion and rights must be considered.', 'Candidate discovery when cooperation, trust, mutual support or social networks materially affect system functioning or response under stress.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0026', 'Meaningful Participation', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Participation; Public Policy; Design', 'Meaningful participation requires that people materially affected by a decision have an appropriate opportunity to contribute relevant knowledge, identify impacts, question assumptions, deliberate alternatives, and influence the decision or its implementation. Participation is substantive when input has a credible pathway into decision-making rather than serving only as information collection or symbolic consultation.', 'Applies when affected people possess relevant experience, local knowledge, interests, rights, or decision-making roles and when participation can improve legitimacy, information quality, implementation, or distributional outcomes. The form and depth of participation should be proportionate to the decision and institutional context.', 'Identify affected actors; provide accessible opportunities to participate; make decision influence explicit; incorporate relevant local knowledge; provide feedback on how input was used; distinguish consultation, participation, co-design, and delegated decision-making.', 'The principle connects knowledge quality and legitimacy with participation. Distinguishing emphasis: the core requirement is genuine stakeholder influence on information, priorities, or decisions; the setting need not involve governance of a shared natural resource.', 'participation; stakeholder engagement; local knowledge; procedural justice', 'People affected by a decision should have meaningful opportunities to contribute knowledge, question assumptions and influence the decision where their interests are materially affected.', 'Participation-Derived; Procedural-Justice-Derived; Knowledge-System-Derived', 'Affected people possess context and interests not always visible to technical decision-makers.', 'Community planning; environmental assessment; participatory design.', 'UN SDG Goals 6, 10 and 16.', 'Established', 'Engineering; planning; law; policy; public services; research', 'Participation cannot replace technical expertise; it must interact with evidence and professional responsibility.', 'Candidate discovery when affected people hold relevant local knowledge, interests or decision rights; participation must be meaningful to the decision at issue.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0027', 'Mechanism-Based Traditional Knowledge', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Traditional Knowledge; History; Engineering; Ecology', 'Mechanism-based traditional knowledge examines historical, Indigenous, local, or community-held practices by identifying the physical, ecological, social, or organizational mechanisms that allowed a practice to function before judging whether the practice remains appropriate. The principle supports critical learning from accumulated experience without treating age or cultural origin as proof of sustainability.', 'Applies when a practice contains potentially transferable knowledge about local materials, climate, ecology, resource management, construction, agriculture, health, water, or social organization. Knowledge must be interpreted with attention to provenance, cultural rights, context, changing conditions, evidence, and the possibility that some practices may no longer be appropriate.', 'Ask how the practice works; preserve relevant knowledge and attribution; test mechanisms under current conditions; distinguish transferable mechanism from historical form; respect knowledge holders and cultural context; combine local evidence with scientific validation where appropriate.', 'The principle turns traditional practice into a source of testable hypotheses rather than nostalgia.', 'traditional knowledge; local knowledge; historical practice; mechanism recovery', 'Knowledge that has enabled a community to live with a particular place should be examined for its underlying mechanisms before being discarded as obsolete.', 'Traditional-Knowledge-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived; Knowledge-Continuity-Derived', 'Long-term local observation may contain system knowledge that is absent from generic models.', 'Traditional irrigation, seed systems, vernacular architecture and local ecological knowledge.', 'UNESCO; FAO; IPCC recognition of Indigenous and local knowledge.', 'Established as a knowledge-recovery approach', 'All disciplines; especially engineering, agriculture, water and architecture', 'Historical persistence alone is not evidence of sustainability or suitability today.', 'Candidate discovery when place-based or community-held knowledge may contain mechanisms relevant to the technical problem; validate the mechanism rather than treating tradition as proof.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0028', 'Transition Function Preservation', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Technology Transitions; Systems Analysis; History', 'Transition function preservation holds that replacing an existing technology, practice, institution, or infrastructure can unintentionally remove functions that were not represented in the primary performance metric. A transition should therefore compare the full set of material, ecological, social, cultural, maintenance, knowledge, and resilience functions provided by the existing arrangement against those provided by the proposed replacement.', 'Applies to technology transitions, infrastructure replacement, organizational change, modernization, digitization, and policy reform where the existing system performs multiple explicit or implicit functions. The principle does not oppose replacement; it requires that material functions and dependencies are identified before they are lost.', 'Inventory existing functions; identify hidden or secondary functions; compare old and new systems on a common functional basis; protect critical functions during transition; provide substitutes where necessary; distinguish obsolete form from valuable function.', 'The principle asks what the old system was doing beyond the function for which it was explicitly measured.', 'technology transition; hidden functions; path dependence; functional comparison', 'When a long-used practice is replaced by a modern system, evaluate not only immediate efficiency but also ecological, social, cultural and knowledge functions that may have been lost.', 'Historical-Practice-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived; Cross-Domain-Synthesis', 'A system can contain tacit functions not represented in performance metrics.', 'Replacement of traditional water, agricultural, building or craft systems.', 'Case-based sustainability transition literature; UNESCO/FAO documentation.', 'Emerging analytical principle', 'Technology transitions; infrastructure; agriculture; architecture; policy', 'Losses must be demonstrated; do not assume the older system was better.', 'Candidate discovery when replacing an established practice may remove ecological, social, cultural, maintenance or knowledge functions not represented in the primary performance metric.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0029', 'Adaptive Learning', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Adaptive Management; Resilience; Decision Systems', 'Adaptive learning treats monitoring, feedback, evaluation, and revision as continuing components of sustainability management under uncertainty and changing conditions. Rather than assuming that an initial design or policy will remain optimal, the system retains the capability to learn from observed outcomes and adjust decisions.', 'Applies when conditions, system responses, knowledge, or forecasts are uncertain or changing and when decisions can be revised without unacceptable delay or irreversible commitment. Adaptive learning requires meaningful indicators, feedback pathways, decision authority, and institutional capacity to act on evidence.', 'Monitor outcomes; compare observations with expectations; learn from deviations and failures; update assumptions; revise interventions; preserve flexibility; distinguish genuine adaptive management from repeated changes without learning or evaluation.', 'Adaptability is treated as a system capability rather than a one-time design property. Distinguishing emphasis: monitoring and feedback are used to learn and revise understanding or practice over time; explicit management of a highly uncertain intervention is not required.', 'adaptive management; learning; feedback; uncertainty; resilience', 'A system facing uncertainty should preserve the ability to learn, adapt and revise its rules rather than optimizing permanently for conditions that may no longer hold.', 'Adaptive-Management-Derived; Foresight-Derived; Uncertainty-and-Learning-Derived', 'Feedback updates decisions as evidence changes.', 'Adaptive water management; climate adaptation; iterative policy.', 'IPCC AR6; UN SDG 13; adaptive-management literature.', 'Established approach', 'Water; climate; infrastructure; governance; AI; organizations', 'Adaptation requires institutional capacity and reliable monitoring.', 'Candidate discovery when feedback, uncertainty and changing conditions require learning and revision rather than one-time optimization.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0030', 'Precaution Under Irreversible Risk', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Risk; Environmental Law; Decision-Making', 'Precaution under irreversible risk states that when an activity presents a credible possibility of serious or irreversible harm and scientific uncertainty prevents reliable prediction of the outcome, decision-making should avoid unnecessary commitment to the harmful pathway and consider proportionate protective alternatives. Precaution concerns uncertainty combined with consequence, not uncertainty alone.', 'Applies when potential harm is serious, persistent or difficult to reverse and the evidence is insufficient for confident risk quantification. The principle does not justify arbitrary action: severity, plausibility, uncertainty, proportionality, available alternatives, evidence quality, and the costs of precautionary measures should be considered explicitly.', 'Identify irreversible outcomes; characterize uncertainty; avoid irreversible lock-in where reasonable; prefer reversible or safer pathways when justified; seek additional evidence; use proportionate safeguards; distinguish precaution from prohibition without evidence.', 'The principle is not zero-risk; it changes the burden and timing of decision-making under serious uncertainty. Distinguishing emphasis: uncertainty is combined with credible serious or irreversible harm, so protective action may be required before learning-by-doing is acceptable.', 'precaution; uncertainty; irreversible harm; risk governance', 'Where uncertainty is high and potential harm is serious or irreversible, decisions should avoid locking the system into damaging pathways before critical uncertainties are resolved.', 'Policy-and-Normative-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived', 'Precaution is triggered by uncertainty plus potentially serious or irreversible harm.', 'Environmental impact assessment; emerging technology risk assessment.', 'UN Rio/precautionary principle; UN SDG environmental governance; IPCC risk literature.', 'Established', 'Environmental engineering; law; technology; public policy', 'Precaution must not be used to ignore proportionality, evidence or feasible alternatives.', 'Candidate discovery when uncertainty combines with potentially serious or irreversible harm; apply only where the risk and uncertainty conditions are evidenced.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0031', 'Cross-Boundary Resource Exchange', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Industrial Ecology; Systems Engineering; Urban Systems', 'Cross-boundary resource exchange identifies sustainability opportunities that become visible when two or more processes, organizations, infrastructures, or ecosystems are analysed as interacting systems and their exchanges of materials, energy, water, information, services, or capacity are made explicit. The principle challenges boundaries that classify a useful exchange as unrelated waste or externality.', 'Applies when there is a real exchange pathway with compatible quantities, quality, timing, location, ownership, safety, or governance conditions. Expanding a system boundary should improve causal understanding rather than simply aggregate unrelated processes, and the assessment must avoid double counting benefits or impacts.', 'Map flows across organizational or process boundaries; identify compatible exchanges; test quantity, quality and timing; evaluate transaction and infrastructure requirements; account for lifecycle effects; verify that an apparent exchange creates a net sustainability benefit.', 'The principle turns boundary selection into an explicit sustainability design decision. Distinguishing emphasis: changing the analytical boundary reveals meaningful resource, energy, water, information, service, or capacity exchanges; a by-product substitution transaction is only one possible form.', 'system boundary; industrial symbiosis; resource exchange; coupled processes', 'When two systems exchange resources, impacts or services, sustainability should be evaluated across their combined boundary rather than optimizing each independently.', 'Boundary-Redesign-Derived; Systems-Thinking-Derived; Cross-Domain-Synthesis', 'A combined boundary can expose by-product, heat, water, data or infrastructure exchanges.', 'Industrial symbiosis; campus resource exchange; urban metabolism.', 'UN SDG Goal 12; industrial ecology literature.', 'Established analytical approach', 'Chemical; civil; mechanical; energy; campus; urban planning', 'Boundary expansion should be justified by real dependencies, not used to hide impacts.', 'Candidate discovery when two processes, systems or actors exchange materials, energy, water, information, services or infrastructure and the combined boundary changes the sustainability assessment.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0032', 'Continued Value Under Change', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Design; Modularity; Adaptability; Lifecycle', 'Continued value under change describes the ability of a product, infrastructure system, digital asset, institution, or body of knowledge to retain useful function when conditions such as use, ownership, technology, standards, demand, or operating environment change. Sustainability can improve when systems are designed so that change does not immediately strand valuable components or knowledge.', 'Applies where future change is plausible and where modularity, interoperability, adaptability, reconfiguration, reuse, or transferable knowledge can preserve function. Flexibility can itself create material, coordination, or complexity costs, so it should be proportionate to credible future uncertainty.', 'Design for change; preserve interoperability; use modular components where justified; enable reconfiguration and reuse; maintain transferable information; avoid unnecessary lock-in; evaluate flexibility against its lifecycle and complexity costs.', 'The principle extends durability from physical life to continued functional relevance.', 'adaptability; modularity; interoperability; future-proofing; reuse', 'A system should be designed so that useful outputs, components or knowledge can remain valuable when the original use, owner, technology or operating condition changes.', 'Engineering-Derived; Life-Cycle-Derived; Anticipatory-Design-Derived', 'Design flexibility preserves value across changing conditions.', 'Modular buildings; interoperable digital systems; adaptable infrastructure.', 'Sustainable design and resilience literature; UN SDG 9.', 'Emerging', 'Infrastructure; software; buildings; products; campuses', 'Flexibility can increase initial complexity and embodied resources; trade-offs must be assessed.', 'Candidate discovery when future changes in use, ownership, technology or operating conditions could otherwise strand useful outputs, components or knowledge.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0033', 'Metric-Aware Optimization', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Optimization; Measurement; Systems', 'Metric-aware optimization recognizes that an optimization result is only as sustainable as the objective, constraints, system boundary, and metrics used to produce it. Improving a measurable proxy can worsen important environmental, social, human, or long-term outcomes when those outcomes are omitted or poorly represented.', 'Applies to engineering optimization, operations research, AI and machine learning, policy metrics, performance management, and multi-objective decision-making. The principle is especially relevant when a metric is a proxy rather than the final outcome of interest or when optimization changes behaviour in response to the metric.', 'Define the real outcome; distinguish proxy from objective; identify omitted externalities; include material constraints; test for gaming and rebound effects; use supplementary indicators where a single metric is inadequate; examine distributional and long-term consequences.', 'The principle connects optimization with measurement validity and system boundaries.', 'optimization; proxy metric; externality; unintended consequence; multi-objective', 'When a system is optimized for a single measurable objective, its sustainability should also be tested against important outcomes that the objective does not measure.', 'Systems-Thinking-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'A metric is a proxy for a broader objective; optimizing the proxy can distort the broader outcome.', 'Algorithmic optimization; engineering design; public-service metrics.', 'UN SDG 12; measurement and systems literature.', 'Established analytical principle', 'AI; operations; engineering; policy; economics', 'Not every unmeasured outcome is material; relevance must be justified.', 'Candidate discovery when optimization relies on a proxy metric that may omit important environmental, social, human or long-term outcomes.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0034', 'Technology for Expanded Human and Ecological Options', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'AI; Emerging Technology; Innovation', 'Technology for expanded human and ecological options considers technology as a mechanism that changes what actions are feasible, affordable, observable, or scalable. Sustainability therefore asks not only whether a technology is more efficient, but whether the new capability expands the set of desirable options, changes system direction, redistributes capability, or merely accelerates an existing unsustainable pattern.', 'Applies to digital systems, AI, biotechnology, automation, sensing, advanced materials, energy technologies, and other innovations that materially change system capabilities. Assessment should include access, governance, rebound effects, second-order consequences, dependency, failure modes, and ecological and social impacts.', 'Evaluate changed option space; distinguish capability expansion from throughput acceleration; examine who gains capability; assess rebound and second-order effects; preserve human and ecological agency; govern new capabilities in proportion to risk.', 'The principle evaluates innovation by what futures it enables, not merely by efficiency gains.', 'technology and society; innovation; option space; rebound effects', 'Technological capability should expand human and ecological options rather than simply accelerate an existing unsustainable pattern.', 'Emerging-Technology-Derived; Human-Wellbeing-Derived; Technology-Society-Derived', 'New technology changes what can be observed, controlled or coordinated; its sustainability depends on direction and governance.', 'AI-enabled monitoring; distributed sensing; assistive technologies; ecological restoration technologies.', 'UN SDG Goals 9, 10 and 4.7; emerging technology literature.', 'Emerging', 'AI; robotics; biotechnology; digital infrastructure; assistive technology', 'Technology can widen or narrow access; sustainability must include distribution and governance.', 'Candidate discovery when technology changes the feasible set of human or ecological actions; evaluate whether the new capability expands sustainable options or merely accelerates an existing pattern.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0035', 'Human–Ecological Computational Coordination', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'AI; Data Science; Cyber-Physical Systems', 'Human–ecological computational coordination describes the use of sensing, data, models, computation, and feedback systems to represent and coordinate interactions among human activities and ecological conditions. Its sustainability value depends on whether computational objectives adequately represent relevant system outcomes and whether the resulting decisions are interpretable, accountable, and adaptable.', 'Applies to cyber-physical systems, environmental informatics, AI, digital twins, monitoring networks, decision-support systems, and other computational systems that influence resource or ecological management. Valid measurements, uncertainty characterization, appropriate spatial and temporal scales, governance, privacy where relevant, and clear decision authority are required.', 'Sense relevant conditions; integrate heterogeneous evidence; model coupled processes; expose feedback; coordinate decisions; represent uncertainty; align computational objectives with human and ecological outcomes; maintain accountability for automated or data-informed decisions.', 'The principle connects digital capability with sustainability only when data are valid and objectives represent the system that matters.', 'AI for sustainability; sensing; digital twins; ecological monitoring; decision support', 'Data and computation should be used to make system conditions visible and coordinate action with ecological and human needs, not only to maximize a predefined operational metric.', 'Human–Technology–Ecology-Derived; Emerging-Technology-Derived; Systems-Thinking-Derived', 'Digital systems create feedback and coordination capabilities; measurement and objective design determine whether those capabilities help sustainability.', 'Smart water management; ecological monitoring; energy coordination; health decision support.', 'UN SDG Goals 9, 11 and 13; UN SDG indicator metadata; AI/sustainability literature.', 'Emerging', 'AI; IoT; CPS; digital twins; environmental monitoring; public services', 'Bad data, biased objectives or opaque decision rules can worsen outcomes.', 'Candidate discovery when sensing, data or computation can expose system conditions and coordinate action across human and ecological needs; validate objectives and governance before recommending.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0036', 'Multi-Future Robustness', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Foresight; Resilience; Planning', 'Multi-future robustness emphasizes maintaining acceptable performance across a range of plausible future conditions rather than optimizing solely for one predicted future. A robust design or policy retains useful function when climate, demand, resource availability, technology, regulation, or social conditions differ from the planning assumptions.', 'Applies where future uncertainty is material and where decisions have long lifetimes or high switching costs. Scenario sets should represent meaningful uncertainty rather than arbitrary extremes, and robustness should be evaluated against explicit performance thresholds and decision objectives.', 'Use multiple plausible futures; identify vulnerabilities across scenarios; prefer solutions that avoid catastrophic underperformance; retain adaptive options; compare robustness with cost; distinguish robustness from simple overdesign.', 'The principle changes design from prediction optimization to robustness across uncertainty.', 'foresight; scenarios; robustness; adaptive capacity; uncertainty', 'A future-facing system should be evaluated for how well it can adapt to several plausible futures rather than how efficiently it performs under one predicted future.', 'Foresight-Derived; Anticipatory-Design-Derived', 'Scenario analysis exposes assumptions that single forecasts hide.', 'Climate-resilient infrastructure; long-lived public assets.', 'IPCC AR6; UN SDG 13.', 'Established approach', 'Infrastructure; climate; water; urban planning; policy', 'Scenarios are not predictions and should not be treated as probabilities without evidence.', 'Candidate discovery when multiple plausible futures materially change design or operating requirements; use scenario robustness rather than assuming one forecast.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0037', 'Problem Inversion for Sustainability', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Design; Systems Innovation; Nature-Based Solutions', 'Problem inversion for sustainability reframes a problem by asking whether the apparent deficit of capacity, supply, space, treatment, or infrastructure is partly created by an overlooked function, waste, resource, ecological process, or system interaction. The reframing can reveal solutions that meet the underlying need without simply increasing the conventional capacity being demanded.', 'Applies when a problem statement is expressed primarily as a shortage or need for additional capacity and when system analysis can reveal alternative functions or resources. The reframed problem must still be tested against physical feasibility, user needs, safety, cost, lifecycle impacts, and evidence.', 'Define the underlying need; question the assumed capacity requirement; identify overlooked functions and resources; search for multifunctional solutions; test the new framing quantitatively; avoid replacing one unsupported assumption with another.', 'The principle deliberately reverses the problem statement to search for a system function that could remove or reduce the problem.', 'problem inversion; missing function; design opportunity; multifunctionality', 'A sustainability problem should sometimes be reframed by asking whether the system causing the problem could instead contribute to the function that is currently missing.', 'Problem-Inversion-Derived; Cross-Domain-Synthesis; Anticipatory-Design-Derived', 'The system causing a burden may also contain space, energy, data or capability that can contribute to a missing function.', 'Infrastructure providing habitat/cooling; waste stream providing feedstock; buildings retaining stormwater.', 'Emerging nature-positive and circular-design literature.', 'Emerging', 'Engineering; architecture; manufacturing; campuses; cities; AI', 'The proposed missing function must be real and measurable.', 'Candidate discovery when the source of a burden may also contain space, energy, material, information or capability that can contribute to the missing function; require a defensible mechanism.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0038', 'Mechanism-Based Principle Transfer', '', 'Knowledge Transfer; Research Methods; Design', 'Mechanism-based principle transfer states that a sustainability principle or useful design logic can be transferred between domains only when the underlying causal mechanism, relevant variables, assumptions, scale, and boundary conditions are sufficiently similar or can be explicitly adapted. Surface resemblance between two practices is not sufficient evidence of transferability.', 'Applies to cross-disciplinary learning, analogy, design transfer, traditional knowledge interpretation, biomimicry, engineering adaptation, and curriculum recommendation. Transfer should be tested for mechanism, operating conditions, scale, constraints, trade-offs, and evidence rather than inferred from shared terminology.', 'Identify the source mechanism; state assumptions; compare boundary conditions; test scale dependence; identify differences that could invalidate transfer; adapt rather than copy; validate the transferred principle in the new context.', 'This is a methodological safeguard for the recommendation system itself.', 'knowledge transfer; mechanism-based analogy; boundary conditions; validation', 'A sustainability principle should be transferred to another context only after its underlying mechanism, boundary conditions and trade-offs have been identified.', 'Cross-Domain-Synthesis; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Failure-and-Lesson-Derived', 'Mechanistic similarity is stronger evidence for transfer than keyword similarity.', 'Cross-domain transfer of water, ecological, social or engineering principles.', 'Research-methods evidence; UNESCO/FAO/IPCC cross-knowledge discussions.', 'Proposed methodological principle', 'All disciplines', 'A plausible analogy can still fail if scale, boundary conditions or interactions differ.', 'Candidate discovery for cross-domain transfer only after mechanism, scope, boundary conditions and trade-offs are explicit; semantic similarity alone is insufficient.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0039', 'Renewal and Recovery Capacity', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Resilience; Ecology; Infrastructure; Organizations', 'Renewal and recovery capacity concerns the ability of a system to absorb disturbance, restore essential functions, reorganize, and continue developing after disruption rather than merely maintaining peak performance under normal conditions. Recovery may depend on redundancy, modularity, ecological regeneration, social networks, learning, resource reserves, or institutional flexibility.', 'Applies where systems face shocks, disturbances, failures, disasters, ecological change, market disruption, or other events that can interrupt normal operation. Recovery should be defined against the functions that matter, including acceptable recovery time and minimum service level; resilience is not synonymous with rapid return to the previous state.', 'Prepare for disturbance; preserve recovery pathways; maintain essential redundancy and reserves; support rapid restoration of critical functions; learn after disruption; allow beneficial reorganization; distinguish recovery from prevention and from normal operating efficiency.', 'The principle distinguishes resilience and recovery capacity from uninterrupted efficiency.', 'resilience; recovery; renewal; regeneration; disturbance', 'A system should preserve the capacity for renewal and recovery after disturbance rather than optimizing only for uninterrupted performance under normal conditions.', 'Resilience-Derived; Nature-Derived; Life-Cycle-Derived', 'Disturbance exposes hidden dependencies and the value of recovery capacity.', 'Ecological recovery; disaster-resilient infrastructure; organizational resilience.', 'IPCC AR6; UN SDG Goals 11, 13 and 15.', 'Established', 'Ecosystems; infrastructure; organizations; communities; digital systems', 'Resilience can involve trade-offs with efficiency and cost.', 'Candidate discovery when disturbance, recovery time or post-failure functioning matters; distinguish recovery capacity from uninterrupted normal performance.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0040', 'Human Flourishing Within Ecological Conditions', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Human Development; Sustainability; Education', 'Human flourishing within ecological conditions is an integrative sustainability principle in which human wellbeing, dignity, capability, and meaningful opportunity are pursued together with maintenance of the ecological and social conditions that make those outcomes possible over time. It rejects both human wellbeing defined without ecological constraints and ecological protection framed without consideration of legitimate human needs.', 'Applies as a higher-level framing principle when a sustainability problem materially involves both human outcomes and ecological conditions. It is not itself a single operational metric; specific analyses must define the relevant capabilities, wellbeing outcomes, ecological conditions, distributional concerns, and time horizon.', 'Treat human and ecological outcomes as jointly relevant; define concrete wellbeing and ecological conditions; protect essential capabilities; consider distribution and future generations; evaluate trade-offs explicitly; avoid using the concept as a generic label for any project described as sustainable.', 'The principle places human flourishing and life-support conditions together rather than reducing sustainability to production or consumption.', 'human flourishing; wellbeing; capability; ecological conditions; sustainable development', 'Human development should increase people''s real opportunities to live healthy, secure, meaningful and capable lives while maintaining the ecological conditions that make those opportunities possible.', 'Human-Wellbeing-Derived; Capability-Derived; Intergenerational-Culture-Derived', 'Human outcomes and ecological conditions are jointly necessary for long-term wellbeing.', 'Human development; sustainable livelihoods; education for sustainable development.', 'UN 2030 Agenda; UN SDG Goals 3, 4, 10 and 15; UNDP human-development tradition.', 'Established integrative principle', 'All disciplines; especially education, health, engineering, policy and economics', 'Avoid treating ''flourishing'' as a vague universal metric; operationalize specific capabilities and ecological conditions.', 'Candidate discovery when human wellbeing and ecological conditions are jointly material; use as an integrative outcome concept, not as a generic label for every sustainability recommendation.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0041', 'Life-Cycle Responsibility', 'SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Lifecycle Assessment; Engineering; Manufacturing; Procurement', 'Evaluating a sustainability decision across the materially relevant stages of a product, service, infrastructure, or process so that impacts are not minimized at one stage by increasing them elsewhere.', 'Applies when upstream sourcing, manufacture, construction, operation, maintenance, use, reuse, and end-of-life stages materially affect the decision. The boundary should be broad enough to capture consequential transfers but bounded by causal relevance and evidence.', 'Define relevant life-cycle stages; compare alternatives on a common functional unit; include embodied and operational effects; identify upstream and downstream burdens; avoid stage-specific optimization; state system boundaries and assumptions.', 'Lifecycle effects should be considered across the stages materially affected by a sustainability decision rather than optimizing a single stage in isolation.', 'life-cycle; responsibility', 'Lifecycle effects should be considered across the stages materially affected by a sustainability decision rather than optimizing a single stage in isolation.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Engineering-Derived', 'Material extraction; manufacturing; transport; operation; maintenance; end-of-life.', 'Life-cycle assessment and life-cycle engineering; product and infrastructure end-of-life planning; environmental and social impact assessment across extraction, manufacture, use, maintenance, transport, and end-of-life.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Engineering; manufacturing; construction; product design; procurement', 'The assessment boundary must include the life-cycle stages materially affected by the decision and must avoid comparing alternatives with inconsistent system boundaries. Results depend on functional unit, allocation rules, data quality, geography, time horizon, and whether indirect or consequential effects are included.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that life-cycle responsibility materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0042', 'Material Circular Cycling', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Circular Economy; Materials; Manufacturing; Waste Management', 'Material circular cycling seeks to retain materials in productive use through reuse, repair, refurbishment, remanufacturing, recycling, and safe recovery while reducing dependence on virgin extraction and avoiding hazardous accumulation.', 'Applies where material flows can be separated, recovered, reused, or safely transformed without unacceptable quality loss, contamination, energy demand, or burden transfer. Circularity is not equivalent to recycling alone and does not justify retaining unsafe or obsolete products in circulation.', 'Keep materials in productive use; prioritize higher-value retention before lower-value recycling; design for recovery; preserve material quality; prevent contamination; account for energy and transport; verify net lifecycle benefit.', 'Material value can be retained through successive use cycles when product design, collection, separation, processing, and demand conditions support safe recirculation.', 'material; circular; cycling', 'Material value can be retained through successive use cycles when product design, collection, separation, processing, and demand conditions support safe recirculation.', 'Contemporary-Practice-Derived; Engineering-Derived', 'Reuse; repair; refurbishment; remanufacturing; recycling; virgin-material displacement.', 'Product repair and reuse systems; component refurbishment and remanufacturing; metals and polymer recycling; construction-material recovery; industrial by-product exchange.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established approach', 'Manufacturing; construction; electronics; packaging; campuses; infrastructure', 'Cycling is beneficial only when recovered material can perform a useful function without disproportionate energy, processing, contamination, transport, or quality-loss burdens. Material safety and accumulation of hazardous substances must be controlled.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that material circular cycling materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0043', 'Renewable Flow Utilization', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Energy; Water; Ecology; Engineering', 'Renewable flow utilization prioritizes energy or resource services derived from continuously or periodically renewed flows—such as sunlight, wind, flowing water, or sustainably available biomass—while respecting the ecological conditions and rates that sustain those flows.', 'Applies where a renewable flow is physically available and its use does not materially degrade the stock, ecosystem, or supporting conditions on which the flow depends. Renewable does not mean impact-free; land, materials, ecological disturbance, storage, transmission, and lifecycle effects remain relevant.', 'Use renewable flows within defensible ecological limits; distinguish flow from stock; account for intermittency; design storage and flexibility where needed; include infrastructure and lifecycle burdens; avoid assuming renewable supply is automatically sustainable.', 'Renewable flows can provide useful services without the same depletion mechanism as finite stocks, provided supporting ecological and physical conditions are maintained.', 'renewable; flow; utilization', 'Renewable flows can provide useful services without the same depletion mechanism as finite stocks, provided supporting ecological and physical conditions are maintained.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Engineering-Derived', 'Solar radiation; wind; hydropower; sustainably managed biomass; renewable resource flows.', 'Solar and wind energy systems; run-of-river and other flow-based water-energy applications; passive environmental flows; sustainably managed biomass where replenishment and ecological limits are established.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Energy; water; agriculture; infrastructure', 'Renewable flows are variable and site-dependent. Extraction or conversion can still damage ecosystems, compete with other users, require substantial materials, or exceed local environmental limits; renewable availability alone does not establish sustainability.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that renewable flow utilization materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0044', 'Demand Flexibility and Load Shifting', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY', 'Energy Systems; Water Systems; Infrastructure; Operations', 'Demand flexibility changes the timing, intensity, or form of resource use so that essential services are maintained while peak stress, losses, congestion, or high-impact supply requirements are reduced.', 'Applies where demand is partly controllable, deferrable, substitutable, or responsive to system conditions. Essential services, user constraints, equity, rebound effects, and reliability limits must be preserved; shifting demand without reducing total burden may merely relocate impacts.', 'Shift flexible demand away from constrained periods; preserve essential service; use storage and scheduling; expose temporal resource costs; coordinate demand with renewable or ecological availability; evaluate total and peak effects separately.', 'Changing when or how a service is delivered can reduce the capacity and resource intensity required to meet the same functional need.', 'demand; flexibility;; load; shifting', 'Changing when or how a service is delivered can reduce the capacity and resource intensity required to meet the same functional need.', 'Engineering-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'Peak shaving; demand response; scheduling; storage; time-of-use management; flexible water use.', 'Electricity demand response; thermal storage; irrigation scheduling; flexible pumping; building load management; computing workloads shifted to periods of lower grid stress or renewable availability.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Energy; water; transport; computing; buildings; industrial operations', 'Shifting demand is useful only when the system has genuine temporal flexibility and the receiving period has lower marginal stress or impact. It must not compromise essential services, equipment limits, user needs, or simply move the burden to another constrained period.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that demand flexibility and load shifting materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0045', 'Ecological Connectivity', 'ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Landscape Ecology; Biodiversity; Planning; Infrastructure', 'Ecological connectivity is the degree to which landscapes, habitats, waterways, or ecological networks allow organisms, genes, energy, water, or ecological processes to move between areas necessary for persistence and ecosystem functioning.', 'Applies at landscape or network scales where fragmentation, barriers, or isolated habitat patches materially affect ecological function. Connectivity must be defined for the relevant species or process; maximizing connectivity universally can create disease, invasive-species, or human-wildlife risks in some contexts.', 'Maintain or restore functional corridors; reduce harmful barriers; protect stepping stones and ecological networks; match connectivity measures to species and processes; evaluate trade-offs with infrastructure and safety.', 'Movement and ecological exchange between habitat components can maintain population viability, gene flow, recolonization, and ecosystem processes.', 'ecological; connectivity', 'Movement and ecological exchange between habitat components can maintain population viability, gene flow, recolonization, and ecosystem processes.', 'Nature-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'Habitat corridors; river continuity; landscape permeability; wildlife crossings; ecological networks.', 'Wildlife corridors; riparian continuity; fish passage; habitat stepping stones; landscape-scale conservation planning; restoration of fragmented ecological networks.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Conservation; urban planning; infrastructure; watershed management', 'Connectivity must be evaluated for the species, ecological processes, spatial scale, and barriers relevant to the case. Corridors can also transmit disease, invasive species, fire, or other hazards, so greater connectivity is not universally beneficial.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that ecological connectivity materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0046', 'Biodiversity Integrity', 'ECOLOGY; LIFE; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Ecology; Conservation; Agriculture; Land Management', 'Biodiversity integrity concerns maintaining the diversity, composition, structure, and ecological interactions necessary for ecosystems and populations to retain their characteristic functions and capacity to respond to change.', 'Applies when decisions affect species, genetic diversity, habitat, ecological interactions, or ecosystem composition. It does not require preservation of every historical state and must distinguish natural change from human-driven degradation using appropriate ecological evidence.', 'Maintain viable diversity; protect habitat and ecological interactions; avoid irreversible species or genetic loss; consider composition and function together; evaluate invasive species and fragmentation; monitor ecological change.', 'Biodiversity contributes to ecosystem structure, function, resilience, and evolutionary potential; degradation can reduce ecological options and recovery capacity.', 'biodiversity; integrity', 'Biodiversity contributes to ecosystem structure, function, resilience, and evolutionary potential; degradation can reduce ecological options and recovery capacity.', 'Nature-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived', 'Species diversity; genetic diversity; habitat integrity; ecological interactions; ecosystem function.', 'Habitat conservation; species and population monitoring; ecosystem restoration; agricultural diversity; ecological impact assessment; management of protected and modified landscapes.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Ecology; conservation; agriculture; urban systems', 'The relevant baseline, spatial scale, species or functional groups, and ecological processes must be defined. Protecting species richness alone may miss composition, abundance, genetic diversity, habitat quality, or ecological interactions.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that biodiversity integrity materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0047', 'Soil Regenerative Capacity', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Soil Science; Agriculture; Land Management; Water', 'Soil regenerative capacity is the ability of soil systems to maintain or recover structure, organic matter, biological activity, nutrient cycling, water-holding capacity, and other functions needed for ecological and productive use.', 'Applies to land-management decisions where soil condition affects production, water regulation, carbon cycling, erosion, biodiversity, or ecosystem services. Recovery rates vary with soil type, climate, disturbance, management, and time; soil improvement should be demonstrated rather than assumed.', 'Maintain soil structure; protect organic matter; support biological activity; reduce erosion and degradation; align extraction and disturbance with recovery; monitor physical, chemical, and biological indicators.', 'Soil functions recover when management maintains the processes and conditions supporting aggregation, organic matter, biological activity, and nutrient and water cycling.', 'soil; regenerative; capacity', 'Soil functions recover when management maintains the processes and conditions supporting aggregation, organic matter, biological activity, and nutrient and water cycling.', 'Nature-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived', 'Soil organic matter; erosion; nutrient cycling; soil biology; infiltration; water retention.', 'Soil organic-matter management; cover cropping; crop rotation; reduced disturbance; erosion control; compost or organic-amendment use; restoration of degraded soils.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Agriculture; land management; watershed planning; construction', 'Recovery rates depend on soil type, climate, degradation history, land use, hydrology, and management. Some losses are effectively irreversible on human timescales, and practices that improve one soil property can create other nutrient, water, or greenhouse-gas effects.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that soil regenerative capacity materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0048', 'Water Security and Allocation Equity', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Water Resources; Public Policy; Public Health; Governance', 'Water security and allocation equity require reliable access to adequate water of acceptable quality while recognizing that water allocation decisions distribute benefits, risks, and ecological consequences among users and ecosystems.', 'Applies where water availability, quality, allocation, infrastructure, or governance affects households, agriculture, industry, ecosystems, or future supply. Equity analysis must consider legal rights, essential needs, ecological requirements, seasonal scarcity, affordability, and local institutional arrangements.', 'Protect essential access; allocate within resource limits; include ecological water needs; reduce avoidable losses; make allocation criteria explicit; assess unequal exposure to scarcity and contamination; integrate demand and supply decisions.', 'Water security depends on both physical resource conditions and the institutions that allocate, protect, monitor, and maintain access to water. Distinguishing emphasis: the scarce resource is water, and adequacy, reliability, quality, affordability, ecological requirements, and allocation equity must be considered together.', 'water; security;; allocation; equity', 'Water security depends on both physical resource conditions and the institutions that allocate, protect, monitor, and maintain access to water.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Governance-Derived', 'Water availability; quality; allocation; access; scarcity; ecological flows; affordability.', 'Urban and rural water allocation; drinking-water planning; irrigation scheduling; groundwater abstraction management; drought planning; basin-level allocation and environmental-flow decisions.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Water resources; public health; agriculture; urban systems; governance', 'Adequacy, reliability, quality, affordability, ecological requirements, seasonal variability, and competing users must be considered together. Aggregate water availability does not establish security for particular communities or ecosystems.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that water security and allocation equity materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0049', 'Responsibility for Environmental Burdens', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Environmental Governance; Economics; Law; Engineering', 'Responsibility for environmental burdens holds that actors whose decisions materially create, control, or benefit from environmental risks should bear an appropriate share of the costs of prevention, control, remediation, or management, subject to applicable law and fair allocation.', 'Applies where causal responsibility or control over a burden can be reasonably established. Responsibility may be distributed across supply chains or institutions, and legal liability is not identical to ethical or economic responsibility.', 'Internalize relevant environmental costs; assign responsibility using evidence; prioritize prevention; avoid shifting cleanup costs to uninvolved groups; distinguish polluter-pays, producer responsibility, liability, and general public responsibility.', 'Environmental burdens create obligations for prevention or remediation that should not automatically be externalized to affected communities or the public. Distinguishing emphasis: this is broader responsibility for who bears environmental burdens and who must address them; it does not necessarily require a cost-internalization rule.', 'responsibility; for; environmental; burdens', 'Environmental burdens create obligations for prevention or remediation that should not automatically be externalized to affected communities or the public.', 'Legal-Doctrine-Derived; Governance-Derived; Economic-Principle-Derived', 'Polluter pays; extended producer responsibility; liability; externality internalization; remediation.', 'Environmental liability; producer responsibility; pollution-control obligations; occupational and community exposure management; project impact allocation; remediation responsibility.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Environmental law; economics; waste; industrial regulation', 'Responsibility must be tied to evidence of contribution, control, benefit, or legal duty and to a defined environmental burden. Diffuse, historical, cumulative, and transboundary effects may prevent simple attribution.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that responsibility for environmental burdens materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0050', 'Environmental Additionality', 'ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Environmental Policy; Conservation; Carbon Management; Project Evaluation', 'Environmental additionality asks whether a claimed environmental benefit occurs because of the intervention and would not have occurred, to a materially similar extent, without it. It prevents attributing existing, unavoidable, or independently occurring outcomes to a project.', 'Applies to conservation, restoration, mitigation, offsets, carbon projects, efficiency programmes, and other interventions claiming incremental environmental benefit. Counterfactual conditions must be defined with evidence, and additionality should not be confused with permanence, leakage, or overall environmental quality.', 'Define the baseline or counterfactual; identify incremental change; test whether the intervention caused the change; separate additionality from permanence and leakage; avoid claiming benefits that would occur anyway.', 'A sustainability intervention has stronger evidentiary value when its incremental effect can be distinguished from the baseline trajectory.', 'environmental; additionality', 'A sustainability intervention has stronger evidentiary value when its incremental effect can be distinguished from the baseline trajectory.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Policy-Derived', 'Baseline; counterfactual; incremental benefit; attribution; additionality.', 'Additionality tests in environmental projects; conservation interventions; restoration projects; carbon and ecosystem-service claims; baseline-and-project comparisons.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Environmental projects; conservation; carbon management; programme evaluation', 'A credible baseline or counterfactual is required, and the intervention must be distinguishable from changes that would occur without it. Additionality claims become weak when baseline assumptions are speculative or the intervention merely accelerates an already occurring outcome.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that environmental additionality materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0051', 'Reversibility and Safe-to-Fail Design', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Resilience Engineering; Risk; Infrastructure; Design', 'When uncertainty is material, sustainability can be improved by preferring interventions that can be monitored, corrected, modified, or withdrawn before failures become irreversible, and by designing systems so that localized failure does not produce catastrophic consequences.', 'Applies where uncertainty, novelty, long system lifetimes, or asymmetric consequences make prediction difficult. Reversibility is not always possible or optimal; safety, cost, performance, and ecological consequences must be assessed, and safe-to-fail does not mean accepting preventable harm.', 'Prefer reversible commitments when justified; stage investments; monitor early signals; limit failure propagation; enable shutdown or correction; test recovery pathways; distinguish safe-to-fail from failure by design.', 'Reducing the consequence and irreversibility of errors can improve decision robustness when uncertainty cannot be eliminated.', 'reversibility;; safe-to-fail; design', 'Reducing the consequence and irreversibility of errors can improve decision robustness when uncertainty cannot be eliminated.', 'Engineering-Derived; Risk-Principle-Derived', 'Reversibility; staged deployment; containment; graceful failure; monitoring; recovery.', 'Pilot projects; staged infrastructure deployment; modular systems; floodable or sacrificial design elements; fail-safe controls; reversible land-use or technology decisions under uncertainty.', 'Established evidence base', 'Emerging/Established design approach', 'Infrastructure; technology; environmental management; policy', 'The approach is most relevant where uncertainty or consequence severity justifies retaining options. Reversibility may be impossible for some ecological, social, or infrastructure decisions; local failure containment must not substitute for adequate safety and prevention.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that reversibility and safe-to-fail design materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0052', 'Just Transition', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Labour; Energy Transition; Public Policy; Economics', 'Just transition requires that shifts toward environmentally sustainable systems protect human dignity, livelihoods, participation, social protection, and fair access to emerging opportunities, while addressing the uneven costs of structural change.', 'Applies where decarbonization, automation, resource restrictions, industrial restructuring, environmental regulation, or other sustainability transitions materially affect workers, communities, firms, or regions. It does not require preserving every existing job or technology; it requires fair management of transition impacts and opportunities.', 'Anticipate distributional effects; support affected workers and communities; enable reskilling and alternative livelihoods; involve affected actors in transition design; protect essential services; distribute new opportunities fairly.', 'Environmental and technological transitions change economic and social structures; sustainability requires managing the human consequences of that change rather than treating them as externalities.', 'just; transition', 'Environmental and technological transitions change economic and social structures; sustainability requires managing the human consequences of that change rather than treating them as externalities.', 'Policy-Derived; Human-Wellbeing-Derived', 'Reskilling; social protection; worker participation; regional transition; livelihood diversification.', 'Industrial decarbonization; coal and fossil-fuel transition; automation-related workforce change; regional economic transition; reskilling; social-protection and worker-support measures.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established policy principle', 'Energy; labour; industry; public policy; regional development', 'The affected workers, communities, sectors, time horizon, distribution of costs and benefits, and available transition measures must be identified. A transition cannot be called just solely because the end-state has lower environmental impact.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that just transition materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0053', 'Universal Accessibility and Inclusive Design', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Design; Infrastructure; Human Factors; Technology', 'Universal accessibility and inclusive design seek to make environments, products, services, information, and systems usable by people with diverse physical, sensory, cognitive, linguistic, and situational capabilities without requiring avoidable special adaptation.', 'Applies to physical infrastructure, digital systems, transport, education, public services, workplaces, and technologies where exclusion or additional barriers can materially affect participation or wellbeing. Universal design does not eliminate the need for individualized accommodations where required.', 'Design for diverse users from the outset; remove avoidable barriers; provide multiple interaction modes where justified; test with affected users; preserve dignity and autonomy; distinguish accessibility from convenience.', 'Design choices determine who can access and effectively use a system; inclusive design can expand capability while reducing dependence on after-the-fact adaptation.', 'universal; accessibility;; inclusive; design', 'Design choices determine who can access and effectively use a system; inclusive design can expand capability while reducing dependence on after-the-fact adaptation.', 'Human-Wellbeing-Derived; Design-Derived', 'Accessibility; universal design; assistive technology; inclusive interface; usability.', 'Accessible buildings and public spaces; inclusive transport; assistive technologies; accessible digital services; universal product and interface design; accommodation of diverse user capabilities.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Architecture; engineering; ICT; education; public services', 'Accessibility requirements depend on users, environments, tasks, standards, and foreseeable temporary or permanent limitations. Universal design may not eliminate the need for individualized accommodation, assistive technology, or specialized services.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that universal accessibility and inclusive design materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0054', 'Transparency and Accountability', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Governance; Public Policy; AI; Organizations', 'Transparency and accountability require that relevant decisions, responsibilities, assumptions, evidence, performance, and avenues for review are sufficiently visible and attributable for affected parties to understand, question, and challenge consequential actions.', 'Applies to public institutions, organizations, infrastructure management, sustainability reporting, algorithmic systems, and resource allocation where decisions materially affect people or environmental outcomes. Transparency should be proportionate and must be balanced with legitimate privacy, security, confidentiality, and safety requirements.', 'Make responsibilities identifiable; disclose material assumptions and evidence; enable audit and review; provide reasons for consequential decisions; track outcomes; establish corrective mechanisms; avoid disclosure that creates disproportionate privacy or security risk.', 'Accountability mechanisms connect decisions and actions to identifiable responsibilities, evidence, review, and corrective action. Distinguishing emphasis: transparency is coupled with answerability, responsibility, or accountability for performance or decisions.', 'transparency;; accountability', 'Accountability mechanisms connect decisions and actions to identifiable responsibilities, evidence, review, and corrective action.', 'Governance-Derived; Legal-Doctrine-Derived', 'Auditability; explainability; reporting; oversight; grievance mechanisms; responsibility.', 'Environmental reporting; public project appraisal; procurement records; impact-assessment disclosure; sustainability performance reporting; auditable institutional decision processes.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Governance; public policy; AI; sustainability reporting', 'Information must be sufficiently complete, understandable, timely, and attributable to support scrutiny and correction. Privacy, security, confidentiality, and intellectual-property constraints may legitimately limit disclosure, but they do not remove the need for accountable decision-making.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that transparency and accountability materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0055', 'Local Capacity and Maintainability', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY', 'Infrastructure; Operations; Community Systems; Engineering', 'Sustainability is strengthened when the people, skills, institutions, tools, spare parts, information, and financial arrangements required to operate and maintain a system are realistically available at the scale where the system depends on them.', 'Applies to infrastructure, decentralized technologies, community-managed resources, digital systems, public services, and facilities whose performance depends on continuing operation and maintenance. Local capacity does not require that every capability be local; external support can be sustainable when it is reliable, affordable, and institutionally appropriate.', 'Design for maintainability; match technology to available capability; preserve manuals and technical knowledge; ensure spare-part and service pathways; train operators; budget lifecycle maintenance; test institutional continuity.', 'A system that cannot be operated, repaired, monitored, or governed with available capabilities may fail despite strong initial technical performance. Distinguishing emphasis: the central test is whether the local people, skills, tools, spare parts, finance, information, and institutions needed for operation and maintenance are realistically available.', 'local; capacity;; maintainability', 'A system that cannot be operated, repaired, monitored, or governed with available capabilities may fail despite strong initial technical performance.', 'Engineering-Derived; Community-Practice-Derived', 'Maintainability; local skills; operator capacity; spare parts; lifecycle support; institutional capacity.', 'Community-scale water and sanitation; decentralized energy; rural infrastructure; maintainable public assets; locally serviceable equipment; spare-parts and technician availability.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Infrastructure; water; energy; campuses; community systems; ICT', 'The required skills, tools, spare parts, financing, documentation, institutional authority, and supply routes must exist or be realistically developable. Local deployment is unsustainable if operation depends on unavailable specialist support or unreliable replacement chains.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that local capacity and maintainability materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0056', 'Knowledge Continuity and Transfer', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Education; Organizations; Engineering; Traditional Knowledge', 'Knowledge continuity preserves the information, skills, practices, rationale, and institutional memory needed to understand, operate, maintain, adapt, or replace systems over time. Sustainability can be weakened when essential knowledge disappears even though physical assets remain.', 'Applies where system performance depends on tacit knowledge, specialized skills, historical records, maintenance procedures, local ecological knowledge, design rationale, or institutional memory. Preservation should include appropriate provenance, access controls, cultural rights, and mechanisms for updating obsolete knowledge.', 'Document critical knowledge; transfer tacit skills; preserve design rationale; maintain institutional memory; support intergenerational learning; distinguish preservation from uncritical replication; update knowledge as conditions change.', 'Continuity of capability depends partly on continuity of the knowledge required to operate, interpret, maintain, and adapt a system.', 'knowledge; continuity;; transfer', 'Continuity of capability depends partly on continuity of the knowledge required to operate, interpret, maintain, and adapt a system.', 'Human-Wellbeing-Derived; Historical-Practice-Derived; Education-Derived', 'Tacit knowledge; documentation; apprenticeship; institutional memory; knowledge transfer.', 'Operations manuals; maintenance records; apprenticeship and skill transfer; institutional memory; traditional knowledge documentation; handover procedures for long-lived infrastructure and systems.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Engineering; education; organizations; traditional knowledge; public services', 'The knowledge that is critical to operation, maintenance, adaptation, and replacement must be identified and preserved in usable form. Documentation alone is insufficient where practical skills, tacit knowledge, or institutional authority are essential.', 'Use SCR_0090 for knowledge/skill continuity recommendations; retain SCR_0056 only for historical traceability.', 'Deprecated — merged into SCR_0090'), ('SCR_0057', 'Interoperability for Sustainable Transition', 'SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'ICT; Infrastructure; Engineering; Standards', 'Interoperability for sustainable transition is the capacity of systems, components, data, and institutions to exchange or use information and functions through compatible interfaces and standards, reducing unnecessary lock-in and enabling incremental change.', 'Applies where long-lived systems must interact with new technologies, organizations, data sources, or infrastructure. Interoperability should be based on relevant standards and real functional requirements; excessive compatibility can add complexity, security risk, or resource use.', 'Use open or well-supported interfaces where appropriate; preserve data portability; avoid unnecessary vendor or technology lock-in; separate stable interfaces from replaceable components; assess security and lifecycle complexity.', 'Compatible interfaces can allow parts of a system to be replaced or upgraded without discarding the entire system, preserving existing value and future options.', 'interoperability; for; sustainable; transition', 'Compatible interfaces can allow parts of a system to be replaced or upgraded without discarding the entire system, preserving existing value and future options.', 'Engineering-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'Open standards; APIs; modular interfaces; data portability; system compatibility.', 'Open technical standards; interoperable energy, water, building, and digital systems; modular equipment replacement; data exchange across organizations; avoidance of unnecessary technology lock-in.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'ICT; infrastructure; manufacturing; smart campuses; cyber-physical systems', 'Interoperability requires compatible interfaces, data semantics, standards, or contractual arrangements that remain usable across relevant system changes. Open interfaces can introduce security, governance, or integration costs and do not by themselves guarantee longer system life.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that interoperability for sustainable transition materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0058', 'Adaptive Modularity', 'SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Engineering Design; Architecture; Infrastructure; Technology', 'Adaptive modularity organizes systems into sufficiently separable components or functions so that parts can be modified, replaced, scaled, or reconfigured as conditions change without unnecessary replacement of the whole system.', 'Applies where uncertainty, maintenance, technological change, or variable demand makes future modification valuable. Modularity introduces interfaces, materials, and coordination requirements; excessive modularity can reduce efficiency or increase complexity.', 'Separate functions where justified; make interfaces explicit; replace or upgrade components independently; support phased capacity; design for inspection and repair; evaluate interface and coordination costs.', 'Modularity can preserve useful system value by allowing localized change instead of whole-system replacement.', 'adaptive; modularity', 'Modularity can preserve useful system value by allowing localized change instead of whole-system replacement.', 'Engineering-Derived; Design-Derived', 'Modularity; reconfiguration; component replacement; phased capacity; maintainability.', 'Modular buildings; replaceable infrastructure components; scalable energy and water systems; modular computing and manufacturing systems; staged upgrades instead of whole-system replacement.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Engineering; architecture; infrastructure; manufacturing; computing', 'Modules must have meaningful functional boundaries and feasible interfaces; excessive modularity can increase material use, cost, complexity, and failure points. Reconfiguration should provide a credible advantage over replacing the complete system.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that adaptive modularity materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0059', 'Cumulative Impact Awareness', 'ECOLOGY; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Environmental Assessment; Planning; Ecology; Public Policy', 'Cumulative impact awareness recognizes that individually small interventions can interact across space or time and produce material combined effects on ecological systems, communities, resources, or infrastructure.', 'Applies where multiple projects, activities, pollutants, withdrawals, land-use changes, or social pressures affect overlapping receptors or system capacities. Cumulative assessment requires attention to interaction, timing, spatial scale, baseline condition, and other reasonably attributable contributors.', 'Consider multiple pressures; define shared receptors and system capacities; account for temporal accumulation; identify interaction effects; avoid evaluating projects only in isolation; distinguish cumulative effects from unrelated background variation.', 'System response can depend on the combined load and sequence of multiple interventions rather than on the effect of any one intervention considered independently.', 'cumulative; impact; awareness', 'System response can depend on the combined load and sequence of multiple interventions rather than on the effect of any one intervention considered independently.', 'Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Policy-Derived', 'Cumulative effects; carrying capacity; multiple stressors; combined exposure; spatial interaction.', 'Cumulative environmental assessment; watershed and airshed planning; multiple-project impact assessment; urban growth analysis; repeated extraction or disturbance over time.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Environmental assessment; urban planning; water; climate; infrastructure', 'Assessment must define spatial and temporal boundaries, interacting stressors, relevant receptors, and a credible baseline. Small individual effects should not be assumed cumulative without evidence of interaction or aggregation.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that cumulative impact awareness materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0060', 'Ecological Regeneration and Restoration', 'ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Ecological Restoration; Conservation; Landscape Management', 'Ecological regeneration and restoration focus on recovering ecological structure, processes, functions, or trajectories after degradation, disturbance, or loss, while recognizing that restoration may produce a functioning future state rather than recreate an exact historical condition.', 'Applies where ecological systems have been degraded and recovery is feasible and appropriate. Restoration goals should reflect reference conditions, current constraints, future climate, ecological trajectories, and social context; some systems may require protection rather than active restoration.', 'Protect remaining intact function; remove or reduce degradation drivers; restore ecological processes; set measurable recovery objectives; use native or ecologically appropriate components where justified; monitor recovery and adapt management.', 'Ecological condition can improve when the drivers of degradation are reduced and the processes required for recovery are restored or supported.', 'ecological; regeneration;; restoration', 'Ecological condition can improve when the drivers of degradation are reduced and the processes required for recovery are restored or supported.', 'Nature-Derived; Scientific-Knowledge-Derived; Contemporary-Practice-Derived', 'Restoration; regeneration; recovery trajectory; habitat rehabilitation; ecological function.', 'Wetland restoration; forest and mangrove recovery; river and habitat rehabilitation; soil restoration; mine-site reclamation; assisted natural regeneration.', 'Established evidence base', 'Established', 'Ecology; watershed management; agriculture; urban restoration; conservation', 'The desired ecological functions, reference conditions, degradation mechanisms, and recovery trajectory must be defined. Restoration may produce a different but functioning ecosystem rather than reproduce a historical state, and recovery can be limited by irreversible thresholds.', 'Candidate discovery when the application context contains evidence that ecological regeneration and restoration materially affects the sustainability outcome; validate the mechanism, scope, boundary conditions, and evidence before ranking.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0061', 'Alternative Material Selection for Sustainability', 'SYSTEMS', 'Materials; Design; Circular Economy', 'Alternative material selection for sustainability is the deliberate comparison and selection of materials according to their functional performance together with their environmental, social, health, resource, and life-cycle implications. It treats material choice as a sustainability decision rather than only a technical or economic selection, while recognizing that the preferred material depends on the intended service, durability, maintenance, end-of-life pathway, availability, and consequences across the life cycle.', 'Applies wherever two or more materials can satisfy the required function and their life-cycle consequences differ. Assessment may consider embodied energy and emissions, resource criticality, toxicity, durability, repairability, recyclability or reuse potential, local availability, manufacturing requirements, maintenance, and end-of-life. A material is not sustainable merely because it is renewable, recycled, natural, or locally available; functional adequacy and whole-life consequences must remain part of the decision.', 'Compare materials on function and life-cycle consequences; avoid single-attribute selection; consider durability and maintenance; account for material scarcity and toxicity; evaluate end-of-life pathways; include context-specific environmental and social effects; prefer evidence-based multi-criteria selection.', 'Sustainability-oriented material choice should satisfy the required function while reducing significant life-cycle burdens and preserving future material options. Distinguishing emphasis: the decision variable is material choice or substitution based on life-cycle sustainability consequences.', 'Alternative material selection; sustainable materials; material substitution; life-cycle material choice', 'Sustainability-oriented material choice should satisfy the required function while reducing significant life-cycle burdens and preserving future material options.', 'Life-Cycle; Resource Stewardship; Circularity', 'Material properties and material supply chains determine resource use, embodied impacts, service life, maintenance needs, and recovery possibilities.', 'Material-selection decisions in buildings, infrastructure, manufacturing, packaging, products, and equipment where competing materials provide similar functions but differ in embodied impacts, toxicity, durability, scarcity, recyclability, or supply risk.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Material alternatives must first satisfy the required function, safety, durability, regulatory, and performance criteria. Environmental comparison should use a consistent functional basis and account for extraction, processing, transport, use, maintenance, and end-of-life effects where material.', 'Candidate when a decision includes alternative materials, material substitution, life-cycle assessment, embodied impacts, durability, toxicity, scarcity, or end-of-life considerations.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0062', 'Material Efficiency', 'SYSTEMS', 'Materials; Resource Efficiency; Circular Economy', 'Material efficiency is the provision of a required function or service with reduced use of virgin material and reduced material loss, while maintaining required performance, safety, durability, and service quality. It concerns both reducing material demand and increasing the useful service obtained from materials already extracted.', 'Applies to products, buildings, infrastructure, manufacturing, packaging, and other systems where material inputs can be reduced through efficient design, lightweighting, durability, reuse, repair, remanufacture, higher utilization, or improved process yield. Material efficiency must not be interpreted as simply minimizing mass when doing so shortens service life, increases failure risk, or shifts burdens elsewhere.', 'Provide more service from less material; reduce avoidable material loss; design for durability and high utilization; retain material value; distinguish mass reduction from whole-life efficiency; evaluate rebound and burden-shifting effects.', 'Material efficiency seeks to reduce virgin material demand and losses per unit of useful service delivered.', 'material efficiency; dematerialization; material productivity; resource efficiency', 'Material efficiency seeks to reduce virgin material demand and losses per unit of useful service delivered.', 'Resource Efficiency; Life-Cycle; Circular Economy', 'Design, manufacturing, use, and recovery decisions influence the amount of material required per unit of service and the duration for which that material remains useful.', 'Lightweight structural design; material yield improvement; near-net-shape manufacturing; waste-reducing fabrication; dimensional optimization; reduced material intensity per unit of service.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Reduced material quantity is beneficial only when required performance, safety, durability, repairability, and service life are maintained. Lightweighting or yield improvement can shift burdens to energy use, maintenance, replacement frequency, or difficult-to-recycle material combinations.', 'Candidate when material quantities, yield losses, lightweighting, resource productivity, or service-per-material comparisons are present.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0063', 'Design for Sustainability', 'SYSTEMS', 'Design; Engineering; Product Development; Infrastructure', 'Design for sustainability is the deliberate incorporation of environmental, social, economic, and long-term system considerations into design decisions so that a product, service, building, infrastructure system, or technology meets its intended function with reduced life-cycle burdens and improved resilience, usability, and future adaptability.', 'Applies from problem definition and requirements engineering through concept generation, material and process selection, detailed design, deployment, use, maintenance, and end-of-life. It is broader than environmental design alone and does not prescribe one design method; the relevant sustainability criteria depend on the system, users, life cycle, context, and evidence available.', 'Define the function before optimizing the artifact; consider the full life cycle; avoid burden shifting; design for durability, repair, reuse, recovery, and adaptation where relevant; include human needs and safety; test sustainability criteria alongside conventional performance requirements.', 'Sustainability should be treated as a design requirement affecting the specification, architecture, materials, operation, maintenance, and end-of-life of a system. Distinguishing emphasis: sustainability requirements are integrated into the broader design process across functions, materials, manufacture, use, maintenance, and end-of-life rather than being limited to material substitution.', 'design for sustainability; sustainable design; eco-design; life-cycle design', 'Sustainability should be treated as a design requirement affecting the specification, architecture, materials, operation, maintenance, and end-of-life of a system.', 'Life-Cycle; Design; Systems Thinking', 'Design choices establish many downstream resource, maintenance, use-phase, safety, and end-of-life consequences.', 'Sustainable product development; building and infrastructure design; service-system design; engineering design reviews; design requirements that include energy, material, environmental, social, maintenance, and end-of-life considerations.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'The design boundary must include the functions and life-cycle stages materially affected by design choices. Sustainability criteria should not override mandatory safety, accessibility, performance, legal, or user requirements, and trade-offs must remain explicit.', 'Candidate when a design or development decision can influence life-cycle environmental, social, resource, safety, or adaptability outcomes.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0064', 'Design for Repair and Maintenance', 'SYSTEMS', 'Design; Asset Management; Circular Economy', 'Design for repair and maintenance is the principle of making products, equipment, buildings, and infrastructure capable of being inspected, serviced, repaired, maintained, and restored with reasonable access, information, tools, skills, time, and replacement components. It treats maintainability as a design property that can extend useful life and preserve function.', 'Applies where continued service depends on periodic inspection, servicing, component replacement, fault correction, or restoration. It includes accessibility, standardization, diagnostic information, replaceable components, maintenance procedures, and availability of parts or skills. It does not imply that every component should be repairable regardless of safety, contamination, cost, or technical feasibility.', 'Provide access to maintainable components; enable diagnosis; use replaceable or serviceable parts where appropriate; preserve maintenance information; support safe repair; consider local repair capability; evaluate maintenance burden over the life cycle.', 'Systems designed for repair and maintenance can retain useful function longer and reduce premature replacement and associated resource demand. Distinguishing emphasis: the primary mechanism is a design property that makes inspection, servicing, repair, and maintenance practicable; actual maintenance activity is not required at the design stage.', 'repairability; maintainability; serviceability; preventive maintenance; right to repair', 'Systems designed for repair and maintenance can retain useful function longer and reduce premature replacement and associated resource demand.', 'Circularity; Long-Life Use; Asset Stewardship', 'Physical architecture, component accessibility, diagnostics, spare-part availability, and maintenance capability determine whether repair can practically extend service life.', 'Replaceable wear parts; access panels; standard fasteners; diagnostic access; maintenance clearances; service documentation; maintainable buildings, machinery, electronics, infrastructure, and public assets.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Repairability depends on access, diagnostic information, compatible parts, tools, skills, safety, and reasonable repair time and cost. Repair is not preferable when the asset has an unsafe failure mode, severe degradation, obsolete functionality, or a replacement with substantially lower total impact is demonstrably available.', 'Candidate when repair, servicing, inspection, maintenance access, spare parts, diagnostics, or service-life extension are relevant.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0065', 'Design for Disassembly and Recovery', 'SYSTEMS', 'Design; Circular Economy; Manufacturing; Construction', 'Design for disassembly and recovery is the principle of configuring products, assemblies, buildings, and infrastructure so that components and materials can be separated at end of use or during major maintenance with feasible effort, damage, contamination control, and identification, enabling reuse, refurbishment, remanufacture, or material recovery.', 'Applies where components or materials have residual value after an initial service period. Relevant considerations include accessible connections, reversible joining where appropriate, material identification, separation of incompatible materials, modular assemblies, and recovery pathways. Disassembly should not compromise required structural integrity, safety, durability, or operational performance.', 'Preserve separability; identify materials and components; favor reversible connections where appropriate; reduce contamination; enable component recovery; design recovery pathways before deployment; balance recovery against safety and performance.', 'Products and built systems should preserve the possibility of recovering useful components and materials rather than making recovery unnecessarily difficult.', 'design for disassembly; design for recovery; deconstruction; material recovery', 'Products and built systems should preserve the possibility of recovering useful components and materials rather than making recovery unnecessarily difficult.', 'Circularity; Design; Residual Resource Value', 'Connection methods, material combinations, identification, contamination, and access determine the technical feasibility and cost of recovery.', 'Reversible joints; material separation; component identification; modular assemblies; selective demolition; design for component recovery and recycling at maintenance or end-of-life.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Disassembly must be technically feasible at the relevant maintenance or end-of-life stage and should preserve component or material value. Fastener choice, adhesives, contamination, mixed materials, access, labour, safety, and available recovery infrastructure can determine whether the design actually enables recovery.', 'Candidate when products, structures, assemblies, or equipment may be disassembled for component reuse, refurbishment, remanufacture, or material recovery.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0066', 'Reuse Before Replacement', 'SYSTEMS', 'Circular Economy; Resource Management; Product Systems', 'Reuse before replacement is the sustainability principle that an existing product, component, material, structure, or asset should be considered for continued use in its current or adapted form before new production or disposal is selected, when reuse can meet functional, safety, health, and regulatory requirements.', 'Applies to products, components, building materials, equipment, packaging, infrastructure elements, and other assets with remaining functional value. Reuse may involve direct reuse, adaptation, relocation, or repurposing. It is not automatically preferable when inspection reveals unsafe degradation, contamination, excessive adaptation requirements, or a demonstrably greater whole-life burden.', 'Check existing assets before procuring new ones; assess remaining function; inspect and certify safety; adapt where justified; preserve component value; compare reuse against replacement using whole-life evidence; avoid premature disposal.', 'Retaining and reusing an existing functional asset can avoid the resource extraction, manufacturing, transport, and waste associated with producing a replacement.', 'reuse; direct reuse; adaptive reuse; reuse before replacement; asset retention', 'Retaining and reusing an existing functional asset can avoid the resource extraction, manufacturing, transport, and waste associated with producing a replacement.', 'Residual Resource Value; Long-Life Use; Functional Sufficiency', 'Reuse preserves an existing stock of embodied material and function, reducing the need for additional production when the existing asset remains suitable.', 'Reuse of buildings and structures; second-life equipment; component reuse; reclaimed construction materials; reuse of containers and packaging; adaptation of existing assets instead of unnecessary new production.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Reuse should be considered when the existing item can safely deliver the required function, directly or after proportionate adaptation. Reuse can be inappropriate when condition, contamination, regulatory requirements, performance, or life-cycle impacts make continued use unreasonable.', 'Candidate when an existing asset, component, product, structure, or material could satisfy a need before new procurement or replacement.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0067', 'Refurbishment and Remanufacturing', 'SYSTEMS', 'Circular Economy; Manufacturing; Asset Management', 'Refurbishment and remanufacturing preserve and restore the value of used products or components by inspecting, repairing, replacing, upgrading, or requalifying them for continued service. The principle recognizes that retaining an existing product or component can preserve material, manufacturing effort, and embedded function compared with producing an equivalent new item.', 'Applies where used products or components can be safely recovered and returned to an acceptable functional state. Refurbishment may involve partial restoration, while remanufacturing generally involves a controlled process intended to return a product or component to specified performance. Neither should be treated as preferable without considering energy, transport, testing, reliability, safety, and market or regulatory requirements.', 'Recover useful products and components; diagnose remaining value; restore function safely; replace only what is necessary; retain embodied materials and manufacturing effort; verify performance after restoration; compare restoration with new production on a whole-life basis.', 'Restoring used products or components can retain a greater share of their embodied material and functional value than discarding them and manufacturing replacements.', 'refurbishment; remanufacturing; restoration; product life extension', 'Restoring used products or components can retain a greater share of their embodied material and functional value than discarding them and manufacturing replacements.', 'Reuse Before Replacement; Residual Resource Value; Life-Cycle Responsibility', 'Inspection, repair, component replacement, testing, and requalification determine whether used assets can be returned to reliable service.', 'Industrial remanufacturing; equipment refurbishment; engine and machine rebuilding; electronics refurbishment; building-system renewal; requalification of used components for continued service.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The used product or component must have sufficient residual technical value and a feasible route for inspection, disassembly, repair, replacement, testing, and requalification. Energy, replacement-part requirements, warranty, safety, and resulting service life must be considered.', 'Candidate when used products or components can be restored, upgraded, requalified, or returned to service instead of being discarded.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0068', 'Cascading Use of Materials', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY', 'Circular Economy; Biomass; Materials', 'Cascading use of materials is the principle of directing a material or biomass resource through successive uses that preserve useful value before final disposal or irreversible degradation, particularly when different applications can extract different levels of functional value from the same resource.', 'Applies where a material can serve multiple sequential functions and where the sequence reduces virgin resource demand or disposal. The appropriate cascade depends on material properties, contamination, energy requirements, competing uses, and recovery infrastructure. Cascading should not be used to justify additional extraction merely because eventual reuse is possible.', 'Retain material function through successive uses; prioritize higher-value uses where justified; delay irreversible disposal; manage contamination; compare cascading pathways with direct recycling, recovery, or ecological return; account for energy and transport requirements.', 'Materials with remaining functional value should, where feasible, be directed through successive uses before their value is finally lost.', 'cascading use; cascade principle; biomass cascade; sequential use', 'Materials with remaining functional value should, where feasible, be directed through successive uses before their value is finally lost.', 'Material Circular Cycling; Residual Resource Value; Resource Regeneration Capacity', 'Material properties and quality degrade or change across use cycles, determining which subsequent applications remain feasible.', 'Sequential use of wood, biomass, fibres, plastics, metals, nutrients, or other materials in applications that progressively extract value before final disposal or irreversible degradation.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Successive uses should retain material value and avoid creating hazardous or technically unrecoverable residues. The sequence depends on material quality, contamination, market or process demand, transport, processing energy, and the feasibility of recovery at each stage.', 'Candidate when materials or biomass can provide successive useful functions before recycling, recovery, disposal, or ecological return.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0069', 'Waste Prevention at Source', 'SYSTEMS', 'Waste Management; Resource Efficiency; Circular Economy', 'Waste prevention at source is the principle of avoiding the generation of waste before it occurs by reducing unnecessary material inputs, losses, defects, overproduction, packaging, hazardous substances, and other activities that create waste requiring subsequent treatment or disposal.', 'Applies across product design, manufacturing, construction, procurement, food systems, laboratories, offices, and service delivery. Prevention is distinct from recycling or treatment because it acts before waste is generated. Measures must not merely transfer waste to another medium or increase other environmental or social burdens.', 'Prevent generation before treatment; identify process losses; reduce defects and overproduction; avoid unnecessary packaging and material use; design processes around yield and demand; distinguish prevention from downstream waste management.', 'The most direct way to reduce waste burden is to avoid generating unnecessary waste at the source.', 'waste prevention; source reduction; waste minimization; prevention hierarchy', 'The most direct way to reduce waste burden is to avoid generating unnecessary waste at the source.', 'Loss Prevention Before Supply Expansion; Material Efficiency; Functional Sufficiency', 'Process design, demand, material selection, quality control, and operating practices determine how much waste is generated before recovery or disposal.', 'Process-yield improvement; defect reduction; right-sizing; overproduction control; packaging reduction; inventory-loss prevention; cleaner production; elimination of avoidable material inputs.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Prevention must address the process or decision that generates the waste rather than merely improve downstream treatment. Reducing waste must not transfer environmental burdens to product failure, unsafe operation, excessive resource use, or hidden waste elsewhere.', 'Candidate when the problem involves avoidable waste, defects, overproduction, unnecessary packaging, process losses, or upstream waste prevention.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0070', 'By-product and Industrial Symbiosis', 'SYSTEMS', 'Industry; Circular Economy; Resource Management', 'By-product and industrial symbiosis is the principle of treating outputs from one process that have usable value as potential inputs to another process, where technically, economically, environmentally, and legally feasible. It seeks to replace some virgin inputs and reduce disposal by coordinating material, energy, water, or other resource exchanges among processes or organizations.', 'Applies where one process produces a consistent and suitable secondary output and another process has a compatible input requirement. Feasibility depends on quantity, quality, timing, location, contamination, safety, regulation, infrastructure, and transaction costs. Not every waste stream is a suitable secondary resource.', 'Identify compatible input-output relationships; characterize quality and contamination; coordinate timing and logistics; verify regulatory and safety requirements; assess substitution of virgin inputs; evaluate the whole network rather than isolated exchanges.', 'Resource outputs that retain usable function can become inputs to another process, reducing both virgin resource demand and disposal requirements. Distinguishing emphasis: a real cross-process exchange is required in which a by-product or secondary resource substitutes for another process input under compatibility constraints.', 'industrial symbiosis; by-product exchange; resource exchange; industrial ecology', 'Resource outputs that retain usable function can become inputs to another process, reducing both virgin resource demand and disposal requirements.', 'Cross-Boundary Resource Exchange; Residual Resource Value; Material Circular Cycling', 'Material and energy flows between organizations create opportunities for substitution when output quality, quantity, timing, and infrastructure are compatible.', 'Exchange of heat, water, materials, by-products, or other process outputs between facilities or operations where one process output can satisfy another process input requirement.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'A proposed exchange requires compatible quantity, quality, timing, location, process requirements, contamination limits, safety, regulation, infrastructure, and economic feasibility. A nominal by-product is not a usable secondary resource without a real receiving use.', 'Candidate when one process produces a by-product or secondary resource that could substitute for another process input.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0071', 'Sustainable Procurement and Supplier Responsibility', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE', 'Procurement; Governance; Supply Chains', 'Sustainable procurement and supplier responsible management is the principle of incorporating environmental, social, ethical, life-cycle, and resilience considerations into purchasing and supplier-management decisions rather than evaluating suppliers solely on initial price, specification compliance, or delivery.', 'Applies to public and private procurement of materials, products, services, construction, equipment, and digital infrastructure. Criteria should be proportionate to the purchase and supported by evidence. Procurement requirements should not impose unverifiable claims, create unjustified barriers for smaller suppliers, or ignore safety, performance, affordability, and legal obligations.', 'Define sustainability criteria before procurement; consider whole-life value; assess supplier practices and risks; verify claims; include durability, repair, resource and social criteria where relevant; monitor supplier performance; avoid lowest-price-only decisions when they create greater whole-life cost or burden.', 'Purchasing decisions influence what is produced, how it is produced, and which environmental and social practices are rewarded across supply chains.', 'sustainable procurement; responsible sourcing; supplier responsible management; green procurement', 'Purchasing decisions influence what is produced, how it is produced, and which environmental and social practices are rewarded across supply chains.', 'Life-Cycle Responsibility; Transparency and Accountability; Responsibility for Environmental Burdens', 'Procurement specifications, supplier selection, contract conditions, verification, and performance monitoring transmit sustainability requirements through value chains.', 'Procurement specifications; supplier evaluation; environmental and social criteria in tenders; life-cycle requirements; traceability; supplier performance monitoring; responsible sourcing requirements.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Criteria should be material to the purchase, proportionate to risk, verifiable, and compatible with legal, technical, safety, affordability, and competition requirements. Supplier claims require appropriate evidence, and responsibility should reflect the buyer’s actual influence over the supply chain.', 'Candidate when purchasing or supplier selection can incorporate life-cycle, environmental, social, ethical, durability, or resource criteria.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0072', 'Preventive Maintenance and Asset Life Extension', 'SYSTEMS', 'Asset Management; Infrastructure; Operations', 'Preventive maintenance and asset life extension is the principle of maintaining assets proactively according to condition, risk, operating requirements, or appropriate maintenance intervals so that function, safety, reliability, and service life are preserved and avoidable premature replacement is reduced.', 'Applies to infrastructure, machinery, buildings, vehicles, energy systems, water systems, digital infrastructure, and other assets where deterioration can be monitored or managed. Maintenance should be risk- and condition-informed rather than indiscriminately scheduled; excessive maintenance can also consume resources without corresponding benefit.', 'Maintain before failure where justified; inspect condition; prioritize high-consequence deterioration; preserve critical components; use maintenance data; distinguish preventive from corrective maintenance; evaluate maintenance resource use against avoided failures and replacement.', 'Timely maintenance can preserve existing assets, reduce failure and replacement demand, and maintain service with fewer additional material and energy inputs. Distinguishing emphasis: the sustainability mechanism is proactive maintenance based on condition, risk, or intervals to preserve safety, reliability, and service life; it is distinct from designing an asset to be repairable.', 'preventive maintenance; predictive maintenance; asset life extension; condition-based maintenance', 'Timely maintenance can preserve existing assets, reduce failure and replacement demand, and maintain service with fewer additional material and energy inputs.', 'Design for Repair and Maintenance; Long-Life Use; Local Capacity and Maintainability', 'Deterioration mechanisms, inspection, condition monitoring, maintenance intervals, and repair capability determine whether service life can be safely extended.', 'Condition monitoring; scheduled inspection; lubrication and servicing; preventive replacement of failure-prone parts; rehabilitation; calibration; maintenance planning for buildings, equipment, utilities, vehicles, and digital infrastructure.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Maintenance intervals and interventions should be based on failure modes, condition, risk, operating environment, manufacturer requirements, or other credible evidence. Excessive maintenance can waste resources, while insufficient maintenance can shorten life or create safety and reliability risks.', 'Candidate when asset condition, maintenance intervals, failure risk, deterioration, or premature replacement are relevant.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0073', 'Demand Management Before Capacity Expansion', 'SYSTEMS', 'Resource Management; Infrastructure; Energy; Water', 'Demand management before capacity expansion is the principle of first examining whether avoidable or inefficient demand can be reduced, shifted, or better managed before increasing supply or physical capacity. It treats demand as a variable that can often be influenced through efficiency, behaviour, scheduling, pricing, design, or operational measures.', 'Applies to water, energy, transport, buildings, computing, telecommunications, infrastructure, and other systems where capacity expansion has material, financial, ecological, or social consequences. Demand reduction is not automatically preferable where it compromises essential services, health, dignity, safety, or equitable access.', 'Measure demand before forecasting expansion; identify avoidable demand; improve efficiency; shift flexible demand; protect essential access; compare demand-side and supply-side options using whole-life impacts and equity considerations.', 'When additional capacity has significant resource or environmental consequences, reducing avoidable demand can provide the required service with lower expansion pressure. Distinguishing emphasis: the primary intervention is broader demand-side reduction, efficiency, scheduling, shifting, pricing, behaviour, or service management before capacity expansion; loss reduction may be one component but is not the whole concept.', 'demand management; demand-side management; efficiency before expansion; capacity avoidance', 'When additional capacity has significant resource or environmental consequences, reducing avoidable demand can provide the required service with lower expansion pressure.', 'Loss Prevention Before Supply Expansion; Functional Sufficiency; Demand Flexibility and Load Shifting', 'Demand patterns, efficiency, user behaviour, system losses, scheduling, and service requirements determine whether demand-side measures can defer or reduce capacity expansion.', 'Water-demand management before new supply; energy-efficiency measures before generation expansion; transport-demand management before road expansion; building-load reduction before HVAC capacity increase; computing optimization before adding infrastructure.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Demand measures should be evaluated against service requirements, rebound effects, equity, reliability, and feasibility. Capacity expansion remains appropriate when demand cannot reasonably be reduced or when expansion is required for safety, health, resilience, or equitable access.', 'Candidate when additional capacity is being considered and demand reduction, efficiency, load shifting, or loss reduction could defer expansion.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0074', 'Resource Allocation by Need and Impact', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Management; Public Policy; Resource Governance', 'Resource allocation by need and impact is the principle of allocating scarce resources using explicit consideration of need, expected benefit, risk, distributional effects, and sustainability consequences rather than relying solely on aggregate demand, purchasing power, historical allocation, or convenience.', 'Applies to public services, infrastructure investment, budgets, water, energy, health-related resources, education, emergency response, and organizational resource allocation. Criteria must be transparent and context-specific. Equity does not necessarily require identical allocation; it may require differentiated allocation to address unequal needs or exposure.', 'Make allocation criteria explicit; identify essential needs; compare expected impacts; account for unequal burdens and benefits; protect vulnerable groups; disclose trade-offs; review allocation as evidence changes.', 'Sustainable resource allocation should account for who needs a resource, who bears the consequences, and what outcomes the allocation is expected to produce. Distinguishing emphasis: applies to allocation of any scarce resource, service, budget, or infrastructure using explicit need, benefit, risk, or distributional-impact criteria.', 'equitable allocation; needs-based allocation; impact-based allocation; resource governance', 'Sustainable resource allocation should account for who needs a resource, who bears the consequences, and what outcomes the allocation is expected to produce.', 'Equitable Sustainability; Human Capability and Dignity; Transparency and Accountability', 'Allocation rules determine the distribution of scarce resources, benefits, risks, and opportunities across people and places.', 'Public-budget allocation; water allocation during scarcity; energy and infrastructure investment; emergency-resource prioritization; service allocation based on need, expected benefit, risk, and distributional consequences.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Allocation criteria must be explicit and relevant to the decision, with attention to unequal need, vulnerability, expected benefit, risk, and distributional effects. The principle does not prescribe equal shares and does not justify opaque or discriminatory allocation.', 'Candidate when scarce resources, services, budgets, or infrastructure must be allocated among users or locations with differing needs, risks, or impacts.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0075', 'Whole-Life Cost and Value Assessment', 'SYSTEMS', 'Economics; Engineering Management; Procurement', 'Whole-life cost and value assessment is the principle of evaluating alternatives over their relevant life cycle rather than comparing only initial acquisition cost. It considers costs and benefits associated with acquisition, operation, energy and resource use, maintenance, downtime, repair, replacement, residual value, and end-of-life, together with material sustainability consequences where appropriate.', 'Applies to investment, procurement, design, infrastructure, equipment, buildings, and service decisions with significant operating or end-of-life consequences. It does not reduce sustainability to monetary valuation; environmental and social effects may require separate metrics or multi-criteria assessment when they cannot be credibly monetized.', 'Look beyond purchase price; include operating and maintenance costs; account for replacement and residual value; compare alternatives over a consistent period; test uncertainty; keep monetary and non-monetary sustainability criteria explicit.', 'An alternative with a higher initial cost may provide greater long-term value and lower resource burden when operating, maintenance, replacement, and end-of-life consequences are considered.', 'life-cycle costing; whole-life cost; total cost of ownership; life-cycle value', 'An alternative with a higher initial cost may provide greater long-term value and lower resource burden when operating, maintenance, replacement, and end-of-life consequences are considered.', 'Metric-Aware Optimization; Life-Cycle Responsibility; Alternative Material Selection for Sustainability', 'Life-cycle cost, service life, operating requirements, maintenance, failure, replacement, and residual value influence the total value delivered by an option.', 'Capital investment appraisal; procurement of equipment and infrastructure; building alternatives; maintenance strategy selection; asset replacement decisions; comparison of alternatives using acquisition, operation, maintenance, and end-of-life consequences.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Alternatives must be compared over a consistent analysis period and functional basis, including relevant capital, operating, maintenance, replacement, and end-of-life effects. Monetary valuation should not be used to conceal environmental or social effects that cannot be credibly monetized.', 'Candidate when alternatives differ in initial cost, operating cost, maintenance, service life, replacement, residual value, or end-of-life consequences.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0076', 'Environmental Cost Internalization', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY', 'Environmental Economics; Policy; Governance', 'Environmental cost internalization is the principle of incorporating significant environmental externalities into decisions, prices, investment criteria, or regulatory mechanisms so that activities causing environmental damage face incentives or requirements reflecting at least part of those broader social and ecological costs.', 'Applies to policy, taxation, regulation, infrastructure appraisal, corporate decisions, and market design where environmental externalities can be reasonably characterized. Internalization mechanisms require careful boundary definition and should not be interpreted as permission to cause harm simply because a monetary value can be assigned.', 'Identify external costs; characterize affected systems and populations; use appropriate economic or regulatory instruments; avoid double counting; account for uncertainty and non-monetizable values; combine internalization with standards and limits where necessary.', 'Environmental decisions should not systematically ignore costs imposed on people or ecosystems outside the immediate transaction or organization.', 'externality internalization; environmental externalities; environmental pricing; social cost', 'Environmental decisions should not systematically ignore costs imposed on people or ecosystems outside the immediate transaction or organization.', 'Responsibility for Environmental Burdens; Polluter Pays Responsibility; Burden Transfer', 'Environmental impacts create costs or losses that may fall on parties other than the decision-maker; policy instruments can alter incentives by assigning responsibility for those consequences.', 'Environmental taxes and charges; emissions pricing; waste fees; resource-use charges; environmental provisions in investment appraisal; regulatory mechanisms that incorporate otherwise external environmental costs.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The externality must be sufficiently characterized for the chosen instrument, including who bears the cost and which environmental effect is being represented. A price or charge is not equivalent to permission to pollute, and poorly designed charges can create distributional or leakage effects.', 'Candidate when an environmental externality or unpriced ecological/social cost materially affects a decision, policy, or investment.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0077', 'Polluter Pays Responsibility', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY', 'Environmental Governance; Law; Economics', 'Polluter pays responsibility is the principle that the party responsible for causing pollution or environmental damage should bear appropriate costs associated with preventing, controlling, managing, and, where legally required, remedying that damage rather than shifting those costs to the public or unaffected parties.', 'Applies within legal and policy systems where responsibility can be established and the relevant costs can be identified. It does not mean that payment makes environmental harm acceptable; prevention, standards, liability, restoration, and enforcement remain necessary. Attribution can be difficult for diffuse, cumulative, historical, or transboundary pollution.', 'Place responsibility with the source where practicable; prevent pollution first; recover appropriate prevention and remediation costs; maintain accountability; avoid cost shifting; recognize limits of attribution and cumulative causation.', 'Those who cause environmental pollution should bear appropriate responsibility for its prevention and consequences rather than systematically transferring the burden to others. Distinguishing emphasis: the responsible actor causing pollution or damage bears prevention, control, remediation, or compensation costs; generic accountability alone is insufficient.', 'polluter pays; environmental liability; cost responsibility; environmental accountability', 'Those who cause environmental pollution should bear appropriate responsibility for its prevention and consequences rather than systematically transferring the burden to others.', 'Responsibility for Environmental Burdens; Environmental Cost Internalization; Burden Transfer', 'Liability, regulation, attribution, monitoring, and enforcement determine how environmental responsibility is assigned and costs recovered.', 'Pollution-control liability; remediation requirements; environmental compensation; waste-management responsibility; regulatory enforcement; allocation of pollution-control costs to responsible actors where causation can be established.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The responsible party and relevant burden must be established with defensible evidence or legal rules. The principle does not make pollution acceptable after payment and is difficult to apply mechanically to diffuse, historical, cumulative, or transboundary pollution.', 'Candidate when responsibility for pollution, environmental damage, prevention, control, or remediation must be assigned.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0078', 'Extended Producer Responsibility', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE', 'Circular Economy; Product Policy; Governance', 'Extended producer responsibility is the principle that producers can retain responsibility for specified environmental impacts of products beyond the point of sale, particularly through take-back, collection, recovery, treatment, information, or financing arrangements at the post-consumer stage, where established by policy or other appropriate mechanisms.', 'Applies to product systems where producer influence over design, materials, packaging, collection, or end-of-life can improve outcomes. The exact responsibilities depend on legal and institutional arrangements. EPR should be designed to create effective incentives without obscuring the responsibilities of consumers, waste operators, governments, or other actors.', 'Extend responsibility across the product life cycle; create incentives for better design; support collection and recovery; make responsibilities transparent; track outcomes; prevent cost and risk shifting to informal or vulnerable workers; align obligations with actual producer influence.', 'Product design and market decisions influence post-use impacts, so responsibility can extend beyond the point of sale to improve recovery and reduce waste burdens.', 'extended producer responsibility; EPR; producer take-back; product responsible management', 'Product design and market decisions influence post-use impacts, so responsibility can extend beyond the point of sale to improve recovery and reduce waste burdens.', 'Life-Cycle Responsibility; Responsibility for Environmental Burdens; Design for Disassembly and Recovery', 'Producer decisions influence material selection, product durability, recovery pathways, and the feasibility of post-consumer management.', 'Product take-back; packaging recovery; battery and electronic-waste systems; producer-financed collection and treatment; design incentives linked to end-of-life management.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Producer obligations must be clearly defined within the applicable legal and institutional system and coordinated with consumers, waste operators, governments, and other actors. Responsibility should create credible incentives for better design and end-of-life outcomes without assuming producers control every downstream condition.', 'Candidate when producer influence over product design, take-back, collection, recovery, or post-consumer management is relevant.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0079', 'Monitoring, Feedback, and Corrective Action', 'SYSTEMS', 'Management; Environmental Management; Control', 'Monitoring, feedback, and corrective action is the principle of using measured evidence about system performance to identify deviation, deterioration, unintended consequences, or emerging risk and then adjusting actions, operations, or controls accordingly.', 'Applies to environmental management, infrastructure, resource systems, organizations, projects, and adaptive decision-making where relevant indicators can be measured with sufficient reliability. Monitoring is not valuable merely because data are collected; indicators must be connected to decisions, thresholds, responsibilities, and corrective actions.', 'Measure what matters; establish baselines; detect deviation; define response thresholds; assign responsibility; correct and learn; verify whether interventions worked; avoid collecting data without a decision pathway.', 'Sustainability management requires feedback from observed performance so that decisions can be corrected when outcomes diverge from intended conditions.', 'monitoring and evaluation; feedback control; corrective action; performance monitoring', 'Sustainability management requires feedback from observed performance so that decisions can be corrected when outcomes diverge from intended conditions.', 'Metric-Aware Optimization; Adaptive Learning; Adaptive Management Under Uncertainty', 'Measurement provides feedback about actual system state, enabling comparison with objectives and triggering corrective or adaptive action.', 'Environmental monitoring programmes; infrastructure condition monitoring; energy and water dashboards; project performance reviews; threshold-based intervention; corrective maintenance and management response.', 'Established', 'Established', 'High', 'Indicators must be relevant, measurable with adequate reliability, and connected to thresholds, decisions, responsible actors, and corrective measures. Data collection without a defined response pathway does not constitute effective feedback control.', 'Candidate when measured performance can be compared with objectives, thresholds, or baselines and used to trigger corrective action.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0080', 'Adaptive Management Under Uncertainty', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Resilience; Governance; Environmental Management', 'Adaptive management under uncertainty is the principle of treating sustainability interventions as revisable decisions in systems where knowledge is incomplete, conditions change, or outcomes cannot be predicted with high confidence. Actions are designed so that monitoring and learning can improve subsequent decisions while avoiding unacceptable irreversible harm.', 'Applies to ecological management, climate adaptation, infrastructure planning, resource governance, and other long-horizon decisions with uncertainty. It requires meaningful feedback and decision flexibility; it is not an excuse for indefinite experimentation where risks are severe or where established protective action is already justified.', 'State uncertainty explicitly; choose informative interventions where safe; monitor outcomes; define adaptation triggers; preserve decision flexibility; distinguish reversible experimentation from irreversible commitment; combine learning with precaution.', 'Under uncertainty, sustainability decisions should preserve the ability to learn and adjust while maintaining safeguards against unacceptable harm. Distinguishing emphasis: the intervention itself remains revisable because outcomes are uncertain; monitoring, review points, and allowable adjustments must be defined.', 'adaptive management; learning by doing; iterative management; uncertainty', 'Under uncertainty, sustainability decisions should preserve the ability to learn and adjust while maintaining safeguards against unacceptable harm.', 'Adaptive Learning; Precaution Under Irreversible Risk; Multi-Future Robustness', 'Uncertain system responses require observation and iterative adjustment rather than reliance on a single static forecast.', 'Iterative ecosystem management; climate-adaptation planning; staged infrastructure decisions; experimental management with monitoring; policy revision based on observed outcomes.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The uncertainty, decision variables, monitoring signals, review points, and allowable adjustments must be specified. Adaptive management is inappropriate as a substitute for immediate protective action where risks are severe, irreversible, or already well established.', 'Candidate when system conditions or outcomes are uncertain and decisions can be monitored and adapted without unacceptable irreversible harm.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0081', 'Participation in Resource Governance', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Governance; Natural Resources; Community Management', 'Participation in resource governance is the principle that people and groups affected by decisions about shared resources should have meaningful opportunities to contribute relevant knowledge, express interests, deliberate trade-offs, and influence decisions through processes appropriate to the context.', 'Applies to water, land, forests, fisheries, urban services, infrastructure, community assets, and other resources with distributed impacts or local knowledge. Participation must be meaningful rather than symbolic; it does not require that every participant have equal technical expertise or that every preference be adopted.', 'Include affected stakeholders early; make information understandable; recognize local and experiential knowledge; disclose trade-offs; provide channels for influence and feedback; clarify decision authority; document how participation affected the outcome.', 'Resource decisions are more legitimate and potentially more effective when affected people can contribute knowledge and influence decisions that shape their resources and conditions. Distinguishing emphasis: the participation mechanism is specifically tied to governance, allocation, monitoring, or stewardship of a shared resource.', 'participatory governance; stakeholder participation; community resource management; co-governance', 'Resource decisions are more legitimate and potentially more effective when affected people can contribute knowledge and influence decisions that shape their resources and conditions.', 'Meaningful Participation; Shared Resource Stewardship; Mechanism-Based Traditional Knowledge', 'Governance processes connect resource decisions with local knowledge, interests, accountability, and collective action.', 'Community water allocation; watershed planning; land-use decisions; fisheries management; local infrastructure planning; participatory environmental assessment where affected groups possess relevant knowledge or interests.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Participants should have a genuine opportunity to contribute relevant knowledge, identify impacts, deliberate alternatives, and influence decisions within a clearly defined decision process. Participation does not eliminate professional duties, legal constraints, or the need for technical evidence.', 'Candidate when communities or affected groups have relevant knowledge, interests, or rights in decisions concerning shared resources.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0082', 'Transparency for Sustainability Decisions', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE', 'Governance; Management; Reporting', 'Transparency for sustainability decisions is the principle of making material assumptions, criteria, evidence, trade-offs, uncertainties, and relevant decision information sufficiently accessible for affected or accountable parties to understand how a sustainability-related decision was reached.', 'Applies to public policy, infrastructure decisions, procurement, corporate sustainability, environmental assessment, resource allocation, and other consequential decisions. Transparency is not equivalent to disclosure of every piece of information; privacy, security, intellectual property, and legitimate confidentiality constraints may apply.', 'Disclose decision criteria; identify evidence sources; state assumptions and uncertainty; explain trade-offs; make material information accessible; distinguish fact from judgment; preserve an auditable decision record.', 'Trustworthy sustainability decisions require enough relevant information about evidence, criteria, assumptions, and trade-offs for decisions to be examined and understood. Distinguishing emphasis: information is made visible, interpretable, and decision-relevant so that sustainability choices can be evaluated; formal accountability is not required.', 'transparency; disclosure; traceability; decision transparency; open evidence', 'Trustworthy sustainability decisions require enough relevant information about evidence, criteria, assumptions, and trade-offs for decisions to be examined and understood.', 'Transparency and Accountability; Metric-Aware Optimization; Meaningful Participation', 'Transparency enables scrutiny, informed participation, accountability, and learning from sustainability decisions.', 'Disclosure of environmental assumptions; public reporting; accessible project information; procurement transparency; explanation of sustainability criteria; publication of monitoring results and decision rationales.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The information disclosed should cover material assumptions, criteria, evidence, uncertainties, trade-offs, and decision responsibilities in a form that affected or accountable parties can understand. Disclosure must still respect legitimate privacy, security, and confidentiality constraints.', 'Candidate when sustainability decisions depend on explicit evidence, assumptions, criteria, uncertainty, trade-offs, or decision records.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0083', 'Accountability Across Value Chains', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Supply Chains; Governance; Corporate Responsibility', 'Accountability across value chains is the principle that sustainability responsibilities should be considered across the interconnected stages through which materials, products, services, information, risks, and impacts move, rather than assigning responsibility only to the organization or process at the point where an outcome becomes visible.', 'Applies to supply chains, infrastructure delivery, manufacturing, food systems, digital services, construction, and other distributed production systems. Accountability should reflect actual influence, responsibility, contractual arrangements, and causal contribution; it should not become an unsupported claim that one actor controls every upstream or downstream outcome.', 'Map relevant value-chain stages; identify material impacts and dependencies; assign responsibilities according to influence and contribution; verify supplier and contractor information; address risks beyond organizational boundaries; avoid responsibility shifting.', 'Sustainability impacts can arise outside organizational boundaries, so effective responsibility requires visibility and appropriate action across relevant value-chain stages.', 'value-chain accountability; supply-chain responsibility; due diligence; chain-of-custody', 'Sustainability impacts can arise outside organizational boundaries, so effective responsibility requires visibility and appropriate action across relevant value-chain stages.', 'Responsibility for Environmental Burdens; Sustainable Procurement and Supplier Stewardship; Transparency and Accountability', 'Value-chain structure distributes impacts, information, risks, and responsibilities among multiple actors.', 'Supply-chain due diligence; product traceability; construction-material sourcing; food-system impacts; electronics supply chains; contractor and supplier responsibility for material environmental and social risks.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Responsibilities should follow actual influence, contractual duties, causal contribution, and available control at each relevant stage. Value-chain framing should not turn remote association into an unsupported claim of direct responsibility.', 'Candidate when environmental, social, or resource impacts arise across multiple organizations or stages of a value chain.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0084', 'Local and Context-Appropriate Provision', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY', 'Infrastructure; Technology; Development', 'Local and context-appropriate provision is the principle of selecting or designing technologies, services, infrastructure, and resource systems that fit local environmental conditions, available skills, maintenance capacity, institutions, affordability, cultural context, and actual user needs rather than assuming that a technically advanced solution is universally appropriate.', 'Applies to infrastructure, water and sanitation, energy, housing, agriculture, health-support systems, digital services, and community-scale technologies. Local does not automatically mean sustainable, and high-technology solutions may be appropriate where local capacity and life-cycle performance support them.', 'Match solution complexity to context; assess local skills and maintenance; consider affordability and access; use locally available resources where appropriate; involve users; test long-term operability; avoid technology transfer without institutional and environmental fit.', 'A solution is more likely to remain sustainable when its technical characteristics are compatible with the environmental, social, economic, and institutional conditions in which it must operate. Distinguishing emphasis: appropriateness is broader than ecological fit and includes skills, institutions, affordability, culture, maintenance, supply chains, and actual user needs.', 'context-appropriate technology; appropriate technology; local capacity; contextual fit', 'A solution is more likely to remain sustainable when its technical characteristics are compatible with the environmental, social, economic, and institutional conditions in which it must operate.', 'Local Ecological Fit; Local Capacity and Maintainability; Technology for Expanded Human and Ecological Options', 'Technology performance depends on environmental conditions, skills, institutions, supply chains, maintenance, affordability, and user practices.', 'Decentralized water and sanitation; local energy systems; climate-responsive buildings; locally serviceable infrastructure; community-scale technologies; technology choices matched to local environmental and institutional conditions.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Suitability must be demonstrated against local environmental conditions, demand, skills, maintenance capacity, affordability, safety, regulation, and life-cycle performance. Local sourcing or small scale alone does not establish appropriateness.', 'Candidate when technology or service performance depends strongly on local environmental, institutional, skill, affordability, or cultural conditions.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0085', 'Affordability and Equitable Access', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Social Sustainability; Public Services; Economics', 'Affordability and equitable access is the principle that essential goods, services, infrastructure, and opportunities should be accessible without imposing disproportionate financial or practical burdens on people, particularly where unequal access can undermine health, dignity, capability, participation, or livelihood.', 'Applies to water, sanitation, energy, housing, transport, education, digital access, health-related services, and other essential systems. Affordability must be assessed relative to income, household circumstances, geography, accessibility, and service quality; low price alone does not establish equitable access if quality or safety is inadequate.', 'Assess access as well as average cost; identify affordability barriers; protect essential service access; consider differentiated needs; examine geographic and disability-related barriers; evaluate who benefits and who bears costs.', 'Sustainability requires that essential services remain practically and financially accessible rather than improving aggregate system performance while excluding or burdening vulnerable users.', 'affordability; equitable access; service access; energy poverty; water affordability', 'Sustainability requires that essential services remain practically and financially accessible rather than improving aggregate system performance while excluding or burdening vulnerable users.', 'Equitable Sustainability; Human Capability and Dignity; Resource Allocation by Need and Impact', 'Prices, service design, distribution, infrastructure location, income, and institutional arrangements shape who can obtain and benefit from essential services.', 'Water and sanitation tariffs; energy access; public transport; housing; digital connectivity; essential services where price, geography, accessibility, and service quality affect actual access.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Access should be assessed using total user burden, including price, connection or travel costs, time, accessibility, reliability, quality, and geographic constraints. Low monetary price is not sufficient if the service is unsafe, unreliable, or practically inaccessible.', 'Candidate when access to essential services or opportunities differs because of financial, geographic, physical, or social barriers.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0086', 'Safer Substitution and Hazard Reduction', 'PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Materials; Chemical Safety; Product Design', 'Safer substitution and hazard reduction is the principle of replacing hazardous substances, processes, or design choices with less hazardous alternatives where technically feasible, while preventing exposure and reducing risks to workers, users, communities, and ecosystems across the life cycle.', 'Applies to chemicals, materials, manufacturing processes, products, laboratories, construction, agriculture, and waste management. Substitution must consider functional performance, toxicity, exposure pathways, persistence, degradation products, fire or physical hazards, and life-cycle consequences. A substitute should not be considered safer merely because one hazard is reduced if another significant hazard is introduced.', 'Eliminate hazards where feasible; substitute safer alternatives; assess exposure pathways; compare hazard profiles; prevent regrettable substitution; use controls where substitution is not feasible; consider workers, users, communities, and ecosystems.', 'Reducing hazard at the source can prevent harm more effectively than relying only on downstream exposure control or waste treatment.', 'safer substitution; hazard reduction; non-toxic materials; chemical substitution; pollution prevention', 'Reducing hazard at the source can prevent harm more effectively than relying only on downstream exposure control or waste treatment.', 'Preventive Sustainability; Alternative Material Selection for Sustainability; Responsibility for Environmental Burdens', 'Hazard properties, exposure pathways, process design, substitution options, and control measures determine risk to people and ecosystems.', 'Chemical substitution; safer construction materials; process redesign; replacement of hazardous solvents or substances; exposure reduction in laboratories, workplaces, agriculture, manufacturing, and waste handling.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The substitute must satisfy the required function while reducing relevant hazard under realistic exposure conditions. Assessment should include toxicity, persistence, exposure pathways, degradation products, fire or physical hazards, worker and user exposure, and life-cycle consequences.', 'Candidate when hazardous substances, materials, processes, or exposure pathways can be reduced or replaced by safer alternatives.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0087', 'Sustainable Use Intensity', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY', 'Resource Management; Infrastructure; Operations', 'Sustainable use intensity is the principle of evaluating the pressure placed on a resource, asset, ecosystem, or service relative to its capacity, condition, recovery, or intended function, rather than judging sustainability from total use alone.', 'Applies where the same aggregate consumption can produce different sustainability outcomes depending on local capacity, timing, concentration, duration, or system condition. Relevant measures may include withdrawal per renewable supply, occupancy relative to carrying capacity, load relative to infrastructure capacity, or resource use per service delivered.', 'Measure pressure relative to capacity; distinguish aggregate use from intensity; account for temporal and spatial concentration; monitor thresholds and recovery; avoid interpreting low intensity as sustainable when absolute limits are exceeded.', 'Sustainability depends on resource or system pressure relative to the capacity of the system to support, absorb, or recover from that pressure.', 'use intensity; resource pressure; carrying capacity; load intensity; withdrawal intensity', 'Sustainability depends on resource or system pressure relative to the capacity of the system to support, absorb, or recover from that pressure.', 'Ecological Limits; Resource Regeneration Capacity; Metric-Aware Optimization', 'Resource availability, system capacity, recovery rate, demand, and temporal or spatial concentration determine sustainable use intensity.', 'Water withdrawal relative to renewable availability; grazing or harvesting pressure relative to ecosystem capacity; occupancy relative to infrastructure or ecological limits; energy or material use per unit of delivered service.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Intensity must be related to a meaningful capacity, condition, recovery rate, or service requirement. The appropriate denominator and threshold depend on the system, and lower intensity per unit does not guarantee lower total impact if total demand rises.', 'Candidate when sustainability depends on pressure or use relative to resource, ecological, infrastructure, or recovery capacity.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0088', 'Prevention Before Remediation', 'SYSTEMS; ECOLOGY; PEOPLE', 'Environmental Management; Risk Management; Public Health', 'Prevention before remediation is the principle of prioritizing measures that avoid the creation or escalation of environmental or social harm before relying on later corrective, treatment, restoration, or compensation measures.', 'Applies to pollution, waste, environmental degradation, infrastructure failure, occupational risk, public-health risks, and other situations where preventive measures can reduce downstream consequences. Prevention is not always sufficient; remediation remains necessary when harm has already occurred or prevention cannot eliminate residual risk.', 'Identify hazards upstream; remove or reduce causes; use preventive controls; monitor emerging impacts; intervene early; reserve remediation for residual or existing damage; compare prevention and remediation over the whole life cycle.', 'Avoiding harm at its source generally reduces the resources, exposure, uncertainty, and residual damage associated with correcting harm after it occurs. Distinguishing emphasis: use this concept when the decision is specifically between preventing a harmful release, degradation, or exposure and remediating it after occurrence.', 'prevention hierarchy; pollution prevention; prevention before remediation; upstream intervention', 'Avoiding harm at its source generally reduces the resources, exposure, uncertainty, and residual damage associated with correcting harm after it occurs.', 'Preventive Sustainability; Waste Prevention at Source; Precaution Under Irreversible Risk', 'Cause-oriented intervention reduces the probability or magnitude of downstream damage and remediation requirements.', 'Pollution prevention; leak prevention; preventive safety measures; source reduction; erosion control; failure prevention; waste avoidance before treatment, cleanup, restoration, or compensation.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Prevention should address identifiable causes before harm occurs or escalates. Where harm already exists or cannot be fully prevented, remediation and residual-risk management remain necessary; prevention must not be used to avoid addressing existing damage.', 'Candidate when upstream prevention can avoid or reduce the need for downstream treatment, remediation, restoration, or compensation.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0089', 'Sustainable Capacity Utilization', 'SYSTEMS', 'Infrastructure; Operations; Economics', 'Sustainable capacity utilization is the principle of operating infrastructure, equipment, facilities, or other capacity at levels that deliver required service without persistent underutilization that wastes embodied resources or overutilization that accelerates deterioration, failure, congestion, or excessive energy and resource demand.', 'Applies to buildings, transport, utilities, manufacturing, computing infrastructure, laboratories, public facilities, and other capacity-constrained systems. The appropriate utilization range is system-specific and may vary over time. Maximizing utilization is not automatically sustainable when it compromises resilience, maintenance, safety, accessibility, or future flexibility.', 'Match capacity to actual service needs; identify persistent underuse; avoid chronic overload; consider shared use; schedule flexible demand; preserve safety margins; account for embodied capacity as well as operating efficiency.', 'Existing capacity should be used effectively where feasible before adding new capacity, while maintaining the margins required for safety, resilience, and future needs.', 'capacity utilization; asset utilization; shared capacity; infrastructure utilization', 'Existing capacity should be used effectively where feasible before adding new capacity, while maintaining the margins required for safety, resilience, and future needs.', 'Demand Management Before Capacity Expansion; Functional Sufficiency; Distributed Resilience', 'Utilization affects whether embodied capacity provides useful service and whether operation approaches limits that increase energy, wear, congestion, or failure risk.', 'Building occupancy; transport-network utilization; equipment loading; manufacturing capacity; data-centre utilization; laboratory and public-facility use where excessive underuse or overloading has resource or service consequences.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'The relevant operating range depends on reliability, maintenance, safety, accessibility, resilience, and service requirements. Maximizing utilization can be unsustainable if it accelerates deterioration, removes contingency capacity, or compromises future flexibility.', 'Candidate when existing capacity is persistently underused or chronically overloaded and utilization affects resource efficiency or service quality.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0090', 'Knowledge and Skill Continuity for Sustainability', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Knowledge Management; Operations; Community Capacity', 'Knowledge and skill continuity for sustainability is the principle of preserving and transferring the knowledge, skills, practices, records, and institutional memory required to operate, maintain, adapt, and govern systems over time, especially when sustainability outcomes depend on locally accumulated or specialized knowledge.', 'Applies to infrastructure operation, traditional ecological knowledge, community resource management, maintenance-intensive technologies, organizations, and long-lived projects. Continuity does not mean freezing practices; knowledge should remain open to verification, learning, adaptation, and integration with new evidence.', 'Document critical knowledge; support intergenerational and cross-role transfer; preserve tacit and procedural knowledge where possible; maintain training pathways; combine local experience with scientific evidence; update knowledge as conditions change.', 'Sustainability over long time horizons depends on retaining the capability to understand, operate, maintain, and adapt systems after individual people, technologies, or project teams change.', 'knowledge continuity; institutional memory; skill transfer; intergenerational knowledge', 'Sustainability over long time horizons depends on retaining the capability to understand, operate, maintain, and adapt systems after individual people, technologies, or project teams change.', 'Knowledge Continuity and Transfer; Local Capacity and Maintainability; Mechanism-Based Traditional Knowledge', 'Loss of operational or contextual knowledge can reduce maintenance quality, adaptive capacity, and continuity of sustainability practices.', 'Training and succession planning; operator certification; maintenance knowledge transfer; community technical capacity; documentation of critical procedures; continuity planning for long-lived infrastructure and organizations.', 'Established', 'Established', 'Moderate-High', 'Critical knowledge must remain available to the people and institutions responsible for operation, maintenance, adaptation, and governance. Continuity should support verification and updating rather than preserve practices unchanged when evidence or conditions require modification.', 'Candidate when long-term operation, maintenance, adaptation, or responsible management depends on retaining and transferring specialized, local, or institutional knowledge.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0091', 'Social Protection as Shock Buffer', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Social resilience and public systems', 'Social protection as shock buffering is the sustainability principle of maintaining basic income, service access, or other essential support so that people facing shocks do not lose the capabilities and minimum conditions required for well-being and recovery.', 'Applies where economic, environmental, health, employment, or other shocks can push vulnerable people below a minimum level of security or access. It concerns protective systems that reduce exposure to severe welfare loss; it does not imply that all risks should be absorbed by public support or that support can substitute for structural risk reduction.', 'Protect minimum capabilities; reduce vulnerability to shocks; maintain access to essential services; prioritize people with greater exposure or lower coping capacity; combine immediate protection with longer-term resilience.', 'Links sustainability and resilience to mechanisms that prevent shocks from converting into persistent loss of basic capabilities.', 'social protection; social safety net; shock-responsive protection; vulnerability buffering; basic income support; resilience support', 'Sustainability should maintain essential human capabilities and access to basic services when foreseeable shocks threaten to push vulnerable populations below a minimum level of security.', 'Social-policy practice; resilience and development evidence', 'Transfers, services, insurance, or other protective measures reduce the immediate welfare loss caused by shocks, preserving health, livelihood, education, and recovery capacity.', 'Social protection floors; shock-responsive social assistance; livelihood support for vulnerable groups during economic or climate shocks.', 'Direct UN SDG target and action evidence', 'Established', 'Disaster resilience; public services; health; livelihoods; education; climate adaptation', 'Protection must reach people exposed to material shocks and be sufficient, timely, and administratively accessible. It should complement rather than replace prevention, risk reduction, or structural reforms.', 'Consider when an application context involves shocks, vulnerability, loss of basic services, or unequal capacity to absorb disruption.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0092', 'Nutrition-Sensitive Local Provision', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; SYSTEMS', 'Food systems and human well-being', 'Nutrition-sensitive local provision is the sustainability principle of designing food-production and provisioning systems so that local availability, livelihood conditions, production diversity, and nutritional quality are considered together rather than treating food volume or income as sufficient outcomes.', 'Applies to food systems where production and provisioning choices affect dietary quality, household livelihoods, and local resource conditions. It does not require all food to be locally produced, and local production is not inherently sustainable if it degrades resources or reduces nutritional diversity.', 'Link production with nutrition; diversify where it improves dietary quality and resilience; consider producer livelihoods; connect food security with resource sustainability; evaluate outcomes beyond production volume.', 'Connects food-system sustainability to the joint consideration of nutritional outcomes, local production capability, livelihoods, and resource conditions.', 'nutrition-sensitive agriculture; nutrition-sensitive food systems; diversified local production; food and nutrition security', 'Sustainable food systems should evaluate production choices by their contribution to nutritional adequacy, livelihood security, and long-term resource conditions together.', 'Food-system practice; public-health and agricultural development evidence', 'Diversified and context-appropriate production can improve availability of nutrient-rich foods while supporting producer livelihoods and reducing dependence on narrowly optimized production pathways.', 'Diversified sustainable agricultural production systems linked to child nutrition and producer livelihoods; Farmer Field Schools promoting agroecological production.', 'Direct UN SDG action evidence', 'Established', 'Agriculture; food systems; public health; rural development; procurement', 'Local production should be evaluated for nutritional value, ecological impacts, affordability, seasonality, and livelihood effects; localization alone is not evidence of sustainability.', 'Consider when a technical or social application affects food production, provisioning, nutrition, agricultural livelihoods, or resource-intensive food systems.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0093', 'Gender-Responsive Resource Access', 'PEOPLE; SYSTEMS', 'Equity and resource governance', 'Gender-responsive resource access is the sustainability principle of assessing and addressing gender-specific barriers to access, control, ownership, safety, participation, and benefit-sharing when resources, services, infrastructure, technology, or opportunities are allocated or designed.', 'Applies where gender roles, legal arrangements, social norms, care responsibilities, safety conditions, or unequal control over resources can alter actual access or outcomes. It does not mean assuming identical needs or prescribing a single gender-specific solution across contexts.', 'Assess differential access; distinguish formal availability from effective access and control; account for safety and care constraints; include affected groups in decisions; evaluate distribution of benefits and burdens.', 'Extends equitable access from nominal availability to gender-differentiated access, control, participation, and outcomes.', 'gender-responsive access; gender-responsive design; gender equity in resources; women’s resource access; gender-sensitive planning', 'Sustainability decisions should account for gender-differentiated barriers and control over resources so that formal equality does not conceal unequal practical access or outcomes.', 'Gender-equality policy; inclusive development practice', 'Identifying and removing gender-specific barriers changes who can access, control, use, and benefit from resources and decisions.', 'Gender-responsive public services and infrastructure; women’s participation in resource governance; reforms addressing women’s land, financial, technological, and leadership access.', 'Direct UN SDG target and action evidence', 'Established', 'Infrastructure; water; land; finance; technology; education; governance; workplace systems', 'The relevant gender-related barriers must be evidenced or established in the application context. The principle should not be reduced to demographic labelling without identifying a mechanism affecting access or outcomes.', 'Consider when an application involves resource allocation, infrastructure access, technology adoption, land or financial rights, safety, or decision-making with gender-differentiated effects.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0094', 'Decent Work and Worker Well-Being', 'LIFE; PEOPLE; SYSTEMS; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Work systems and economic sustainability', 'Decent work and worker well-being is the sustainability principle of designing economic and production systems so that productive activity is compatible with safe working conditions, fair treatment, human dignity, rights, and durable worker capability.', 'Applies to employment, production, service delivery, supply chains, and technology-mediated work where productivity choices can affect worker safety, health, security, or dignity. It does not equate sustainability with employment quantity alone.', 'Protect health and safety; preserve dignity and rights; maintain productive capability; consider working conditions alongside productivity; prevent shifting environmental or economic gains onto workers.', 'Connects economic sustainability with the conditions under which productive work is performed and the preservation of worker capability and dignity.', 'decent work; worker well-being; occupational safety; safe work; labour rights; sustainable employment', 'Economic activity is not sustainable when productivity or growth depends on unsafe, degrading, or rights-incompatible working conditions.', 'Labour standards; social sustainability; economic development practice', 'Safe and fair working conditions reduce harm, preserve human capability, improve continuity of productive activity, and prevent externalization of social costs onto workers.', 'Demand-driven skills and apprenticeship programmes; occupational inspection and labour-protection systems; decent-work initiatives.', 'Direct UN SDG action and target evidence', 'Established', 'Manufacturing; construction; services; agriculture; digital work; supply chains', 'Worker impacts must be material to the system being evaluated. Productivity gains should not be classified as sustainable if they depend on avoidable safety, health, rights, or dignity losses.', 'Consider when course concepts affect workplace design, automation, production systems, infrastructure construction, supply chains, or employment conditions.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0095', 'Policy Coherence for Sustainability', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE; ECOLOGY; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Governance and systems integration', 'Policy coherence for sustainability is the sustainability principle of aligning policies, rules, incentives, plans, and implementation mechanisms across sectors and levels of governance so that actions supporting one objective do not systematically undermine another sustainability objective.', 'Applies where multiple policies, institutions, sectors, or governance levels influence the same sustainability outcome. It concerns interaction and consistency among policy instruments; it does not require all policies to have identical objectives or eliminate legitimate trade-offs.', 'Identify policy interactions; detect contradictions and adverse incentives; align objectives and implementation; manage trade-offs explicitly; coordinate across institutional boundaries and time horizons.', 'Treats sustainability as a cross-policy coordination problem in which contradictory incentives or fragmented governance can undermine otherwise beneficial interventions.', 'policy coherence; coherent sustainability policy; cross-sector policy alignment; policy integration; governance coherence', 'Sustainability outcomes improve when policies and institutional incentives are mutually coherent across sectors, scales, and time horizons, with unavoidable trade-offs made explicit.', 'Sustainable-development governance; policy-integration practice', 'Alignment reduces contradictory incentives, duplicated effort, policy leakage, and cross-sector displacement of environmental or social burdens.', 'Mechanisms for policy coherence for sustainable development; coordinated multi-sector implementation and planning under the 2030 Agenda.', 'Direct UN SDG target evidence', 'Established', 'Public policy; infrastructure planning; water-energy-food systems; climate policy; institutional strategy', 'Coherence must be assessed across the policies and institutions materially affecting the outcome. Alignment does not imply that conflicts disappear; significant trade-offs still require explicit decision-making.', 'Consider when an application spans multiple policies, departments, governance levels, regulations, incentives, or sustainability objectives.', 'Active'), ('SCR_0096', 'Shared Knowledge and Resource Mobilization', 'SYSTEMS; PEOPLE; FUTURE_GENERATIONS', 'Partnerships and implementation capacity', 'Shared knowledge and resource mobilization is the sustainability principle of deliberately combining complementary knowledge, expertise, technology, financial resources, and implementation capabilities across actors when no single actor can effectively address a sustainability challenge alone.', 'Applies to multi-actor problems requiring complementary capabilities, resources, or knowledge. Collaboration is not inherently sustainable: roles, incentives, accountability, power, data rights, and distribution of benefits must be considered.', 'Combine complementary capabilities; mobilize resources around a defined outcome; enable knowledge and technology exchange; clarify roles and accountability; retain local ownership and implementation capacity.', 'Frames partnership as a mechanism for overcoming capability and resource discontinuities in complex sustainability implementation.', 'knowledge mobilization; resource mobilization; capacity sharing; multi-stakeholder collaboration; knowledge exchange; implementation partnership', 'Complex sustainability challenges should mobilize and share complementary knowledge, expertise, technology, and resources across capable actors while maintaining clear roles, accountability, and local implementation capacity.', 'UN SDG partnership practice; development cooperation', 'Pooling complementary capabilities closes technical, financial, institutional, and knowledge gaps that can prevent otherwise feasible sustainability interventions from being implemented or scaled.', 'Multi-stakeholder SDG partnerships; technical and capacity-building collaborations; knowledge and technology sharing initiatives.', 'Direct UN SDG target evidence', 'Established', 'Research; education; infrastructure; public services; technology deployment; community development', 'Partnerships should address a real capability or resource gap and include mechanisms for accountability, knowledge transfer, and durable local capacity. Partnership formation alone is not evidence of sustainability.', 'Consider when a sustainability application requires complementary expertise, technology, finance, institutional capacity, or community knowledge from multiple actors.', 'Active') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO repo_guardrails (guardrail_id, guardrail_level, topic, allowed_concepts, excluded_claims, evidence_type, status) VALUES ('GR_COURSE_001', 'Course', 'Data Structures & Algorithms', 'Foundations & Linear Data Structures; classes; objects; encapsulation; state and behaviour; abstraction; implementation of abstract data types; LIFO operations; push/pop; stack representation; expression processing; backtracking; Linear Data Structures; FIFO operations; queue representation; buffering; scheduling; breadth-first processing; dynamic nodes; pointers/references; insertion; deletion; traversal; Trees & Graphs; binary search trees; ordered search; balanced search trees; rotations; height balance; logarithmic search; update operations; Sorting & Searching; comparison sorting; partitioning; merging; time complexity; auxiliary space; search space reduction; comparison operations; Hashing; hash functions; hash tables; collisions; chaining; open addressing', 'Ordinary data-structure implementation, complexity analysis, searching, sorting, or traversal does not by itself establish an environmental, social, resource, governance, or other sustainability mechanism.', 'Curriculum-derived disciplinary scope', 'Active'), ('GR_COURSE_002', 'Course', 'Discrete Mathematics', 'Propositional Logic; logical connectives; satisfiability; implication; equivalence; Proof Techniques; mathematical induction; base case; inductive hypothesis; inductive step; proof structure; Set Theory & Relations; sets; relations; reflexivity; symmetry; transitivity; equivalence classes; Combinatorics; counting arguments; finite sets; injectivity; occupancy; combinatorial proof; Graph Theory; trees; vertices and edges; paths; connectivity; spanning structures', '', 'Curriculum-derived disciplinary scope', 'Active'), ('GR_COURSE_003', 'Course', 'Database Management Systems', 'ER Modeling; entities; attributes; relationships; cardinality; integrity constraints; Relational Model; relations; selection; projecti